const LIBRARY_BOOKS = [{"id":"lib_a_hard_winter","title":"The Fires on the Ground","author":"Orrin Pale, sexton of Oakhaven","genre":"chronicle","year":"413 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"chapel","how":"pile","at":"chapel","style":"parch","summary":"The Oakhaven sexton's account of the winter after the Fracture, when the orchards blew away, the town starved, and the ground had to be burned before it could be dug.","text":["I am not a writing man. I dig. But the Cantor says that what is written is kept, and somebody should keep this winter, and he has enough to carry, so I have taken it on. These are the dead of Oakhaven from the day the orchards went to the day the wagons came, and what it took to put them down.","The orchards went on a still morning at the back end of summer. I was in the churchyard turning the new plot for old Hesketh's widow, who was ailing, and I felt it before I heard it: a sort of hiss in the air, like milk boiling over on the far side of a wall. Then the wind came off the hills, and the reed-rows below the town, that had sung every wind of my life, did not sing. They chimed once, thin, like a glass knocked with a nail, and then the whole valley of them lifted off their stalks and went past me in a glitter. It got in my hair and my collar and my mouth. The children ran after it with their tongues out because it looked like sugar. It tasted of nothing at all. That frightened me more than the rest of it, and I could not have said why.","By evening there was not a standing reed in the Reach, and the Cantor was walking the rows with his hands open, the way you walk a field after hail.","The milk soured that night, every pail in the town. The dogs began howling at noon, which they had never done, and went on doing it all winter. When I tolled for Hesketh's widow in the first week of autumn, the bell came out flat. Not cracked. Flat, by a hair, as if it were somebody else's bell ringing in the next valley. Every person in the square stopped and looked up at the tower. I rang it out, because she was owed it, and then I tied off the rope. I have not tolled since. The Chapel-reader says it is the same everywhere, that the whole world has gone a little out of true and will come right with prayer. I pray. I have not untied the rope.","Then the grain from the south stopped. The carters said the roads were bad. The carters stopped coming to say it. The Governor stood on the Greenhall step and told us to keep faith and to ration, and we did both, and only one of them lasted.","The winter wheat came up wrong. It came up early and white and very fast, and stood in the field like a crop of straws, and when you split the ear there was nothing in it but a bead of water. Coker the miller ground what there was and wept at the stones, which I had not known a miller could do. By midwinter we had eaten the seed-corn. Everybody knew what that meant. Nobody said it.","Then the frost came. I have dug this ground forty years and never once met frost below the turf. The Reach does not freeze; everybody knows it. That winter it froze a spade's depth down and then two, hard as a step, and the spade rang off it and jarred me to the teeth. So I went to the woodpile.","This is the way I found to do it. The night before a burying I laid a fire on the grave's length, furze first and then whatever would burn, and sat up with it till the small hours, and raked it out and dug the soft hand's depth it had made, and laid another on top, and so on down. Three nights for a grown man. Two for a child. I will not write how many nights there were. I will write who they were for.","Wilf and Alban Wetherby, twins, four years old, the week before Candlefast, in one grave, because I had not the wood for two and their mother said they had never slept apart.","Avis Brindle, who went into the Flume at the sluice-gate. There was nothing to put in the ground. The Cantor sang her down at the gate, and I stood by with my spade because it did not seem right to come without it.","Tilda Hale, nine years.","Mother Peascod, who gave her bread to the Wetherby girl and did not tell anyone, and was found in her chair.","By Candlefast we were boiling harness. I want that written plainly, because people will say afterward it cannot have been so bad. It was the Governor's own carriage-harness first. He sent it down to the Greenhall kitchen himself and stood at the copper while it boiled, so that nobody could say he had kept it back.","Lettie, Osgood and the baby, the miller's three, in the space of nine days. Lambert Coker dug alongside me for the last of them and would not be stopped, and the two of us sat by the fire on his daughter's grave all night, and he told me every word she had ever learned, in order, which I think is the longest anybody has ever spoken to me.","Jonas Garlick, who walked out into the dead reed-rows one night and lay down in the dust, and was found smiling.","Hobday's infant, three days old. Bettany the midwife brought her to me herself, wrapped, and said that was the seventh child I had had off her that winter and she would bring me no more if she could help it, and then she sat down on my step and could not get up for a while.","The Sawtell brothers, both together, of the cold, in the cart-shed where they had gone to save the walk home.","Widow Dimmock. Old Hesketh's sister. Two of the Ackroyd household, whose names their mother has asked me to leave out, and I have. A carter from Greenroot whose name nobody knew, who came up the road with an empty cart to ask if we had anything, and stayed.","That is seventeen, and Hesketh's widow before them, who would have gone in any year. The Cantor sang every one of them. Some nights he sang with nothing left in him but breath, and I could hear his breath going down into the ground with them, and it was enough. He says a song for the dead does not have to be heard by the living. I hope that is true. There were not many living out by then.","In the spring the frost went out of the ground all at once, in a night, and the next morning forty wagons came up the Greenroot road with grain under the sheets and men in green and black walking beside them. The Governor went out to meet them in the square in his good coat. There was a paper. He read it at the table in the Greenhall with the doors open and the whole town standing in the square outside, smelling the sacks, and nobody inside or out said a word. Then he signed it.","I do not know what was in it. I have heard it said since that he sold something that was not his to sell. I will say this, and then I will stop. I am the one man in Oakhaven who knows to the night what it cost not to sign. I have put the spade back on its pegs, and I have let the fires go out, and I do not mean to be the one who tells him he paid too much."]},
{"id":"lib_a_wool_wall","title":"The Wool Wall","author":"Dorcas Ribble, carder at the Carders' mill","genre":"chronicle","year":"417 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"mill","how":"quest","at":"p22_hanger","quest":"ilse","style":"paper","summary":"The carder who first proposed the Hedge tells how Oakhaven planted it in one season, out of thorn and the fleece of every sheep in the Reach.","text":["It was my idea. I want that written down in my own hand, while there is still time to be blamed for it properly.","The hounds came at the turn of the year. Not into the town. They came to the edge of the orchards on a Massday night and sat down in a row in the dust where the reeds used to be, eleven of them, with their heads on one side, like a congregation waiting for the reader. They did not howl. They listened. In the morning they were gone, and every one of us had lain awake till dawn trying to remember whether we had made a sound.","The Cantor sent to the three nearest parishes for help. Two Cantors came. The third sent a note that said: We have stopped being able to send. They sat in the Greenhall all one afternoon with the Governor and the bench, and talked about walls and fires and singing, and every plan was a loud one, because men's plans are. I was there to bring the bread. I am not on the bench and never wished to be.","I said, it isn't a wall you want. It's a stopper. You pad a cradle against a draught. Pad the town. Wool eats sound; I have stood in the fleece-loft at the mill with forty bales round me and heard my own heart. Plant thorn deep, so nothing can come through it, and pack the thorn with wool, so nothing can hear through it.","Nobody laughed, which was worse than if they had. The Cantor wrote yes in his register, and looked at it, and said he did not feel any better, and nor did I.","He did not see it go in. The hounds had him at the Pellow farm in the spring, and the Hedge went in that summer without him. The Consortium paid. I set that down too, since it is the one time. They sent wagons of blackthorn and hawthorn whips from somewhere south, and bramble crowns in wet sacking, and a young engineer in green and black with a bag of pegs and a measuring chain, who walked the line of it round the town in two days and stuck a peg every ten paces and never once asked anybody what the Hedge was for. He knew. He had the kind face of a man who has been told not to look up from his chain.","The wool was ours. Every sheep in the Reach came to be shorn. The Farrants brought the Downs flocks down off the sheep-walk in one great grey river, and the Wethercombe folk came in behind them with their carts and their bedding, and the Bellwether put up its shutters, and the fold on the Downs was left standing empty with the gate open. We shore in the square, in the lanes, in the Chapel porch. We shore ewes that had been shorn in spring and had barely a fleece to give. The mill book says nine thousand fleeces by the end, and that is not counting what came in loose in sacks from the far farms, smelling of the hill.","We carded it all. That was our part. The mill engine ran day and night until it was too hot to stand by, and then we carded by hand on our knees in the yard, all of us, the old women and the girls and the men who had never touched a card in their lives, by lamp, in our aprons, with our hands bleeding into the fleece. Nell sang the work-song all night, the whole of it and then from the top again, and nobody asked her to be quiet, because it was the last night anybody would have to ask.","This is how it went in. Thorn in a trench as deep as a man's waist. A man's height of packed wool either side of it, rammed down with posts. Thorn again. Wool again. Felt and old sacking hung over the top, straw in the roots, three men deep all the way round. Four hundred hands, and the Governor's two, which blistered on the first morning and which he would not have bound. He worked the north side with the carters and did not speak to anybody, and was there at dusk and there at dawn.","When the last row went in at the North Gate, somebody said we should try it. So the whole town stood on the rise inside the thorn and shouted together, every one of us, as loud as we could, the first loud sound any of us had made since the hounds came. It went into the Hedge like a stone into a pond with no bottom. No echo. Not a whisper of one. It was the most terrible thing I have ever heard, and we cheered it, and then we stood there and were very quiet, because we could be.","Harrow has the gate key now. The gate shuts at dusk.","I go round the Hedge most evenings, along the inside, looking for where the wool has slipped. It has grown a yard since we planted it, the thorn. Everything grows here now, faster than it ought. The flocks are inside with us, in the paddocks behind the tannery and the orchard closes and anywhere there is grass, and one of the Farrants walks them every morning and counts them every night. There are a great many sheep and a great many of us, and the winter is coming, and I have not yet heard anyone say aloud what everyone has worked out. I will not say it either.","I said a stopper. I did not stop to think what a stopper does with what it stops. A cork does not throw the wine away. It keeps it.","I know wool. I have handled it all my life. Wool keeps. It keeps the warmth of the one who wore it, and the shape of them, long after they have taken it off; you can lift a shawl off a peg a week after a woman has gone and still find her shoulders in it. Every sound this town makes goes into my wool now and does not come out the other side. That was the whole of the plan, and it works, and I am proud of it, and on still nights I put my hand flat on the fleece at the cooperage wall, the way a person feels a loaf to see whether it has proved, and it is warm."]},
{"id":"lib_a_greenroot_road","title":"What Came Up the Greenroot Road","author":"Josiah Seddon, under-clerk at the Greenhall","genre":"chronicle","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"greenhall","how":"pile","at":"greenhall","style":"office","summary":"A Greenhall under-clerk sets down, very carefully, how the Consortium of Bores came to Oakhaven between the famine spring of 413 and the bore, and what he copied along the way.","text":["I have been under-clerk at the Greenhall for eleven years. I copy what I am given and I file what I copy, and I have never once been asked what I think of it, which suits me. What follows is not what I think. It is only the order things happened in, which is a clerk's business, and which I find is already being forgotten.","Spring, 413. The forty wagons came up the Greenroot road the week the frost went out of the ground. I counted them from the Greenhall step because counting is what I do when I am frightened. Forty, each with a sheet roped over it and a man in green and black walking at the wheel. They stopped in the square and nobody unroped a single sheet. They did not need to. The whole town could smell the grain.","The paper was two sides long. I held the wax. The heading was the Consortium of Bores, and the body was grain against survey: so many wagons now and so many to follow, in return for the right to measure the quarry and the ground beneath it, and first refusal of whatever the measuring found. The Governor read it through twice at the table with the doors open and the square full of people outside, not one of them speaking. Then he said, \"Write that the Governor signs for bread,\" and I wrote it in the margin of the minute, and he signed. His hand is large, and it shook, and the S of his name goes through two lines. I made the fair copy myself. I have it still.","Frostmonth, 413. The surveyors came. Three of them, in grey, with brass staves and a measuring chain, walking the Flume bank by the Carders' mill one pace at a time and writing every pace down. They drove a stake above the sluice and hung a little glass bell on it, and the bell hummed by itself, very low. The Cantor put his hand on the post and said it was our note, coming up out of the ground. I minuted the visit as Survey, Consortium, one day, no charge.","Harvestmonth, 414. The lease. I want to set the words down exactly, because people say them wrong. The Consortium of Bores takes the quarry, the shaft, and such resonances as lie beneath. In return, bread: a cart a week, at a fixed price, for nine years. The Governor read it aloud standing, in his good coat, and did not look at the room. The bench voted. One voice against, and it was the Cantor's, and I wrote it down: Ashgrove, no. Then I wrote the rest, ayes, carried. I have looked at that page a good deal since. It is the neatest page in the book.","415 and 416. The works. You will know them. The fence round the headframe, that hums if you lay a hand on it. The sheds, the stores, the pay-office where the men are paid in the Consortium's paper, which the Consortium's stores take and nobody else's do. The mast, a hundred and twenty feet of riveted iron on four legs bolted into the quarry rim, that took a summer to raise and that every child in the Reach climbed the hill to look at. A Factor came to live in the works house, in black with green at the cuffs. Grain came every week, as the lease said. Nobody went hungry. I want that set down, because it is true.","417. The bore. They began to sink it and the ground began to ache. You felt it in your teeth before you heard it, a grinding a long way down that has not stopped since. The bells went that year: the Chapel's six and the clock-bell off the Greenhall roof and the little bells in the old reed-rows. A young man of the Church came with the Consortium's cart and said it was a blessing, and the bronze went down the quarry road to be made into teeth for the bore. I minuted it as Requisition, bronze, by order, no charge. That is the second time I have written no charge on a thing that cost us a great deal.","The tankers began after that, once a day, east toward the Organum, green glass sloshing behind iron grilles, and a soldier with a bell for a head walking on either side. The oaks began to ring when the wind moved them. A notice came saying no oak was to be felled, the oaks being now the property of the Consortium, like the quarry, and the Root, and the glass. I had not known until I copied that notice that the Root was a thing that could be owned. I had not known it was a thing at all.","Then we lost the Cantor, and the week after, two men in green and black came to the Greenhall with a crate, and carried his chair out from the Governor's right hand, and put the Queen's seal up in its place: pale green glass, a yard across, with her face pressed into it. I was told to see it dusted.","That is the order things happened in. Here is the one thing I have noticed, and then I will put this away.","Every decree that comes to the Greenhall now comes BY ROYAL ASSENT, under Queen Maelis's seal and signature. I have copied some hundreds of them. I have them in the press beside my desk, and in the same press, at the bottom, I have my fair copy of the paper of 413. When nobody is in the hall I sometimes lay them side by side.","The Queen's hand is small and beautiful and it does not shake. It has not shaken once in a hundred and some decrees. People in the square say that is because she is a hundred and forty years dead, and they say it very quietly, and I do not repeat it.","The Governor's hand goes through two lines.","I am a clerk. I am not supposed to have an opinion about signatures. But I know which of those two hands was afraid of what it was signing, and I know which one I would rather trust."]},
{"id":"lib_a_almanack_419","title":"Tench's Reach Almanack for the Year 419","author":"Cuthbert Tench, almanack-maker; with notes in the margin by Ezra Dunnock, farmer","genre":"almanac","year":"419 SR","origin":"printed at Stillwater Landing; annotated in Oakhaven","where":"p22_bellwether","how":"shop","at":"moor_bookseller","price":1,"style":"paper","summary":"A cheap printed almanack of feasts, weather and planting for the Reach in 419, argued with all year in the margins by an Oakhaven farmer.","text":["TENCH'S REACH ALMANACK for the Year 419. Feasts, Weather, Planting and Sundry Useful Matters, for the Use of Households in the Reach and along the Flume. Printed at Stillwater Landing. One Chime, or the value.","In the margin: Paid a Chime for it at the gate. A Chime! Ezra Dunnock, his book, north field, Oakhaven.","THE COMPILER TO HIS READERS. The year has three hundred and sixty days, twelve months of thirty and forty-five weeks of eight, and so every date falls on the same day of the week as it did last year and will next. A household that keeps this almanack need never buy another. The compiler hopes it will.","In the margin: He hopes it will not, or he is a poor sort of tradesman.","THE EIGHT DAYS, and their old uses, as the country people keep them. Rootday, for setting and sowing. Hearthday, for baking. Massday, for carting and heavy work. Stasisday, for mending what is broken. Dawnday, for the Chapel. Driftday, for journeys. Memoryday, for letters to the absent. Nullday, for rest.","In the margin: My gran would not fell a tree on a Rootday. Said that was the trees' own day and you let them have it. Nobody rests on Nullday. You keep somebody by you on Nullday and you leave the lamp in.","STILLMONTH. The year begins on the first, which is a Dawnday, as it always is, the morning after the Longest Night. The Hearth Feast, the lighting of the forges, on Hearthday the sixth: chiefly kept in the Caldera. Weather: open and mild, as is usual in the Reach. Mend tools, prune, carry muck.","In the margin: Mild. Mended the long fence. Nobody here has lit a forge for a feast in my life.","THAWMONTH. Ice off the Flume, where there is any. Contract Day, the Feast of the Dial, on Stasisday the second, when every term in the province falls due and is made again. Settle accounts. Turn the soil when it will take the spade.","In the margin: There's no ice on the Flume. There never is. Tench has copied that out of somebody's book from the Peaks. Contract Day: went to the Greenhall to make my ground-rent again. The clerk wrote the same figure. Nobody makes a term again in Oakhaven. They copy it out.","SEEDMONTH. The Root Feast on Rootday the first: the planting feast, the one day of the year kept loud on purpose. Sow barley, oats and beans. This is the loudest month in the Reach and the compiler wishes his readers a noisy one.","In the margin: We were loud. Inside the wool, all of us in the square, the old planting song, the whole of it, top to bottom, the first time since the Hedge. The Hedge took it. Nothing came. Then everyone went home very quick and quiet as if we'd done something wrong. Sowed the north field Rootday. The ground said wait. I didn't.","LAMBMONTH. Lambing. Weather: soft. Sow the late peas. Watch the hedges for nesting.","In the margin: No lambs. The flocks went into the Hedge two years back and into us after. Watched the Hedge anyway. It nests something. Not birds.","REEDMONTH. When the wind-reeds come up, Reed-Hanging: take down last year's reeds over the door and hang new, cut to each child's own note. The Cantor's year properly begins.","In the margin: No wind-reeds since the year the Spire went. Cut the girl's new reed from the beds by the Flume, same as last year, and the Cantor tuned it at the door against her own breastbone, and it hangs there. Flume reed does well enough. It doesn't sing like the old ones. Nothing does.","LONGSUN. Hay. The Empyrean, the dawn service, on Dawnday the third, which all are bound to attend. The Longest Day on the thirtieth, a Nullday, on which there is no feast and nothing is to be done.","In the margin: Stood in the square at dawn as the paper says. Chapel-reader read the service. Nobody sang. Did nothing on the thirtieth. Felt it all day, like weather coming. Hay was in twice. Cut it Massday and it stood again by Dawnday, thinner. The second hay smells of nothing.","DUSTMONTH. Dry. The Feast of the Drift and the Great Race on Driftday the sixth, on the Coast, where the captains keep it. Water stock. Watch for fire.","In the margin: Not dry. Damp all month, and everything growing as if it were being chased. Sapling up through the yard cobbles. Sawed it. It came back from the cut by morning. Sawed it again. That is my Dustmonth, Tench.","FIRSTFRUIT. Early apples, plums. The Eel-Run, the night the eels go down the Flume, late in the month: lanterns, traps, and quiet.","In the margin: No eels. Old men at the crossing say they'll go down late and they've never been wrong about eels. Apples split on the bough from swelling. Pressed what I could.","SHEAFMONTH. Reaping. The Keystone, the Weighing at Fortis, on Massday the seventh, where the soldiers weigh their kit and cut names in the wall. Bind sheaves dry.","In the margin: Harrow had it off a carter that they cut more names this year than last at Fortis. Reaped the long field. Bread from it tastes of the bin. Third cut since the spring. Every cut is less. My girl asked why bread used to taste of something. I said it still does, you're just used to it. She knew I was lying. They always do, at eight.","HARVESTMONTH. The Last Sheaf, which the almanack cannot date, for it falls when the last field is cut, and is known by the sheaf hung on the gate. The whole village cuts the last stand together, one cut to the beat. The sheaf is plaited and hung over the gate till spring, and is the one sheaf the Reach lets stay cut.","In the margin: The last stand is mine this year, in the north field, the corner by the oaks. Had the plait-straw ready and the girl had her ribbon. Then the Church came up the Greenroot road singing their one note with the white fire on poles, and the reader read it out in the square: there is to be a Rite on Hearthday the sixteenth, and the Last Sheaf is to wait until after, so as not to sing two things at once. So it waits. It stands there in the corner with the light on it. I went out at dusk and put my hand on it. Feels like it's waiting for something.","ASHFALL. Lift roots. The Canopy, the remembering of the dead, on Memoryday the seventh. (Not presently kept.)","FROSTMONTH. Mild, as is usual in the Reach. Ringing-Wood Day at the first hard frost, when the year's timber is felled and named. The Longest Night on the thirtieth, a Stasisday. The Reach has known one hard winter in living memory, in the year of the Fracture, and the almanack does not look for another.","In the margin, the last thing written in the book: Sheaf still standing. We'll cut it when we're let."]},
{"id":"lib_a_orchard_craft","title":"The Orchard Craft: A Short History of the Cantors of the Reach","author":"Jessamy Yarrow, Cantor, of the Reedside Conservatory","genre":"history","year":"396 SR","origin":"Oakhaven (the Reedside Conservatory)","where":"lyra_house","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"history","style":"parch","summary":"Written while the wind-reed orchards still sang, a Cantor's proud, funny and slightly cross account of what her craft is, where it came from, and why it is not a church.","text":["I have been asked by the Cantor-Master to write down the history of our craft for the new students, because the Conservatory has a library and the library has a gap on its shelf where our history ought to be. I said that the craft has no history, only a great many old women who remember things. She said, then write down the old women. So I have.","Nobody knows how old the craft is. Ask a Cantor and she will hum at you, which is not an answer but is the truth. It is older than the Greenhall, which the Cantors built three hundred years ago as a guildhall for the reed-growers, and where a Cantor still sits at the Governor's right hand on court days and hums when the petitions grow dull. It is older than the reed-orchards. My own teacher, Mother Sefton, who was ninety when she taught me and had been taught by a woman of ninety, used to say that the craft was old when the Synod was a rumour, and that it would be old when the Synod was a rumour again. She said this in the Chapel porch, loudly. She was a great trial to the Chapel-reader.","What is the craft? People from outside the Reach think it is singing. It is not, or not chiefly. It is listening, and then giving back the right note at the right moment, and very often the right note is barely a note at all.","A student comes to the Conservatory at eight, and the first thing she learns is not a song but a breath: four counts in, a long fall out. For a year she breathes on seedlings. Trees hear breath before voice. Children who come wanting to sing find this very dull, and the best of them go on finding it dull for about a month and then stop finding it anything, and that is how you know.","Then she learns the Root note, the note the whole Reach is tuned to: one hundred and seventy-four beats to the second, sung a hand's width above the ground with the mouth nearly closed. That is the note for bringing things on: seedlings, grafts, wounds, babies. A Cantor at a lying-in hums it into a clay jar pressed to the mother's belly, so that the note goes into the body instead of into the room. That trick is older than anything else we have, and it works, and nobody knows why, and every midwife in the Reach has learned it from one of us and pretends she thought of it herself. We let her.","Then she learns the falling fifth, which is the Root note and the note a fifth below it, for letting things down: a fever, a fright, a branch that has grown wrong. And the calling-song, the one loud thing in the craft, for bringing lost lambs home across three fields, which every student practises on the Conservatory roof at dusk and which the whole of Reedside has learned to sleep through. At sixteen, last of all, she learns the felling-song, which is sung to an old tree before the axe: the falling fifth held, let go, and then a note below it that you feel in the floor more than hear. It tells the sap to go down. I have sung it four times in my life and I wept at three of them, and so does everybody, and that is correct.","That is the whole of it. A breath, a note, a fifth, a call, a farewell. Everything else a Cantor does is made of those five things, put together in the right order for the person or the tree in front of her.","We have no catechism. I want that understood, because the Chapel-reader has more than once called the craft a superstition and more than once called it a heresy, and it cannot be both. We do not pray to the trees. We ask them things. A Cantor can lay her palm flat on the bark of an old pear and be still, and the tree will answer one question, yes or no, if you ask it plainly. That is not worship. That is manners.","The Church teaches that holiness is one note held without end, and that the second note is sin. I do not quarrel with the Church; it has its own business. But I will point out, for the students, that there is not one tending in the whole craft that is done on a single note. The fifth is two notes. The felling-song is three. The craft is made out of the distances between notes, the way a hedge is made out of the gaps between the stakes, and anyone who has stood in the orchards in a west wind knows that one note on its own is not a song. It is only a noise that has not been answered yet.","The orchards. The Cantors planted the wind-reed orchards row by row over three hundred years, and tuned them by pruning: you cut the long cane to sound the Root, and you let the others down a little at a time over many seasons, until a whole slope of them gives a chord when the wind comes off the hills. People in Greenroot, three miles off, know the weather by which chord the orchards are singing. There are rows on the south slope that were tuned by women whose names nobody remembers, and they are still in tune. That is our history. It is standing in the field.","Two customs I cannot explain, and will set down so that somebody else may.","First: there is a lullaby, five notes, a turn, the five again lower, and a fall, that every mother in the Reach sings, and every Cantor learns before any other song. It is the oldest thing we have. No one has ever said who made it or what it was first made for. Mother Sefton said it was made for something very large that could not sleep.","Second: on the Longest Night, every Cantor in the Reach goes out to the oldest tree in her orchard and hums into its hollow until dawn. I have done it every year of my life, with frost on my shawl, and I have never once been told why. When I asked Mother Sefton, she said only that it was done in place of. I said, in place of what? And she looked at me for a long time and said, in place of what we used to do, and would not say more, and I do not think she knew.","Every mending borrows. That is the last thing a student is told, and the first thing she forgets. A Cantor's voice is her own body, and whatever she gives out through it comes out of the same account. There are old Cantors in the Reach with no voice left at all, and they are not sorry.","I have written this down not because I am afraid our craft will be lost. Nothing the whole Reach hums in its sleep can be lost. I have written it so that the students have something on the shelf to argue with. If you want to know what this book was trying to say, put it down and go and stand in the reed-rows when the wind comes off the hills. They will tell you better than I can."]},
{"id":"lib_a_first_history","title":"A First History of Oakhaven, for the Orchard School","author":"Garra Thorne, teacher at the orchard school","genre":"history","year":"419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"tavern","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"history","style":"paper","summary":"A schoolteacher's history of Oakhaven in eight short lessons, from three rival stories of its founding to the planting of the Hedge, with questions for her pupils at the end of each.","text":["To the children of the orchard school. This book is to be read aloud in class, quietly, a lesson at a time. When you come to the questions, answer them on your slates. Do not shout out. You know why.","Lesson One. Where Oakhaven stands. Oakhaven is the chief town of the Resonant Reach. It stands on a rise above the Chlorophyll Flume, which is the green river that runs in a channel of grown wood and climbs when it should fall. Downriver, three miles east, is Greenroot. West are the Watch Downs and the combe at Wethercombe. East, a long way east, past the Spectral Woods, is the Organum, which you will not see, and need not. All round us are the iron-oaks, which ring when the wind moves them.","Questions. Which way does the Flume run? Which way does it want to run? Why do we not climb the oaks any more?","Lesson Two. How Oakhaven began. Nobody knows, and so there are three stories, and you may choose.","The drovers' story is that a man was bringing sheep down off the Downs in a storm, and lay down under the great oak on the green, the one we call the Old Haven, and the oak hummed him to sleep, and in the morning he would not leave, and built a house against its roots. That is why it is Oakhaven: a haven in the oaks.","The Cantors' story is that there were Cantors here before there were houses, singing to the orchards, and the town grew up round their school the way a village grows round a well.","The clerks' story is that Oakhaven began when somebody first wrote it down, and the first roll in the Greenhall is three hundred years old, so Oakhaven is three hundred years old.","The clerks' story is the only one with paper in it. It is also the only one nobody believes.","Questions. Which story do you like best? Which story do you think is true? Are those the same story?","Lesson Three. The Queens. Long ago the Reach had its own crown, as every one of the eight realms did. The last of our queens was Queen Maelis. Her face is on the green glass seal in the Greenhall and on every notice at the well-house. She reigns still, at the Organum, among the other monarchs of the Glass Court, and signs the Synod's decrees for us in her own hand.","We do not say more than that in school.","Questions. Copy out the words at the top of the newest notice on the well-house. Whose seal is under them?","Lesson Four. The Greenhall and the orchards. Three hundred years ago the Cantors built the Greenhall for the reed-growers, and carved every beam with reeds and apple boughs and singing birds. Over the same three hundred years they planted the wind-reed orchards below the town, row by row, and tuned them by pruning, so that when the wind came off the hills the whole valley sang. Your grandparents could tell you the weather by which chord the orchards were singing. Ask them.","Questions. Ask someone at home what the orchards sounded like. Write down what they say. If they will not say, write that down instead.","Lesson Five. The year the Spire broke. In 412, far away in the Dawning Heights, the great Spire of Light broke, and the whole world went a little out of tune. On the same morning every wind-reed in the Reach turned to something like glass and blew away as dust. The winter after was the hard winter, the only one anyone remembers, and seventeen people of Oakhaven died of it. The sexton has written an account. In the spring the Consortium of Bores brought grain up the Greenroot road, and the Governor signed for it, and Oakhaven ate.","Questions. Why do you think the Governor signed? What would you have done? (You may leave this one blank.)","Lesson Six. The quarry. In 414 the Consortium leased the quarry north of the town, and the shaft, and what was under it. They built the works, and the fence that hums, and the great iron mast. In 417 they began the bore. The bells of Oakhaven were taken down that year, all of them, and sent to the quarry. That is why there are no bells, and why, if you find a bell, you do not ring it.","Questions. Put your hand flat on the schoolroom floor. What do you feel? Do not write it down. Just know it.","Lesson Seven. The Hedge. In the summer of 417 the town planted the Hedge: blackthorn, bramble and hawthorn, three men deep, packed with the wool of every sheep in the Reach. Everybody helped. Some of you helped, though you were very small, carrying fleece in your aprons. The Hedge swallows every sound we make, so that nothing outside can hear us. That is why we are safe. That is why we murmur. That is why, when you are sent to the gate, you go no further than the gate.","Questions. Name the three trees of the Hedge. What is the wool for? Who keeps the key?","Lesson Eight. The last lesson. When you are grown, someone from outside may ask you what Oakhaven was. You will tell them about the oaks and the orchards and the Greenhall and the Hedge. I hope you will also tell them that the children of Oakhaven learned to murmur before they learned to read, and learned to read anyway, and that this was not nothing.","And one question, as always, to finish.","What is on the other side of the Hedge?","Do not answer this one aloud. Write it on your slate, and look at it, and then rub it out."]},
{"id":"lib_a_four_seconds","title":"Four Seconds at the Margin","author":"Gerda Stahl, of Tier Three, Cinderhold; taken down and printed on Broadsheet Row","genre":"chronicle","year":"418 SR","origin":"printed on Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile; carried up the river through Stillwater Landing","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"history","style":"paper","summary":"A drill-hand of Cinderhold's Tier Three tells, between the hammer-falls, how nine hundred of her tier died in four seconds at the Thermal Margin, and why she is the one left to tell it.","text":["Printed on Broadsheet Row. The printer's note: This was spoken to us by a woman of Cinderhold who came down the coast in the spring with nothing but a gauge-house key on a string. She would only talk between the falls of a hammer, so we found her one at the forge on the Lantern Yard, and wrote down what she said in the spaces. We have kept the spaces.","I am Gerda Stahl, of Tier Three. Still standing.","You want the Margin. Everyone wants the Margin. Let the hammer come round.","The Margin is the ring of drills outside the caldera, where the heat comes up out of the ground and the Consortium puts pipes in it. Tier Three works it. Worked it. Nine hundred and seven on the day shift, though nobody had counted until after. My Odo had the number-six head. I had the gauge-house behind it. That is a stone hut the size of a cart with a basalt wall a yard thick on the drill side, and a slot to read the needle through.","The order came on a Massday in 417, by a runner in grey. Pressure up by a third. The Synod wanted more out of the ground and wanted it before the Weighing. Our foreman read it out between falls. He said it was against the seal-tolerance. The runner said the tolerance had been revised. The foreman said, revised by who. The runner said, by people who are not standing here. That was true.","We put the pressure up. You do what the paper says. Odo kissed me at the gauge-house door. He tasted of sulphur. Everybody did.","For nine days it held. The needles sat high and steady and the Synod's men wrote it down. On the tenth day the fires in the drill-houses went cold.","Not out. Cold. You have not seen it, down here by your sea. A furnace burning white, and frost on the iron round it. Men pulled their hands off the rails with the skin left behind, and it was the frost that burned them. We have had the Cold Burn on the upper tiers since the Screaming Year. We had never had it at the Margin. The Margin was the hottest place in the world.","Let the hammer come round.","Then the whistle. The old women have a name for it. F-sharp. It is the heat remembering what it is for. It goes up out of the ground like a kettle the size of a mountain, and when you hear it you have four seconds, and the only thing in the world that will stand four seconds of it is basalt.","I was in the gauge-house. I did not decide anything. My body got down behind the wall before I knew the sound had a name.","One.","Two. I heard Odo shout my name from the number-six head. He was forty yards off in the open. He was not shouting for help. He was shouting to tell me where he was, so I would not come out to look.","Three. I did not come out. I want that printed. I did not go out. A Cinderholder will call that cold. Let them.","Four.","When I stood up, the frost had gone off everything at once and the air was so hot it hurt the eyes, and the Margin was quiet, and there was nobody on it. Not bodies. Shapes on the ground like the outlines a soot-fall leaves round a cart when you move it. Nine hundred of us, from the drill-heads out to the pipe-road. Seven came out alive. Six were in the gauge-houses, like me. The seventh is a scrapper of ours who dived behind a basalt wall-end out in the open, and he has not stopped talking about it since, loud, in every tavern on the tier, because when he stops he hears it.","The Consortium sent a clerk the next week with a form for the widows. Next of kin, number of the head, hours worked. There was a line for cause, and the cause was printed already. Seal failure. Nobody had looked at a seal.","They say in the deep galleries that the glass down there whispers. I never worked the deep galleries. But I will tell you what the Margin said, in the half-second before the whistle, under it, and I have not told this at home because I know what they would call me. It said a word. It sounded like a word. It sounded like somebody who has been holding something hot for a very long time, saying, enough.","They are talking about a strike in the guild-halls now. They talk about it between the falls, very low. When it comes, I will be there.","Odo kept his hand on it. On the ingot. Let the hammer come round."]},
{"id":"lib_a_what_is_written","title":"What Is Written: A Short Account of the Silent Veil, for Novices","author":"Umber, scribe of the House","genre":"history","year":"410 SR","origin":"the House of the Silent Veil, the Spectral Woods","where":"mausoleum","how":"shelf","at":"lib_scriptorium","shelf":"history","style":"parch","summary":"An elderly Veil scribe welcomes new novices to the House with a short, proud history of her order, its rules and customs, and its happy arrangement with the Office of the Catalogue.","text":["You have come up the long path through the willows, and stood under the round arch, and read the words over it, though you could not read them, because they are written in the old pitched hand. Here is what they say. What is written is not lost.","That is the whole of our faith, if we have one. The rest of this little book is only the furniture.","The House has stood on its rise above the pool for eleven hundred years. The Veil is older than the House. Nobody knows how much older, which is a strange thing to admit in a house of records, and I admit it. The order kept its books in other places before this one. They were burned, or drowned, or forgotten, which is how we learned to bind the Ledgers as we do. The first of them was bound eleven hundred years ago out of something that is not paper, and what it holds it holds against fire and water and time. You will not touch one for some years. When you do, you will feel why.","There are three rules, and you will learn them before you learn your letters. Write what happened. Write who it happened to. Do not comfort yourself in the margin.","The third is the hard one. You will be asked, in your life, to stand in a place where something terrible is happening and write it down while it happens. That is what the Veil is for. Kings have been crowned and realms have ended and cities have gone under the water, and in every one of those places there was a scribe of the Veil standing a little way off with a board on her arm, writing. We do not help. We do not hinder. We take no side, because a record that takes a side can be unwritten by the other one. This will seem cruel to you at first. It is the opposite of cruel. It is the one kindness that lasts. A thing that has been witnessed truly cannot be made never to have happened, by anyone, ever.","When you are sworn you will set your own name aside, as you set your cloak on the peg at the door, and take a designation from the ink-cupboard, as every scribe of the House has done. Mine is Umber. I have had it fifty-one years and I do not miss the other. When I lay the pen down it will go back in the cupboard for the next novice, as it came to me. It is kept. Nothing here is lost.","The House. The scriptorium is on the first floor at the east end: fourteen sloped desks in two rows, a stove at either end. The duty-scribe lights the east stove at the second bell. On the last day of every month we clean the desks with vinegar, so the whole floor smells of it, and of tallow, and of wet wool, because the Woods are damp and so are we. You will love that smell before your first winter is out, and you will never be able to describe it to anybody, so do not try.","Below the kitchens a stair goes down into the labyrinth. Its passages were cut to the shape of a chord that silences, so that nothing below can be heard above. Below the labyrinth are the stacks, where the roots of the Weeping Canopy come down through the ceiling. Novices are not taken there. On Memoryday the duty-scribe goes down with a lamp and reads one page of the dead aloud, in the flat voice, from wherever the last scribe left off, and comes up again. Nobody has ever been able to tell me to whom she reads it. It has been done every Memoryday for longer than the House has stood, and it will be done every Memoryday after I am gone.","The dead of the Woods are not like the dead of other places. When a soul goes out here the Echo catches it, as the songs say, and the Grove keeps it. So we keep the Canopy, the remembering of the dead, with more feeling than they do elsewhere, and on the night the Grove's water is highest we set lanterns on it, one name to a lantern, read aloud by whoever sets it. You will set your first lantern this year. Choose your name carefully.","The Office. A hundred and ten years ago the Veil came into the Synod's house as the Office of the Catalogue. Some of the old scribes wept, I am told, and three walked out into the Woods and were not seen again. I understand them, and I think they were wrong. Before the Office we were three hundred and twelve scribes with a leaking roof, begging vellum from the Moot. Now we have wages, and grey ink by the cask, and a place in the Organum's great archive where our oldest books are drier than they have ever been. The Office has never once asked us to alter an entry. The Registrar rules a page more beautifully than anyone I have known, and he likes to say that every name is a unit, by which he means that none may be counted twice and none left out. That is the Veil's own rule in a clerk's coat, and I was glad to hear it.","This year the Office has asked the House for twelve scribes for a great register of the Woods: every soul in the Grove and the villages round it, by name and voice and age, the most complete account of any country that has ever been attempted. I have put my own name forward, old as I am. I hope you will one day be chosen for such work yourselves.","It is a great thing to be trusted with every name in a country. I do not think a scribe of the Veil has ever been trusted with more."]},
{"id":"lib_a_going_up","title":"Going Up: How the Free Captains Took to the Air","author":"Piet Ollander, keeper of the mast-tower, Port Cantabile","genre":"history","year":"416 SR","origin":"printed on Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile","where":"p16k_kestrel_below","how":"quest","at":"flume_lowrings","quest":"odile","style":"paper","summary":"The old keeper of Port Cantabile's mast-tower explains, with no great respect for anybody, how skyships came to be, and why sea-captains who swore they never would have started to fly them.","text":["I have kept the mast-tower at the end of the Long Mole for twenty-two years. It is sixty feet of iron rungs with a cradle at the top, and in the first ten of those years I moored perhaps one ship a month, and every one of them flew green and black and paid me in Consortium paper and looked at me as if I were part of the iron. Now I moor four a day, and half of them are ours, and they look at me as if I were their mother. I liked it better before. Here is how it changed.","Skyships are not ours. I say that first, because the young ones forget it. They were the Consortium's, and before that they were nobody's, because a Fortis-cut core will make a hull light but will not make her rise, and there was no drift-glass to be had. My father's generation never saw one. Then the drills got good. When the runic bore came in, the Consortium began to bleed the Velocity country, and what came out was a green glass, pale as sea-ice, that wanted more than anything in the world to move. Pack it in brass with a reed-valve on the end and it will strain at the valve like a dog at a door. The whole art of the skyship, the engineers say, is to persuade it that the direction it most wants to go is up. They say this as if it were a small thing. I have seen what it does when it changes its mind.","So the first skyships were survey craft, and then freighters, slow and fat under their envelopes, carrying for the Consortium what the Consortium did not trust to a road. A six-day march became a day's flight. The Synod built its own cutters and painted them iron-grey. The Coast watched them go over and spat, and went on sailing, because a Free Captain has a deck and a keel and the sea, and the sea has always been enough trouble for anyone.","Then the Screaming Year, and the sea changed its mind too.","You will have heard. The tides started singing in keys nobody could read. The water would forget, for an hour, that it was water, and go to a blue jelly round a hull, and when it remembered, the hull would be gone. And the trenches: a mouth in the sea, opening under a ship with no warning, with no bottom to it, and closing again on the far side. A captain who had sailed forty years could not tell you on any given morning whether the channel would still be a channel by noon. Hulls began to be laid up at their moorings. Crews went to the bottle-trade, or the drink, or both.","The first of ours to go up was Ysmay Cordell. She bought a condemned Consortium freighter at the yard auction for the price of its brass, patched the envelope with her own mainsails, and took four lift-tanks off a Synod cutter that had come down on the Cay, which is another story and a better one. She named her the Gull's Errand and brought her into my cradle on a still evening with half the Coast on the Mole to watch her fall in the harbour. She did not fall. She moored like a feather, came down my rungs, and said, I am not going back down there, and pointed at the water.","Then came the question of the Articles. The Articles were written for the sea, and the Free Captains will not have a thing they do not have an Article for. Was a ship in the air at sea? If she came over the harbour with nobody at the wheel, did the Ninth give her to the Harbour, as it would a hull drifting in under no hand? If she dropped a crate in the water, did the Sixteenth give it to whoever fished it up? The captains argued it three nights running. The Harbour ruled, in the flat voice it uses for hangings and tide-tables, that anything over the harbour is at sea, however high it goes. There is a rope from my cradle to the Mole-head derrick now, and on the derrick a board, and on the board the Articles, and somebody has chalked under them: and above.","Most of the Coast still sails. The old ones will not fly; they say a ship ought to have something under her she can feel. The young ones will not sail; they have seen too many laid-up hulls. And there is a third sort, which I have moored more than once, who fly and will not even look at the water. They come down my rungs with their eyes on the iron, and walk the whole length of the Mole looking at the town, and if you ask them about the sea they talk about the price of drift-glass, which is a scandal, a hundred and ten sovereigns a tank and rising.","People ask me if the air is safer. It is not. A holed tank does not burst. It weeps, a cobalt smoke, very slowly, and takes the lift out with it, and you have as long as it takes to weep to find somewhere to come down. I have watched ships come in nose-high and crabbing and nobody speaking on deck.","But I will tell you what the captains tell me, at the top of the tower, when they have had a drink. The sea wants you. That is what changed. It always had you, if it could get you, but now it wants you, the way a hound wants. The air does not want anything. It does not care whether you live. Some days, they say, that is a great comfort."]},
{"id":"lib_b_narrow_cry","title":"A Good Narrow Cry","author":"Damaris Teasel, midwife of Stillwater Landing","genre":"memoir","year":"418 SR","origin":"Stillwater Landing","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"lives","style":"parch","summary":"An old river-port midwife sets down forty years at the bed-foot, from the loud births of her girlhood to the whispered ones of now.","text":["I am asked by the girl who keeps the books at the Bonded Store, who can read and write and is not shy about either, to put down what I know before I am too old to know it. I said I had nothing in me worth the ink. She said then I could waste the ink, she would bring more. So this is her ink, and these are my hands, and you must forgive the both of them.","I have been the midwife at Stillwater Landing for forty-one years. I learned from my aunt, who learned from hers, and between us three women we have caught every child born on this basin since before the lock had iron gates. I have delivered the sons of every man who ever lied to the lockkeeper, and a fair few of the lockkeepers' own, and I will say no more about that, because they all still drink here.","When I was a girl a birth was a loud thing. I want that written down first, because the young ones do not believe it. A woman shouted if she liked, and she generally liked. The men stood in the yard and sang to keep from fretting, and if it went long they sang worse, and the whole wharf knew how a labour was going by the state of the singing. There was a reed hung over the birthing door, cut for the household by the Cantors upriver, and it sounded in every wind. And we waited for the first cry the way you wait for the tide to turn, with our whole bodies, and when it came we laughed out loud. I have heard a boatman cry at a first cry and not be ashamed of it. That was the world.","Then the Screaming Year came, and the hounds came with it, and by the next spring every birthing room on the Flume was packed with fleece and hung with felt like a coffin lined for winter. I cut my first bite-strap out of a harness my husband was still using. He noticed. I learned to put my mouth to a woman's ear and say, in the Narrow, love, in the Narrow, and to keep saying it, for hours if need be, until she could hear nothing else. I learned to measure a cry with my palm, a hand's width of voice and no more. I learned to be afraid of the very sound I had spent twenty years praying for.","You can learn anything. That is the dreadful thing about people.","I will tell one story from those years, because it is the one they ask for at the Store. Keziah off the rafts went into labour on a timber raft in the middle of the basin, with the drovers ashore and the lock shut and no boat to fetch her in. I rowed out alone with my bag on my knees. She had the strap in her teeth already and bit it so hard she broke a tooth on it, and she never once rose above a murmur, and I was prouder of her than of any woman I have ever tended. And then the boy came out roaring. A great red roar, the kind I used to laugh at, going out across the open water where there was no Hedge and no wool and nothing at all to stop it. Every soul on the wharf stood still. I could hear the reeds on the far bank go quiet, the way they do. And Keziah, without a word, took him off me and put him to the breast, and he stopped as if she had shut a door. There is no Narrow in the world as good as a full mouth. I have taught that to every girl since. It is the most useful thing I know.","Then they put the drill into the quarry at Oakhaven, and something came down the Flume that you could not see, and I learned to be afraid of the other thing.","I will not write much about the still ones. The Grafters call it infant pallor, and write it in their book, and go on to the next house. I am an old woman and I know what the old women call it, and I will not write that either, because some words you do not leave lying about where a child could pick them up. I will say only this. The first one I lost, I sat with the mother until the lamps were lit, and neither of us said anything, which was the worst thing we could have done and we both knew it. In the morning there was less of him. I have his name in my own book. I have eleven names in it now. I read them over on Nullday, under my breath, so that the room does not go quiet while I do.","It was a midwife upriver who put me right. She has no more voice than I have, a voice like a gate on a windy night, and she told me she had watched the Cantors hum through a clay jar laid to the belly, so that the note goes into the child instead of into the air, and that she did it herself when there was no Cantor to send for. I said I could not sing. She said the jar does not know that. She said a bad note held is better than a good note kept in your mouth, and she said it in a way that made me think she had lost a few before anyone told her, too.","So now I hum. I hum the Root note, low and flat and wandering, and I am sure it is the worst Root note ever sounded on this river. I hum it through the jar while the mother labours, and when the child comes out silent, which they do more often now, I bend over the soft of the head and hum it straight in. And mostly, not always, there is a breath, and a second, and then a small offended sound like a moth bumping a windowpane, and I say what my aunt said and her aunt before her, though the meaning has turned right round: there, a good cry. Only now I say a good Narrow cry. She'll live.","One thousand, eight hundred and six children, by my count, and by the girl's, who checked it. Most of Stillwater, then. If you are reading this at the Store, the odds are you were one of mine, and I have very likely smacked you since.","So here is my instruction, for what it cost me. Keep to the Narrow. Keep a lamp whistling. Keep a jar by the bed. And when your time comes, send for me, and if I am not there to come, hum anyway. Badly will do. Badly has always done."]},
{"id":"lib_b_stone_then_glass","title":"Stone, Then Glass","author":"Silas Hurlock, quarryman, of Oakhaven","genre":"autobiography","year":"419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"quarry","how":"found","at":"quarry","style":"parch","summary":"A quarryman who cut Oakhaven's millstones and chapel steps for thirty years tells, bluntly, what it is to work the same hole for the Consortium.","text":["My name is Silas Hurlock. I have cut stone out of the Oakhaven quarry for thirty-four years, and for the last two I have cut none at all and been paid better for it, and I should like somebody to explain that to me before I die.","I went into the quarry at twelve, under old Master Cleeve, who had a voice like a dropped anvil and the softest hands in the Reach, because he had not used them for anything but pointing since before my father was born. In those days it was a hole in the ground and a good one. A bowl of grey stone, stepped down in terraces a quarter-mile across, and at the bottom of it the water, black and still, so cold in the summers that when you dived your teeth sang in your head. Half of Oakhaven learned to swim down there. I courted Sukey on the third ledge with my boots off. She will tell you it was the fourth. It was the third.","The work was simple. You read the stone, you set your wedges along the line it wanted to break on, and you struck, and if you had read it right the block came away clean and rang when you tapped it, like a struck pot. That ringing was how you knew. A good block sang. A bad one thudded and you broke it up for rubble and said nothing about it in the alehouse. We cut millstones for every mill from here to Greenroot, and lintels for half the doors in town, and the steps of the Chapel of the Pure Note, which I will come back to.","In the year of the lease, the Consortium came with paper from the Governor and pumped the bottom dry in eleven days. I stood on the rim and watched the water go down off the ledge where I courted Sukey. Then they put the headframe in where the water had been, and since the bore the quarry has been lit from underneath, green as a bottle held up to a candle, all night and all day, so that you lose the habit of shadows.","I will tell you what the work is now, because nobody else will. We do not cut. We haul. We move timber for the shoring and iron for the gantries, we clear spoil off the terraces, we wheel barrows up and down the old steps I cut myself as a boy. We keep the glass-channels clean. We load the tanker-wagons at the bays and stand back while they go out east with a bell-headed soldier walking beside each one. Day-hands do not go down the shaft. I have never been down the shaft. I can feel it, though. Everyone can. It is in your back teeth from the moment you come through the gate, a grinding that does not stop, so that you go home at night and lie by your wife with your jaw aching like you have been chewing gravel. Sukey says I grind my teeth in my sleep now. I tell her that is not me.","We are paid in scrip. Green and black paper with a drill and a wheel on it, which is to say a promise from the Consortium, signed by the Consortium, that the Consortium owes me. I am told this is money. The works store takes it at its face. The Boar & Dulcimer takes it too, and I will not say at what rate, because I have to go on drinking there.","Some things a quarryman learns. You do not stand near the soldiers at the gate. You pass them close because you must, and as you pass you hear what everyone hears, the murmur inside the bell, like somebody talking in their sleep in the next room. You learn to say good morning to them, very civil, because the Factor walks the line of them every morning with her coloured lenses on and listens to each one, and the young engineers say she can hear back everything they have seen. So I say good morning, and I say it nicely, and I have never once complained about the scrip within ten yards of a bell.","There is a crew at the pumps none of us has ever had a drink with. Contract men, the foreman says, brought in from elsewhere, who keep to the pumps the way the pumps keep to them. They do not come up to the canteen. They do not come up at all that I have seen. I asked once. The foreman looked at me the way Master Cleeve used to look at a block that thudded, and I have not asked since.","This year the bore-head went. I was on the third terrace with a barrow of spoil, and I felt it before I heard anything, a pitch climbing in my teeth like a kettle coming to the boil, faster than any kettle, and then the whole bowl jumped under me once, the way a dog jumps when you tread on its tail in its sleep. Then the green light down the shaft went brighter for a moment, and then quiet. They brought up four from the platform at the face. Marten, who owed me two Chimes from before the Fracture and knew it. Old Coyle. The Tanner twins, who were not twenty. The Cantor sang the twins down side by side, in the Narrow, so low that I had to stand near to hear it, and I stood near. The Consortium paid their mother in scrip.","Now the chapel steps. I cut them the summer I was twenty-two, eleven of them, out of the best seam the quarry ever gave. The third step from the top has a flaw in it shaped like a running hare. Master Cleeve wanted it broken up. I said nobody would ever see it but the people who looked at their feet, and those were the people I cut it for. He let me keep it in.","On Nulldays I go and sit on that step. It is the last stone in Oakhaven that does not glow, and does not hum, and does not ache in your teeth when you put your hand on it. It is just stone, cold, where I left it. Stone only ever wanted to be left where it was. You would think that was the easiest thing in the world to give it. Nobody did."]},
{"id":"lib_b_the_pruner","title":"The Pruner: A Life of Winifred Ruddock, Cantor of the Reed-Rows","author":"Jennet Lindley, of the Oakhaven dye-works, once her pupil","genre":"biography","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"lyra_house","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"lives","style":"parch","summary":"An old dye-works woman remembers the Cantor who taught her for one year, tuned a valley with a pair of shears, and once sang a Church examiner into silence.","text":["They will tell you a Cantor is a voice. Win Ruddock would have told you a Cantor is a pair of shears.","I was her pupil for one year, the year I was twelve, which was so long ago that the people who remember it with me could sit round one small table, and soon will not need to. I am writing this down because the young ones at the vats keep asking what the orchards were, and I find that I cannot tell them without telling them about her.","She was not a big woman. She was brown and narrow and bent a little forward, as if she were always walking into a wind, and she had the shears on a cord round her neck the way other women wear a locket. Every Cantor kept a row of the wind-reeds in those days, and Win kept the longest, the one that ran from the old stone wall clear down to the water, nine hundred reeds by her own count. A reed is tuned by cutting. Shorter sounds higher. Cut a hair too much and it is spoilt for a year. She would stand in her row in the evening with the wind coming down off the hills and listen with her head on one side, and then reach out, quick as a heron, and take the very tip off one reed out of nine hundred, and the whole row would come right. I never once saw her cut twice.","When the wind came properly, the rows sang. I do not know how to make you hear it. Slow chords, big as weather, rolling down the valley one into the next, so that a man ploughing at Greenroot would stop and lift his head. It was the most ordinary thing in the world, like rain. We did not even think it beautiful. You do not think your mother's voice is beautiful. It is just the sound the world makes when it is all right.","I came to her with no voice to speak of. My mother had hopes. Win heard me sing one verse of the harvest song and said, kindly, that I had a very good ear and a voice like a wet sack, and that ears were the rarer thing, and she would have me for a year anyway, because somebody had to hold the ladder. So I held the ladder. I carried the lantern and the twine and the pot of pitch for sealing cuts. And I watched her work, which no one else alive now did, and so it falls to me.","She sang to everything. To apple trees on frost nights, with her lips to the bark, a low note going down into the wood to keep the sap moving. To a farrier's boy in a fever, sitting on the end of his cot for two nights and never once raising her voice. To the cows at the Pellow place, which she said was nonsense but which made the farmer happy, and a happy farmer pays in cheese. She tuned the First Reeds for every child born in our end of the valley. She would lay a raw cane against the baby's breastbone and hum until the cane hummed back, and then cut the slot to what she heard, and that reed went over the door and sounded in every wind, and every Reed-Hanging after, the household cut the new one to the old one's note. My mother kept the first reed Win cut for me in a drawer for sixty years. I have it still. It still sounds.","She had sayings, and she said them so often that I can still hear the exact tune of them. One note is a shout, two notes is a conversation. Never sing to a tree you would not prune. A Cantor who wants to be listened to should go on the stage.","Now the thing everybody asks about, which is the man from the Church.","In the year I was with her, the Choir-Church sent an examiner up from the Organum to look into the Cantors of the Reach. Somebody had written to somebody that we taught the children intervals, which we did, and that intervals were the second note, which they are, and that the second note is sin, which every child in the Reach could recite and none of us believed. He came in white with a clerk behind him, a tall soft-spoken man who was polite to everyone and frightened Win's dog, and he sat in her kitchen and asked her to sing him one note, so that he could judge whether it was pure.","She said she would be glad to, but not indoors. She said a note wants room. She took him out into her row at dusk, and I came behind with the lantern, and she stood him in the middle of nine hundred reeds and opened her mouth and sang one note, the Root note, very plain, and held it.","And the wind came down off the hills.","I have thought about it for sixty years and I do not believe she planned it, and I do not believe she did not. The whole row took up her note and spread it into a chord, and the next row took the chord and turned it, and the next, all the way down the valley in the dark, until we stood in the middle of a sound as wide as the sky, with her one plain note still at the bottom of it like a stone at the bottom of a river. The examiner's clerk sat down on the ground. The examiner did not move at all. When it was over he stood there a long time. Then he thanked her, very civilly, and went back to the Organum, and I have been told by people who should know that his report on the Cantors of the Reach was two words long. Agricultural custom. We have lived inside those two words ever since. I like to think she wrote them.","She grew old the way the reeds did, from the top down. Toward the end she could not climb, and pruned from a stool that I carried behind her down the row, a few yards a day. She died in her chair by the window in the late summer of three hundred and ninety-one with the shears in her lap. We buried her at the bottom of her row, by the water, with her mouth free, as we did then. And the next spring her row came up out of tune for the first time in forty years, and nobody would touch it, and it stayed out of tune until the Cantors who came after her could bear to.","I was at the vats the morning the reeds went. I did not see it. By the time I got to the wall it was over, and the valley was full of something glittering, like frost blown off a roof, and the next gust took it away. People say there was a sound. I listened very hard, standing where I used to hold her ladder, and there was none.","It was the first thing in the Reach I ever heard go without a sound. I am glad, at least, that it was not her."]},
{"id":"lib_b_letters_from_the_gate","title":"Letters from the Gatehouse","author":"Cyril Tolley, junior clerk to the Consortium of Bores, to his sister Prudence","genre":"letters","year":"418–419 SR","origin":"the Oakhaven works gatehouse","where":"p16h_depot3","how":"pile","at":"p16h_depot3","style":"office","summary":"A young clerk from the Organum, posted to keep the ledger at the Oakhaven works gate, writes home to his sister about the Reach, the scrip, and the soldier who talks in its sleep.","text":["Fair copies, kept, as a clerk should keep them.","Rootday the ninth of Seedmonth, 418. Dear Prue, I am here and alive and I have already disgraced myself. At the coaching inn at the edge of the Reach I asked the landlady, in an ordinary voice, whether there was any soup, and she put her hand flat over my mouth like a cork in a bottle and held it there until I stopped. Then she let go and said, very low, that there was. Nobody here speaks above a murmur. They measure their voices with the palm of the hand. Every room has a little lamp with a reed in it that whistles all night, so that the house is never quite silent, and the first night I lay awake listening to mine and thinking it was a kettle someone had forgotten. People greet you with \"Does it ring?\" and you are to say \"True enough.\" I said \"Yes, thank you,\" and was looked at.","My post is the works gate. I keep the ledger of every cart and wagon in and out, and pull the lever for anyone who holds up the green glass disc, and there are two soldiers of the Legion standing either side of me with their bells tilted to the sky. I miss the hour. Nobody plays it here. I did not know how much I leaned on it until I stood at a gate at what I am fairly sure was the sixth hour of the morning and had no idea whether it was.","Nullday the second of Lambmonth, 418. The soldier on the left of the gate talks in its sleep. They all murmur, you can hear it if you stand close, which you are told not to, but the one on the left says words. The same words, every morning, just before the day shift: second shelf, behind the flour. Second shelf, behind the flour. I am told this is nothing. There is a Catalogue circular pinned inside the gatehouse door which says that speech emitted by a damaged engram is to be disregarded and is not to be recorded. So I have not recorded it. I am telling you, which is different.","Dawnday the eleventh of Longsun, 418. You asked what the town is like. Prue, the fingernails grow. A quarter of an inch in a night. There is a knife beside every washbasin in Oakhaven and I have one by mine and I use it every morning before I shave. Little oaks come up through the cobbles overnight like teeth through a gum. When the wind gets up the big trees ring, actually ring, like spoons on a bowl, and everyone in the street stops and looks toward the Hedge. They took all the bells in the first year of the bore, for the bore's teeth, and nobody here misses them, which is the strangest thing I have heard yet. At dusk the lamplighters go round, and old people take stools out to the crossings and hum till dawn. There is an old man at the Weavers' Cross who hums the same song every night and misses the same high note every time, and I have started walking home that way to hear him miss it. There is said to be a Synod man living alone outside the Hedge whom the hounds will not touch. Nobody will tell me his name, because I am from the Organum. I find I do not mind as much as I should.","Massday the third of Dustmonth, 418. Father writes that engrams are harmonic templates and possess no identity, and that I should not let rustic talk work on me. He is quite right. A template can hold a pattern of words the way a mould holds the shape of a loaf. The mould does not know it is a loaf. I have written that out twice to be sure I believe it. All the same I have begun to say good morning to the soldier on the left, because it seems rude not to, when one has heard about the flour every day for four months.","Memoryday the twenty-fifth of Firstfruit, 418. They have a rule here for children: never answer a voice you cannot see. Things outside the Hedge call in the voices of people they have heard. Last night at the Listening Hour I was walking the lane past the thorn and something on the other side said my name, quite kindly, in my landlady's voice. My landlady was in her kitchen. I could see her through the window, cutting bread. I did not answer. I walked on at the same pace, as they all do, and I was sick in the yard when I got in, and she held my head and did not ask. This morning I asked a woman at the well whether a voice inside a bell counts as a voice you cannot see. She looked at me for a long moment and then walked away without her water.","Nullday the twentieth of Stillmonth, 419. A lean winter, and the scrip will not change to sovereigns at anything like its face, so I am sending you less than I promised. I did a foolish thing. The Factor walks the line of the Legion every morning with her lenses, and listens to each soldier as if she were reading it, and this morning I asked her whether the one on the left of the gate might be posted somewhere else. I said it unsettled the carters. She looked at me through the coloured glass for a long time and said my colour was a little anxious, and that she would note it. Then she listened to the soldier on the left for longer than to any of the others. Prue, it occurs to me that if she can hear back everything it has heard, then she has heard me say good morning to it every day since Dustmonth. I do not know why that should frighten me. It is only good manners.","Rootday the seventh of Thawmonth, 419. I have stopped saying good morning. I am sorry to say I find I have begun to answer it instead, under my breath, the way you would answer anybody who told you the same thing every day. Behind the flour, yes. I know. I will look. I do not know what I would look for. I do not know whose flour. The circular says not to record it, and I have not, and I have been keeping the gate ledger very neatly.","Nullday the twelfth of Sheafmonth, 419. I have applied for a transfer home. Please do not tell Father until it is granted, and if it is not granted, please do not tell him at all. I am quite well. The harvest is coming in, and they have a festival here when the last field is cut, and I am told the clerk is not invited, which is fair. I only wanted to write down one more thing, and then I will not write about the gate again.","This morning, just before the day shift, the soldier on the left did not say anything about the flour. It said, quite clearly, in a voice like a little girl's, mind the step, Gran. There is no step at the gate. I minded it anyway.","Your loving brother, C."]},
{"id":"lib_b_true_and_modest","title":"A True and Modest Account of the Life of Captain Teodor Brisk, Free Captain of the Air","author":"Captain Teodor Brisk, written by himself, with a note by his bosun, Walter Gurney","genre":"autobiography","year":"417 SR","origin":"Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile","where":"p16k_kestrel_below","how":"shop","at":"vane","price":3,"style":"paper","summary":"A Rust Coast captain who left the sea for the sky tells his own legend at full sail, and his bosun is given the last page to correct it.","text":["I was born on the Rust Coast in a storm so famous that three separate taverns on the Long Mole claim it, and I have drunk free in all three on the strength of it. My mother said it was a squall. My mother was not a reliable witness; she was busy.","I signed the Articles at fourteen, like every man on this coast, and I could read them, which was rarer. I read them all the way through before I signed, and the old captain who held the pen laughed so hard he had to sit down. I tell young men this so they will know it can be done.","By twenty I had run the Roaring Strait eleven times by night with no lamp, steering by the pitch of the tide. Any child on the Coast can tell you what the sea is singing, but it takes a captain to hear when she is lying. I have been round Cape Dissonance in a gale with the coral howling under the keel like a choir that has been told bad news. I have wrestled an eel forty feet long in the shallows off the Cay with nothing but a boathook and my natural charm, and I have the scar. I have outrun a Synod frigate across open water in a coaster so slow that the gulls used to land on her to rest. I have dined with the Harbour-Queen herself, at her own table, and she laughed at my stories, and she does not laugh at many.","I have never once been afraid. I mention it only because people ask.","In the year after the Fracture the sea became unreliable in its manners. You will know what I mean if you are from the Coast, and if you are not, I will tell you plainly. The tides began to sing in keys nobody had charted. Water forgot what it was, here and there, and went soft and blue and thick as jelly, and a good hull would settle into it at her moorings like a spoon into custard. Holes opened in the sea. Not storms. Holes. I watched a coaster go down into one off the Cape with all her sails set, and the water closed over her afterward without a ripple, and there was no sound from it at all.","I am not a fool. I sold my hull while she was still a hull, and I bought a ship that does not touch the water.","She is the Fair Excuse. Ninety feet of patched grey envelope, a gondola built by a man who drank, and four lift-tanks under the keel that burn blue at night and hum like a congregation that has forgotten the words. On a still night you can hear her coming two miles off. She sounds like a barn being dragged across the sky by somebody who loves it. I have flown her from the Coast to the Reach and back more times than I have counted, and I have carried anything you care to name. Black glass. Rum. Salt mutton. Cheese, which is worse than glass for the smell. A bishop, once, who was sick on my boots and blessed them after.","I have never carried the bottles. I want that printed, and printed large. The Coast sells the last words of the dead in stoppered blue glass to anyone who is grieving and has money, and when the grieving pull the stopper the hounds come to the sound. I will not have them aboard. I have no wish to meet a hound at four hundred feet with nowhere to step back to. If you want to hear your dead husband say goodbye, get somebody else to fly it to you, and stand well away when you open it.","I hold to the Articles. All of them, but the First above the rest: no captain shall leave a hand in the water while the ship can bear the weight. The Harbour-Queen amended it in her own hand three years ago, as every captain knows, with the words or until she cannot. I am told that amendment was needed. I have never had cause to use it, and I never shall.","That is my life so far, and I commend it to the young as an example of what courage and modesty can do with very little money. I have been asked to let my bosun write a page at the end, for balance. I said he could have a short one.","A note by Walter Gurney, bosun of the Fair Excuse.","The eel was a conger and about four feet long and it bit him, and that is the scar. The frigate was a customs cutter and it caught us, and he talked us out of it, which is the cleverer thing and he will not tell it. The Harbour-Queen has asked the printer to set down that she has never dined with Captain Brisk, has never laughed at him, and is owed eleven sovereigns by him, payable at the Mole office.","He says he has never been afraid. He sleeps with a lamp lit and the hatch open every night of his life, so that he can hear the ship breathing. Make of that what you like.","But the part about the Article is true, and it is the only part I would have you remember. There are nineteen of us aboard a ship built to carry nine. We have a cook who cannot hear and a rigger with one hand and a lookout who cannot see past his own nose and listens instead. We have two old women who were hands on his first coaster and are no use to anybody, and a boy who was crying on the Mole one morning and has never been asked where he came from. Every captain on this coast has told him to put us ashore. He has never put one of us ashore. He says the ship can bear the weight.","That is why she flies so low."]},
{"id":"lib_b_year_of_nights","title":"A Year of Nights","author":"Godwin Pettigrew, lamplighter of Oakhaven","genre":"journal","year":"418–419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"watchhouse","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"lives","style":"parch","summary":"An Oakhaven lamplighter keeps a year's notes on his round of whistling lamps, the people who sit up with them, and one night-hummer in particular.","text":["Sheaf on the gate, 418. My mother kept a book of the weather and my father kept a book of debts, and I have neither weather nor money worth the writing, so I shall keep a book of nights. I light two hundred and twelve hush-lamps, from the tannery steps to the top of Cooper's Row, with a pole, a taper and a bad knee. People think a lamplighter's trade is fire. It is not. Any fool can light a wick. A lamplighter's trade is listening. When the flame takes, the reed in the chimney catches the draught and begins to whistle, and every reed has its own note, and I know all two hundred and twelve the way a shepherd knows his ewes. If one is wrong I hear it from the end of the lane.","Last lamp of the round is the one at the corner by the well, which I call Well Corner because nobody else has bothered to call it anything. Tibby Lockwood sits up there. She is a night-hummer, and younger than most of them, being only my age, and she hums lower than anyone in Oakhaven. You have to stand quite close to be sure she is humming at all. I stand quite close. It is my job.","First frost. Oil is four pence the pint and the Consortium says it will be five by the thaw. I said this to Tibby, who said nothing, because she was humming, which is the trouble with courting a night-hummer. I did not say I was courting. I am writing it here so it is written somewhere.","Frost again, harder. The lamp at the end house by the dye-works went wrong in the night. The cold split the reed, and it stopped whistling, and I was at the far end of the Hollow and did not hear it go. In the morning the door was on the latch. Her kettle was cold on the hob. Her shawl was on the back of her chair, folded the way she folded it, and there was nobody in either. Her name was Abigail Starling. She was seventy, and deaf in one ear, and she liked her lamp turned low so it would not keep her up. I have written her name here so that it is written somewhere too. I carry six spare reeds in my coat now, wrapped in flannel against the frost, and I walk the round twice.","Rime on the Hedge. Nails. I cut mine every morning by the basin with the little knife, the same as everyone, and this morning I cut them twice, because I lit the round late and lay in. Tibby says a man can tell how long he has slept by his fingers now, like a tree. That is the most she has said to me in a month. I am counting it.","Catkins on the hazel. Walking home past the thorn at the dead hour I heard my mother call my name from the other side of it. Godwin, your supper is going cold. She has been dead four winters. She used to say it at the door every night of my boyhood, in exactly that voice, a little cross, a little fond. I made the sign, two fingers to the lips and to the ear, and kept walking at the same pace, which is the hardest thing I have ever done with my legs. When I got to Well Corner I must have looked a sight, because Tibby stopped humming and took my hand and held it, and then began again, and did not let go until the hummers changed.","Seed in. At the change of the hummers the old ones murmur all's well, all's quiet, keep to the Narrow, as they hand over the stools, and I have taken to saying it with them under my breath, though I am not one. Old Tam at the Weavers' Cross hums The Miller's Daughter every night of his life and every night he goes for the high note at the end of the verse and misses it. I said to Tibby that one of these nights he would get it. She said she hoped not. She said if Tam ever got that note it would be because something was out there listening hard enough to make him try. I have not been able to get that out of my head.","Reeds hung. The whole street was noisy all day with the tuning of the new First Reeds over the doors, and the Chapel sent a deacon round to say it was a very great deal of second notes for one street, and everyone said yes, deacon, and went on tuning. I looked at the reed over Tibby's door when I passed. Her mother still cuts the new one every year and tunes it against Tibby's breastbone at the kitchen table, as if she might have changed. She never has. It is a good low reed. I would know it anywhere now.","Hay in. I have done something I shall probably have to confess. I cut a new reed for the hush-lamp at Well Corner. I tuned it at home at the kitchen table for nine nights, against the lamp across the lane, which is my own lamp, the one on my own house. I tuned it a third above. The Church teaches that holiness is one note held without end, and that the second note is sin, and I was raised to say it and I said it. But I have listened to two hundred and twelve reeds a night for six years, and I tell you every one of them is lonely.","Eels down the Flume. The Eel-Run, late this year, because everything is late. The lamplighters set out the lanterns all down the bank for the fishing, and then the whole town stood along the water in the dark with traps and lines and nobody spoke at all, because the eels can hear. It is the only festival in the Reach you keep with your mouth shut. Tibby fished beside me for three hours without a word, which I found I liked very much. At the end she put an eel in my bucket, a fat one, and went home. In the Reach that is very nearly a proposal. I am almost sure.","Corn standing. They say at the well that the Church has views about the Last Sheaf this year. Let them have views. The north field will still be standing when they are gone.","Tonight I put the new reed in the lamp at Well Corner. I lit it last, as always, and the reed caught and whistled, and across the lane my own lamp answered it a third below, and the corner by the well made a chord. Very small. No louder than two people breathing in the same room. Tibby came up the lane with her stool and her shawl and stopped dead and listened to it for a long time, with her face doing nothing at all. Then she sat down, and settled her shawl, and began to hum.","She hummed the low note. Under both of them."]},
{"id":"lib_b_reeds_miner","title":"The Reeds-Miner: A Short Life of Tomas, Timber-Cutter, Against His Wishes","author":"Gwen Fitch, his sister's daughter, for the Timber Compact's harvest supper","genre":"biography","year":"419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"clearing","how":"pile","at":"clearing","style":"paper","summary":"A niece writes, for the timber-cutters' supper, the life of the uncle who told her not to: how a man who dug reeds for the Cantors came to fell ringing oaks for the Synod.","text":["My uncle Tomas does not want this written. He said so, at length, across his own kitchen table, with the glove off so that I would have to look at the hand while he said it. I am writing it anyway, because the Compact asked me to, and because I am the only one in the family he cannot frighten, having been sick on him as a baby.","You will all have wondered why he is called the Reeds-Miner, when he has cut timber the whole of your acquaintance. Here is the answer. Before the Fracture, when a wind-reed went sour in the orchards, the Cantors could not simply pull it out. The crown sat a yard down, a knot of root the size of a man's head, and if you left any of it the new reed came up out of tune. So there was a trade for lifting them, with a long narrow spade and a lantern, and the men who did it were called reeds-miners, because they went down a hole in the ground and came up dirty. My grandfather did it. Tomas went down his first hole at eight, because he was the only one small enough. He could tell a sour crown by the smell before he touched it. The Cantors trusted him. He says that was the best thing anyone ever paid him in.","On the day the Spire broke, all the reeds in the Reach went to sugar and blew away in an hour, and his trade went with them. He was thirty-three and had a wife and a spade. Within the month he had a timber licence from the Synod and a grey mare called Dumpling, who is still with us, and still bites.","He was a good timber-cutter and a very loud one. In those days a wagon creaked. Everything creaked, and you could hear him coming down the Greenroot road from a mile off, singing to the mare, badly, in a voice that the Compact men tell me could split a log by itself.","Then the Consortium put the bore into the quarry, and the oaks began to ring like bronze when the wind moved them, and the wagons stopped creaking and began to hum, and the hounds learned that a ringing tree is a dinner bell. Tomas stopped singing. He learned to fell a bough with wool wound on the blade, and to wait for a gust to cover a stroke, and to call the work off when a hound lifts its head, with two notes, falling, which my aunt says is the only tune he has ever got right.","Last year the saw took three fingers off his hand. The fourth grew back, as things grow back here now, and it grew back wrong, and that is all I will say about the glove, because he will read this. He went back to the clearing nine days later. He says he has one good hand and a bad temper and that people may choose which they speak to. Most choose the hand.","He works for the Synod's quota, as some of you do. If he is a load short on quota day they take the wagon and Dumpling, and then he has nothing. So he fells what he is told to, a stone's throw from where the hounds sleep, and carts it up to the works, where it goes to shore the headframe over the shaft. He told me this himself. He said, I know what it holds up. He said he has been thinking it since the summer. I did not ask him what he thinks, because I could see.","At the end of the evening I asked him what he would like written, since it was going to be written whether he liked it or not.","He was quiet for a long time. Then he put the glove back on and said, write that the wagons used to creak."]},
{"id":"lib_b_shepherds_ear","title":"A Shepherd's Ear: Letters from the Conservatory of Bores","author":"Owen Hatherly, scholar of the Conservatory, to his mother at Wethercombe","genre":"letters","year":"414–416 SR","origin":"the Organum","where":"p22_bellwether","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"lives","style":"paper","summary":"A shepherd's son from the Downs wins a place at the Synod's great school for drill-engineers and writes home about forks, ewes and the city that plays the hour.","text":["Nullday the fourth of Sheafmonth, 414. Dear Mam, I am here and I have not been robbed, though a man in the coach-yard tried to sell me a map of the city for two pence and the city is mostly straight lines, so I suppose I was nearly robbed. You will want to know what it is like. It is like standing inside an organ, if the organ were a mile wide and somebody had laid the streets between the pipes. The towers are grey and very tall and have mouths cut near the top, and the wind blows across them all day so that the whole city breathes one long low note you feel in your teeth. Then on the hour the bellows under the Choir-Hall push, and every pipe sounds at once, and the hour comes down on you like weather. The first time, I sat down in the road. Nobody laughed. A woman with a basket said I would stop hearing it in a week, and walked round me.","I lodge over a cordwainer's in a room with a window and a bed and a smell of leather. There are forty of us in the first year. I am the only one from the Reach. There is a book on every desk with a name on the spine from our end of the world, and you know whose, and I shall not write it, and you will not say it at the Bellwether, and that will be best for both of us.","Stasisday the twentieth of Ashfall, 414. Our first lesson was one line, and we spent a week on it. A struck fork held near a second fork a little out of true will beat. Loud, soft, loud, soft. Mam, I knew that before I came. It is the two bells on Father's old wethers on the far side of Hob's Knap, one a hair flatter than the other, so that you could hear them pulsing together across the whole down on a still evening, and tell from the beat which way the flock was moving. I said so in class. Everyone laughed except Master Hollister, who said that I had a shepherd's ear, and that a shepherd's ear was worth more in this building than the Synod's money, and then gave me twice the work.","Memoryday the first of Frostmonth, 414. The woman with the basket was right. On the eighth day I stopped hearing the hour. I know it still sounds, because I see the others look up at it, and I can feel it in the floor of my room if I put my hand there. But my ears have let it go. Brann, who sits next to me and is from the Peaks and says almost nothing, says that is how you know you have become a citizen. How many lambs did we lose in the cold snap? Tell me the true number, not the one you think I can bear.","Rootday the ninth of Seedmonth, 415. I have been spoken to. Brann and I were checking a pair of forks in the practice room, and to save time I hummed the one and he hummed a fifth above it, the way the old Cantors used to, so we could hear if the forks sat true between our notes. Master Hollister came in and stood there until we stopped. He was not angry. He said that we must not do that, that it was a folk habit, and that in this building it was very nearly devotional, in the wrong direction. Then he checked the forks himself, with a little brass meter, and they were true, and he said nothing more about it. Brann has not hummed since. I find I hum all the time now, under my breath, in my room, so the room is not so quiet. It is the Root note. I do not think I could stop if I tried.","Massday the third of Dustmonth, 415. Second year. Today we began the anatomy of the voice. There was a sheep's larynx on a tray at the front of the room, and the lecturer turned it with a probe to show us the folds and where they lie, and how they are stretched to change the note. Mam, it was a Downs ewe. I would know that gristle anywhere, I have helped Father with enough of them. I did not say so this time. I have learned that much. Nobody has told us why men who drill rock must know exactly how a voice is made, and where a wire would sit along it. When Brann asked, the lecturer said we would understand in our fourth year. Brann wrote it in his book. I wrote it in mine.","Memoryday the twenty-seventh of Sheafmonth, 415. We drilled our first sample in the works yard, a block of lively stone from somewhere east, set in an iron cradle with a little bore-head no bigger than a churn. We were to hold the bit beneath the failure-pitch, as the book on every desk says we must, always beneath. I had the meter. And as the bit went in, the stone's pitch climbed away from it, a little, the way a ewe shifts away from the shears when she feels the first cold of the blade. Master Hollister said samples are lively and that we would learn to correct for it. Tarquin, who is from the Organum and very clever, asked whether stone could mind. Everyone laughed. He was not in the hall at the start of term. They said his family had sent for him.","Nullday the eighteenth of Lambmonth, 416. Dear Mam, I am coming home.","I have not failed. Master Hollister says I am the best ear in the year and he wrote it down and I will bring you the paper so you can show it at the Bellwether, which I know you will. I have not been sent away. I have done something worse, which is to leave a scholarship, and Father would have had a great deal to say about it, and I will listen to all of it from you instead.","I will tell you the reason, because you will ask, and because I have only just found it out myself. This morning I walked across Registry Square at the hour, and the whole city sounded, every pipe, and I did not hear it. Not at all. A year and a half I have not heard it. And I thought: they told me I would stop hearing it, and they were pleased when I did, and so was I. And then I thought about the stone in the yard, shifting away from the bit, and the ewe on the tray, and Tarquin, and I understood that I am being taught, very carefully and very well, to stop hearing things.","So I am coming home by the Reach road and I will be at the stile before Reed-Hanging. Please do not take my reed down. I should like to hear it."]},
{"id":"lib_b_between_the_falls","title":"Between the Falls","author":"Marthe Unhaus, of Tier Eleven, for the Bellows","genre":"memoir","year":"418 SR","origin":"Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile, from a copy carried out of Cinderhold","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"lives","style":"paper","summary":"A woman from the lowest, hottest tier of Cinderhold tells how four hundred workers with no House learned to organise in the gaps between the hammer-blows.","text":["Set on the Row from a copy carried out of the Caldera in the lining of a boot. The Bellows do not sign their papers. We print the name she gave us, which is not a name, and you will see why.","Still standing.","That is how we say good morning on Tier Eleven, and it is not a joke, though you may laugh. I was born at the bottom of Cinderhold, where it is hot enough that a heel of bread goes to rusk in your pocket between the gate and the furnace. Tier One has clean air and a House. Every tier below it has a House, all the way down, until you come to us, and we have none. So we are written down in the indenture books as Unhaus, of no house, and some of us have taken it for a surname out of spite. I am one. You are born owing on Tier Eleven, for your mother's bed and the water she drank while she carried you, and you work it off, and you die owing more.","Above everything is the Beat. If you have never heard the Great Beat I cannot give it to you, any more than I could give you the heat. It is a hammer the size of a chapel, and it falls in fours, and it has fallen in fours for two hundred years except on two days that we count our history from. You do not hear it after a while. You live in it. A child on Tier Eleven learns to talk in the gaps, between one fall and the next, three counts of speech and then shut your mouth, because on the fall nobody hears anything, not even the person shouting. Outsiders talk across the fall and you see their lips going and you feel sorry for them. I have been told that when we go abroad we stop in the middle of sentences for the rest of our lives. I have never been abroad. I expect it is true.","My father kept his hand on it. That is the best thing you can say of a man in Cinderhold. When an oath is sworn among us it is sworn bare-handed on an ingot just out of the mould, and the one swearing holds his palm there while the oath is said, and a man who takes it off before the end is cold, and there is no worse word. My father took the Bellows' oath with his hand on the ingot for the whole of it. He had the mark till he died. He died cold anyway, not that way, the other way. The Cold Burn went through our furnace the winter I was twelve and the heat went out of him with it. He walked about for a year with his eyes open and no warmth in his hands, one of the ember-blind, and I used to hold them to warm them, and it was like holding two spoons.","We are called the Bellows because a bellows is the only part of a forge that breathes, and nobody has ever thanked one.","There are four hundred of us. We have no leverage, as the Houses are fond of telling us, and no money, and no hall. What we have is the Beat. A meeting of the Bellows is held in the open, in the noise, because no overseer can hear a thing in it. Questions are put between the falls. We vote by tapping the pipe-rail, once for aye and nothing for nay, in the gap after the fall, and a man down the line counts, and four hundred people can decide a thing in the time it takes the hammer to fall eighty times. I have stood in a vote like that with my brother Hanno on one side and a girl I had never met on the other, and felt the rail ring under my palm with four hundred taps, and I will tell you it is the nearest I have come to hearing a choir.","In the spring of four hundred and fifteen we sat down. Three days, every hand on the tier, sitting on the walkways with our arms folded. The Houses brought in slag-folk from the Scraps, who have even less than we do and could not be blamed, and paid them in food, and the furnaces never once went out. On the fourth day we stood up and went back owing more. Hanno says that is how we learned the first law of Cinderhold, which is that nothing done here is louder than the Beat. He says it as if it were the end of something. I think it was the beginning.","I will put down one more thing, because the Row asked for it. Men who come up from the deep galleries say that the glass down there talks. I do not mean it rings. I mean talks. They do not say this above Tier Nine. Below it they say it all the time, very quietly, between the falls, and they say it the way you would mention that a neighbour has been crying in the night, and that someone should go round.","Upstairs the Houses have started calling something the Gasket Strike. They have given it a name before we have, which is how we know they are frightened, because you only name the thing you are expecting.","We have learned to say everything that matters in the space between two blows of a hammer. It has always been room enough. One day the hammer will have to wait for us.","Still standing."]},
{"id":"lib_c_fireside_tales","title":"Fireside Tales of the Reach, Lately Told","author":"Margery Fallow, who keeps the little school on Cooper's Row","genre":"folk tale","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"tavern","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"tales","style":"parch","summary":"Three new tales told as if they were old, written down by a schoolmistress who noticed how quickly the children changed them.","text":["I keep the little school on Cooper's Row. When the slates are put away and the hush-lamp is lit, we tell tales. I have written three of them down, because I noticed something about them and I want somebody after me to notice it too.","In every one of my grandmother's tales, the clever girl sang. She sang the wolves to sleep. She sang so loud that the miller heard her at Greenroot. The children will not have those tales now. If I tell one, a hand goes up: But Mistress, the hounds would come. So the tales have changed, and they have changed in about three years, and the children tell them back to me as if they were three hundred years old. Here they are as the children like them. I have only tidied the grammar.","CLEVER DORCAS AND THE JAR","There was a girl called Dorcas who lived on the far side of the water-meadow, back when there were people on the far side. Her mother sent her to her gran's for a jar of honey and told her to take the baby, because the baby would not settle for anyone else.","So Dorcas went over the meadow with the baby on her hip, and she came back with the honey in the good brown jar with the blue ring round its neck, which had been her gran's mother's. She was halfway home when the baby woke, saw the dark, and opened his mouth.","You know what a baby can do. He cried properly, a cry like a gate dragged over cobbles, and far off at the edge of the trees something heard it and turned. Dorcas saw the air go shivery there, the way it shakes over a summer road.","She could have clapped her hand over his mouth. But her gran had told her what becomes of babies who are made to go quiet in the dark, and Dorcas was not going to carry home a quiet baby. So she did a clever thing. She ate the honey. She ate all of it with two fingers, and never tasted a drop. Then she held the empty jar to the baby's mouth and let him cry into it, the whole cry, the long one. When he stopped to breathe, she pushed the stopper in.","She threw the jar as far as she could towards the Flume. It struck the stones by the bank and broke, and out of it came the baby's cry, every bit as loud as he had made it. The hounds went to the jar. Of course they did. They go where the loud thing is.","Then the baby, having cried himself empty, went still on her hip, and stiller, and she felt him getting cool. So she put her lips to the top of his head, where the bones are soft, and hummed into him: not loud, not quiet, the width of a hand. She walked home through the meadow humming, step for step, and the hounds were busy with the jar the whole way.","When she got in, her mother said, Where is the honey? Dorcas said, In me. Her mother said, Where is the jar? Dorcas said, Over by the Flume, with the hounds. Her mother sat down on the settle and laughed without making a sound, which Reach mothers can do, and then she cried the same way. Then she sent Dorcas to bed with no supper, which was fair, because Dorcas had had the honey.","And that is why, in the Reach, nobody will lend you a good jar. (The children insist on this ending. One of them tried to borrow mine afterwards.)","EBBO STINT, WHO ANSWERED","Ebbo Stint was the meanest man in Oakhaven, and in Oakhaven that is a contest. He had a wife called Tilda who did the kindness for both of them, and she died of it, the children say, though they say that about everybody. She was buried the proper way, with her jaw bound and a stone on her tongue. Then Ebbo went home to count his money, which she had always hidden for him, because he could never decide where. It was not where she had said. He turned the house inside out.","One night at the Hedge he heard her. Ebbo? Ebbo, love, are you warm enough?","Everyone knows you do not answer. Ebbo knew it better than anyone, because answering costs something, and he never paid for anything he could get free. So he walked past. For a month he walked past. She asked whether he was eating. She asked about the cat. She asked whether he had found the money yet, and he did not even slow down, though it nearly killed him.","Then one night she said, very sweetly: I put the thirty sovereigns under the hearthstone, love. You'll find them there.","Ebbo stopped dead in the lane. He turned to the thorn, and for the first time in his life he raised his voice. It was forty! There were forty!","In the morning there was his stick, leaning against the Hedge, as neat as if he had stood it there himself.","They lifted his hearthstone, of course. There was nothing under it. They took up the floor and the chimney, and to this day nobody has found Ebbo Stint's forty sovereigns. But if you walk along the Hedge behind the tannery of a night, and you are mean, and you are listening, you may hear a woman's voice say, very gently: Forty, love. It was forty.","The moral, the children tell me, is not to answer. I think the moral is that it did not know the number until he told it. That is the whole of the Hedge in one sentence, and I would rather they learned it from Ebbo than from anyone they love.","WHY THE OAKS RING","When the Synod's men came for the bells, the year the quarry opened, they took the six from the Chapel and the little wind-bells out of the reed-rows. They put them in a wagon under sacking and drove them up the quarry road to be made into teeth for the bore. Nobody said anything. By then everybody knew what bells bring.","But the oaks watched too. The oaks of Oakhaven are old, and they had heard those bells every morning for three hundred years, the way you hear your mother come down the stair. When the wagon went by under the branches, the oaks reached down, only a little, the way a sleeper reaches for the blanket, and each of them took a ring of sound out of the wagon and kept it. Not the bronze. The Synod could have the bronze. Just the ringing.","That is why the oaks of Oakhaven chime like a spoon on a bowl when the wind gets up. They are holding the bells for us. They keep them very quiet, because they know about the hounds too, and they let out only a little at a time, so that we will not forget what bells sounded like. And one day, the children say, when it is safe, the oaks will give the bells back all at once, and the whole town will ring the way it did on a feast morning, and nobody will be afraid of it.","A Consortium man told me last winter, quite kindly, that the oaks ring because the wood has gone soft and dense since the bore began drawing on the Root, that it is a known effect, and that it is not a good sign. I expect he is right. He is paid to be. I have kept the children's version. I notice that he did not say what will happen on the day the oaks stop."]},
{"id":"lib_c_tuppence_narrow","title":"Tuppence Keeps the Narrow","author":"Elspeth Garland (words and pictures)","genre":"children's","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven, Cooper's Row; copied out by hand","where":"cooper_house","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"tales","style":"parch","summary":"A picture-book for small children about a lamb who learns, the hard way, to keep her voice the width of a hand.","text":["For the children of Cooper's Row, who have not seen a lamb this year.","Tuppence was a little lamb\nwith a very big voice.\nBAA! said Tuppence in the morning.\nBAA! said Tuppence at noon.\nBAA! said Tuppence to the moon.","Hush now, said her mother, the old grey ewe.\nToo loud brings the hounds, little one,\nand too still brings the Hush.\nWe keep to the Narrow, you and I:\na hand's width of voice. No more. No less.","But Tuppence liked loud.","One evening, at the edge of the fold, she gave the biggest BAA in the world, just to see how far it would go.\nIt went over the wall.\nIt went over the hill.\nIt went all the way to the dark trees.\nAnd in the dark trees, something that had been asleep lifted its head.","Tuppence saw the air at the edge of the wood go shivery, like the air over a hot stone.\nShe ran all the way back and pushed her nose into the old grey wool, and her mother stood very still and hummed, low and steady, until the shivery air went away.","All right, thought Tuppence. If loud is bad, I shall be quiet. I shall be the quietest lamb that ever was.","She did not baa in the morning.\nShe did not baa at noon.\nShe did not baa at the moon.\nShe lay down in the long grass and kept so still that the beetles walked over her hooves.","It was very quiet in the long grass.\nAfter a while, Tuppence felt cold, though the sun was out.\nAfter a longer while, she could not quite remember what her mother called her.\nHer wool had gone pale, like the mist on the Flume.","Her mother came looking. Tuppence? Tuppence?\nBut a lamb who makes no sound is a hard lamb to find.","So the old grey ewe did a clever thing. She stopped calling, and she began to hum: the low, warm, middle hum, a hand's width wide. She hummed the way a hush-lamp whistles. She hummed the way the night-hummers hum at the crossings.","And Tuppence, in the long grass, heard it, and before she could think about it she hummed back.","Mmm, said Tuppence.\nNot loud. Not still. Just the width of a hand.","And all at once she was warm, and her wool was her own colour again, and she remembered her name. It was Tuppence, which is what you call a small thing that is worth keeping.","That night, in the fold, by the whistling lamp, Tuppence murmured:\nGoodnight, Mother.\nGoodnight, lamp.\nGoodnight, wall. Goodnight, hill.\nGoodnight, dark trees, far away.\nI am not talking to you.","And her mother laughed without making a sound, and the two of them kept the Narrow together all night long, a hand's width apart, until the morning came."]},
{"id":"lib_c_still_wheel","title":"The Still Wheel at Lathbury","author":"told by Kester, a miller's man of Stillwater Landing; written down by a raftsman waiting on the lock","genre":"ghost story","year":"418 SR","origin":"Stillwater Landing","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"tales","style":"paper","summary":"A miller's man tells what he did not find at a mill up the Flume, which is the only safe way to tell it.","text":["You'll want me to tell you what was in the mill. Everyone does. I'll tell it the way my gran told hers, which is the only safe way: I'll tell you what was not there. If you want the other kind of story, there's a pedlar on the wharf who sells them by the sheet, with pictures. He has never been to Lathbury.","Lathbury Mill stands on the leat off the Flume, two miles above the lower lock, where the grown-wood channel lets a little water down to turn a wheel for the Lathbury farms. The Pargeters had it: the miller and his wife, two girls, a boy and the old mother. In the spring after the Hedge went up round Oakhaven, Lathbury stopped sending flour down to the Landing. It didn't stop all at once. The sacks came lighter, then late, then not at all. My master, Saul Ainsley, who has the mill here, was owed for a season's sacks, and on a Hearthday he took me up the towpath to ask about it. That's all it was: a man going to ask about a debt.","The first thing that was not there was the noise. You can hear Lathbury wheel from the bend below. It has a knock in the third paddle that every boatman on this water knows. We came round the bend and the wheel was turning, and there was no knock. There was no sound from the leat at all. The water came down the race white and fast and went over the wheel, as quiet as milk being poured in another house.","Saul said, Hm. That was all.","The second thing that was not there was the dog. The Pargeters kept a black bitch who barked at every raft that passed. The kennel was there, and the chain, and the collar on the end of the chain, still buckled.","The third thing was the hush-lamp. There was one in the mill-house window, and it was lit, in daylight. I could see the flame, steady, with the reed set over it the way they are, and the reed was not whistling. A lit lamp with a dumb reed over it. I have thought since that I would give a great deal to have heard that reed.","We went in, because Saul was owed. The door wasn't locked. In the kitchen the table was laid for six, and somebody had cut the bread: six slices, and the knife lying by the loaf with crumbs on it. The bread had not gone hard. That is what I could not stop looking at. It had been cut, and it had not gone hard, and there was nobody.","In the mill the stones were turning, grinding nothing, with the hopper empty. There was flour on the floor as thick as a fall of snow, and in the flour there were footprints, small ones and big ones, six people's worth. They went from the kitchen door across to the far wall. Up to the wall. Not back.","There is no door in the far wall at Lathbury. I went and looked. I put my hand on it. It was a wall.","I said to Saul, We ought to go. He said nothing. I said it again, and then I understood that I had been talking for some time, about the knock in the paddle and the bread and the wall, the way you talk when you are frightened, and he had not answered me once.","He was standing by the stones with his cap in his hands. He was there; I want to be clear about that. I could see him. But he was standing the way a man stands at a graveside, and he had not said Hm in a long while, and when I said his name it seemed to take him a moment to know it was his.","I did the only thing I knew. I hummed. It's my gran's trick, the one every child in the Reach is given with their first spoon: a hand's width, not loud, not still. I took hold of his wrist, and it was as cold as a jug from the cellar. I hummed the first thing in my head, which was a hymn, because it was Hearthday. I walked him out of that mill backwards, the two of us, keeping our eyes on the wall, and I did not stop humming until we were past the lock.","Saul Ainsley is my master still. He paid off the Pargeters' debt himself, out of his own pocket, which nobody understood, and he has never gone up the towpath again. He is a fair man and a good miller, and the Landing likes him. His wife says he was always a quiet one.","He said good morning to me today, the same as every day. I couldn't tell you how it sounded. I've lain awake trying. I have known the man eleven years and I cannot remember his voice. Nor can she, if you ask her plainly.","That's all. That's what wasn't there. If you hear me humming at the lock of a night, now you know why, and you'll do me the kindness of humming back."]},
{"id":"lib_c_lamplighters_note","title":"The Lamplighter's Second Note","author":"by a Lady of Cooper's Row (anonymous)","genre":"romance","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven; a fair copy passed from house to house","where":"orla_house","how":"quest","at":"orla_house","quest":"ilse","style":"parch","summary":"A cooper's daughter waits all autumn for a stammering lamplighter to propose at a hand's width, and he finds another way to say it.","text":["In the Reach a proposal of marriage has always been made at a hand's width: mouth to ear, so softly that nobody else could swear to the words. The orchard people said it was so the reeds would not learn it and sing it about the valley. If a third person overheard and could repeat it, it did not count, and the poor man had to begin again. My grandmother said half the weddings in Reedside were delayed a year by a sister behind a door.","It was a pretty custom when the rest of the world was loud. It is a hard one now, when everybody in Oakhaven speaks at a hand's width from morning to night. A girl cannot tell, any more, which murmurs are meant.","Mercy Hatchard could not tell. She was the cooper's daughter on Cooper's Row, twenty-four, with her father's hands and her late mother's patience. Every evening at the Listening Hour she stood at her door to watch Crispin Whitlock light the lamps.","He lit them the way other men say their prayers, one at a time and without hurrying. He put the taper up on his pole and held it to the wick, and in the moment the flame caught, before the reed above it began to whistle, he looked down at her. Then the reed found its note, thin and high, and under the cover of that note he would say something. It was never much. Cold tonight. Your father's hammer was going late. There's frost coming; I can smell it. They had eleven seconds, from the catch to the full whistle, and in a year of evenings they had spent every one of them on the weather.","Crispin had a stammer. In the old days nobody minded it. In the Narrow it was a cruelty, because a word that sticks has to be pushed, and a pushed word comes out loud. So he said short things, and he said them under the reed, and Mercy, who could have listened to him talk about frost until she was eighty, began to fear that he would never say anything else.","Her father did not fear it. Gabe Hatchard had views. He had a man in mind: Mr Osgood, a clerk at the works, who had clean cuffs, a room near the Greenhall and wages in Consortium scrip, green and black, and who came to supper on Rootdays and praised the pickled walnuts in complete sentences.","In the week the eels went down the Flume, Mr Osgood asked her. He did it properly. He leaned in at a hand's width and gave her the whole proposal in one breath, without a stumble: his prospects, his regard, a cottage with a slate roof. It was perfectly done. It was like being read a receipt.","Mercy said she would give him her answer on Nullday. Then she went upstairs and sat on her bed and did not cry, because crying carries.","On the evening before Nullday, Crispin came down Cooper's Row with his pole as usual. He lit the Bramleys' lamp, and the widow Ferrier's, and then he came to the Hatchards' and put the taper to the wick. Mercy stood at the door with her arms folded, because she had decided that if he talked about the weather tonight she would marry Mr Osgood and have done with it.","The flame caught. Crispin looked down at her and said nothing at all.","Then the reed began to whistle, and it was not her reed. He had cut a new one and set it over the lamp in the afternoon while she was at the well. It found its note, thin and high like every lamp in Oakhaven, and then, a breath later, it found another one, a little above the first. Two notes. Sweet, and wrong, and leaning on each other the way two reed-rows used to answer across a valley before the Cascade.","Every child in the Reach knows the catechism. What is holiness? One note, held without end. What is sin? The second note.","Mercy stood very still. A lamp that sang two notes could be reported, and anyone on the Row who wanted to could hear it. But nobody on the Row could have told you what it meant. It said nothing to anybody but her. He could not say it at a hand's width, so he had put it in a reed, and a reed could be overheard all it liked and nobody could repeat a word of it. By the oldest rule in the Reach, it counted.","She did not trust her voice, and in the end she did not need it. She leaned in towards the lamp, close enough to feel the heat on her cheek, and hummed the second note back, softly, at a hand's width, so that only the man on the ladder could hear.","Crispin's taper shook so badly that he dropped it, and it went out in the gutter.","Mr Osgood had his answer on Nullday, in a short note that was kinder than he expected. Gabe Hatchard came out in the morning, looked at the lamp for a long time, and said, That'll want reporting. Then he went back in to his hammer, and he never reported it. He had courted Mercy's mother in the orchards, long ago, and some people say he did it with a reed.","If you walk down Cooper's Row at the Listening Hour, among all the lamps keeping their single notes, you will hear one that does not. Nobody has reported it yet. A good many people stop to listen.","Crispin still stammers. He has never needed to say it twice."]},
{"id":"lib_c_saffery_lightfoot","title":"Saffery Lightfoot and the Wind That Never Stops","author":"Captain Teodor Brisk, of the Fair Excuse","genre":"adventure","year":"418 SR","origin":"Port Cantabile, Broadsheet Row","where":"p16k_kestrel_below","how":"quest","at":"flume_lowrings","quest":"odile","style":"paper","summary":"A penny tale of the Rust Coast: a skyship captain, a Synod revenue-cutter with a brass ear, and the one wind on the Coast nobody flies into.","text":["A COMPLETE TALE IN ONE NUMBER. ONE PENNY. Printed on Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile. Not to be read aloud below decks.","They said no skyship on the Coast could outrun a revenue-cutter of the Synod. They said it mostly in taverns that Saffery Lightfoot had not yet drunk dry, and they said it to her face exactly once.","Reader, she was thirty-one years old and had been flying since she was nine. Her ship was the Patient Jenny, a lift-bellied coaster patched more often than a deacon's stockings, with two brass tanks that sang flat in cold weather and a captain who did not. Her bosun was Old Mattock, who had one eye and a great many opinions. Her lookout was the boy Fairweather, who could read a wind by licking his finger and could read nothing else.","On the night this tale begins, the Jenny was lying off the mast-tower with forty crates of moss-poultice in her hold. Every crate was untaxed, every crate was bound for Driftwood Cay, and every one was a crime. On the Cay the salvage crews work the blue jelly bare-handed, and their hands crack open in it, and the Consortium's poultice costs a sovereign a pot. Saffery had bought hers in the Reach for a tenth of that. She meant to sell it on the Cay for the same tenth. Reader, she was not a saint. She simply disliked the Consortium more than she liked money, which on this coast is very nearly the same thing.","She cast off at the dead hour with no lamps lit. Halfway across the harbour mouth, Fairweather licked his finger, held it up, and went white.","\"Cutter,\" he whispered. \"Off the Cay. Coming round.\"","There she was against the last of the moon: the Rectitude, revenue-cutter of the Synod, long and grey, with three tanks and a brass ear on her bow the size of a chapel door. Her listening-horn could hear a cough at a mile and a dropped spoon at two. Her commander was Grume, who had never in his life done anything that was not written down first, and who had sworn on the Synod's own paper to see Saffery Lightfoot dropped from the derrick on the Mole-head.","\"Nobody breathe,\" said Saffery, very softly.","Nobody breathed. The Jenny ran south on the land breeze with her tanks throttled down to a murmur, and for a quarter of an hour the brass ear swung left and right like a dog's nose and found nothing. Then Fairweather sneezed.","It was not much of a sneeze. It was the smallest sneeze ever sneezed on the Rust Coast. But the brass ear swung round and stopped, and the Rectitude's tanks opened up with a roar, and she came after them like a gull after a herring.","\"Well,\" said Old Mattock, \"that's torn it.\"","There is only one place a ship can go on this coast where a Synod cutter will not follow, and every captain knows it, and none of them goes there. South of Cape Dissonance there is a wind that has been blowing west since the year the world went flat, and in all that time it has never once paused for breath. The fishermen call it the Endless. In the Drift, a thing set moving does not stop. A gull that flies into the Endless is never seen again. Neither is a ship.","\"Mattock,\" said Saffery, \"get out the Widow's Washing.\"","The Widow's Washing was a storm-kite. It was forty feet across and stitched out of every sail Saffery's mother had ever owned, and it lived rolled in the bow with a thousand fathoms of tarred line on the big winch. Saffery had never once flown it. Her mother had flown it once, long ago, and come home white-haired at forty and never said where she had been.","They put it up over the Cape with the Rectitude a quarter-mile behind and closing. For a moment it hung in the ordinary air, a great patched flag of a dead woman's sails. Then it rose into the Endless, and the line came taut with a crack like the sky breaking its knuckles, and the Patient Jenny was snatched forward so hard that Fairweather sat down on the deck.","Reader, they went. They went faster than any ship on the Coast has ever been dragged. The sea went by underneath them in a blur, and the Cape fell behind them in a single breath. Behind them the Rectitude's brass ear filled with the roar of the Endless and heard nothing else at all. A cough at a mile, a spoon at two, but not a ship inside the loudest wind in the world.","Commander Grume consulted his regulations. His regulations said that no vessel of the Synod was to enter the Endless. He turned for home, and he wrote it down.","So the Jenny was free. And the Jenny was going west at a speed no ship can live at, behind a kite in a wind that would never stop, towards an edge of the world nobody has charted, and the big winch began to smoke.","\"Cut it,\" said Mattock.","Saffery looked up at her mother's sails, high and small and pulling. She thought of her mother at forty with her white hair, saying nothing. She took out her mother's knife.","\"Captain,\" said Fairweather, \"will it come down?\"","\"No,\" said Saffery. \"Not ever. Not in the Drift.\" And she cut the line herself.","The Jenny fell out of the Endless like a stone out of a sling and came down into ordinary air above an ordinary sea, slow and quiet and very surprised. They coasted to Driftwood Cay on what was left of the land breeze and sold forty crates of poultice for a tenth of a sovereign a pot. On the Cay, a man with a cracked hand shook Saffery's, and it was the best price she ever got.","Fishermen off the Cape say that on a clear evening you can still see it, very high and very small, going west, and going faster. In the Drift, nothing that is let go of ever stops. Saffery Lightfoot knew that when she cut it.","Reader, she cut it anyway."]},
{"id":"lib_c_under_a_hands_width","title":"Under a Hand's Width","author":"Edwy Marchbank, formerly reed-pruner of the Reedside orchards","genre":"poetry","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven; hand-sewn, one copy","where":"chapel","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"tales","style":"parch","summary":"Nine short poems by an old orchard pruner, from the last singing summer to a child born quiet and brought round.","text":["I pruned reeds in the orchards for thirty-one years. Now I sweep the Chapel. These are short, because everything is. Whoever finds this in the pew: it is yours. Read it under your breath.","I. PRUNING\nYou cut a reed to make it sing.\nToo long, it mumbles. Too short, it shrieks.\nMy master put the knife in my hand at nine\nand said, Listen first. Cut after.\nThirty-one years I listened first.\nI should like that written on me somewhere.","II. THE HOUR OF SUGAR\nThe reeds went clear as barley-sugar\nin the time it takes to say a grace,\nand then the wind came down off the hill\nthe way it always had, meaning no harm,\nand I watched thirty-one years of listening\nblow over Greenroot like a fine snow.","III. FLAT\nMy wife says the reds have gone.\nI say the reds are the same; it is our eyes.\nWe have had this argument for six autumns.\nNeither of us has once said the other thing:\nthat we would both give a great deal\nto be wrong.","IV. THE BELLS\nThey took the bells on a Massday.\nNobody rang them going.\nThat was the worst of it, I think:\nnot that they went, but that we let them\nleave the town as quiet as stolen spoons.","V. WOOL\nWe planted the Hedge with our own hands\nand packed it with our own fleece,\nand every cough in Oakhaven goes into it now\nand stays. I think of them some nights:\nall those coughs, all those dropped pans,\nall those children told to hush,\nkept in the wool like letters never sent.\nI hope it is a kind keeper.\nI know what it is keeping them from.","VI. NIGHT-HUMMER\nOld women on stools at the crossings,\nhumming nothing in particular\nso that the street will not go quiet.\nI used to think the Cantors held the Reach together.\nIt was these. It was always these.","VII. THE OAKS\nSince the bore, the oaks ring when the wind moves:\na spoon on a bowl, a bell in the next valley.\nThe children think it lovely.\nI pruned for sound all my life, and I know\nwhat a thing sounds like when it has been\npulled too tight.","VIII. INSTRUCTIONS FOR MY BURYING\nBind my jaw. Fine. Put the stone on my tongue.\nI have said what I meant to.\nBut leave my hands loose, Hannah,\nin case there is pruning.","IX. FOR A CHILD BORN QUIET, WHO WAS BROUGHT ROUND\nYou came out without a sound,\nand the whole room held its breath with you,\nand then the Cantor hummed into the top of your head\nand you creased your face up like a walnut\nand made a noise the width of a hand.\nNobody cheered. We have forgotten how.\nBut I want you to know, small one,\nthat an old man in the next house,\nwho used to tune the orchards,\nheard you through the wall,\nand stood up,\nand took off his cap."]},
{"id":"lib_c_bellwether_book","title":"The Bellwether Book: Songs Writ In Behind the Bar","author":"kept by Reuben Thrale, landlord, and the drovers who stopped; last entry by G. Thrale","genre":"song","year":"c. 380–417 SR","origin":"the Bellwether, Wethercombe","where":"p22_bellwether","how":"pile","at":"p22_bellwether","style":"parch","summary":"The drovers' inn's songbook, written in by many hands over forty years, until the night the house was shut.","text":["Rule of the house. Any drover who sings a song in this house that is not in this book shall write it in, or buy the room a jug. Spelling no object. R. Thrale.","THE DROVE-ROAD\n(Every man knows it. Written in so the boys get the verses in the right order.)\nOver the Long Down and down by the thorn,\neight hundred ewes and a tup with a horn,\nbell at his throat and the dogs at his heel,\ndrive 'em to Oakhaven, home for a meal.\nWalk on, walk on, it's only a mile\nto the Bellwether's fire at the foot of the stile.","(The second verse is ruder. You know it. A. L.)\n(He means he don't know it. R. T.)","FOLD-FIRE\n(For the back-end of the year, when the beacons are lit.)\nLong Down burning, north and high,\nHob's Knap answers from the eastern sky,\nEwe Barrow west on the old long mound:\ncome down, my flock, come down, come down.\nCome down to the light, come down to the fold,\nthe night is long and the hill is cold.","(Sung at the window while Absalom goes up and lights them. He cannot sing. He lights them anyway. R. T.)","SCOURING THE RAM\n(For the seventh summer. Sung on the scarp, one verse to a stake.)\nChalk him white and cut him clean,\nthe whitest Ram that ever was seen;\nscour him once in seven year\nor he'll walk off the hill and the flock with him, dear.","Scoured 395. Scoured 402. Scoured 409, rain all week, and the Farrant girl fell in the trench and came up white as the Ram. R. T.","THE SHEAF SONG\n(Oakhaven's way. Wethercombe cuts its own last stand to it. One cut to a beat, and never one man alone.)\nCut it low, and cut it slow,\nthe last of the year is let to go.","(That's all there is of it, and all there needs to be. You sing it round till the stand's down. R. T.)","THE MILLER'S DAUGHTER\nNot written. Both verses. If you need it written down, you are too young to hear it. R. T.","A NOTE, IN THE SAME HAND, LATER AND SMALLER\nSince the year the reds went, a song carried over the Downs brings what we do not name in this house. Sing them low now. Any man who sings full voice outside this door buys nobody a jug, because he will not be here to. R. T., 413.","THE LOW SONG\n(Made in this house the winter of 414, for the Narrow, by all of us, but mostly by Cicely from the fold.)\nLow, low, the width of a hand,\nlambs in the fold and the ewes on the land;\nnot loud for the hounds and not still for the other,\nlow, low, as you'd hum to your mother.\nLow, low, and the lamp on the sill,\nsomebody's humming on every hill.","(Best sung by three or four, so there's always one breathing in while another breathes out. That way it never stops. A. L.)","My nephew Gideon has the house from Reed-Hanging. My chest will not do the stairs, and the house wants a pair of legs. Keep the book, boy. Make them write in. R. T., 416.","416, high summer. Nobody went up to scour the Ram. Next year, we said. G. T.","417, high summer. Shut tonight. The wool has gone down to Oakhaven for the Hedge, and the flocks with it, and we are all to go in behind it: Tamar and all the Farrants, the fold, me. The road is empty. I sang the Drove-Road on the doorstep, low, the way Uncle said, to nobody.","I am leaving the book on the bar for whoever opens the door next. If it is me, I will know the songs. If it is not, learn the low one first.","G. Thrale."]},
{"id":"lib_c_last_sheaf_play","title":"The Last Sheaf: A Harvest Play in Three Scenes","author":"as played at the edge of the north field, Harvest Home 418; set down by Ursula Dimmock for next year's players","genre":"play","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"watchhouse","how":"shop","at":"moor_bookseller","price":1,"style":"paper","summary":"The old harvest play as Oakhaven now plays it: a reaper who cannot cut alone, a voice at the gate nobody may answer, and a sheaf that asks to be let end.","text":["A NOTE FOR PLAYERS. The play is older than anyone in Oakhaven. In my mother's day the third part was the Thief of Bread, and the whole field shouted him off. You cannot shout anybody off now, so since the Hedge the Thief has become the Voice at the Gate. He is not shouted off. He is not answered. It works better, I think. It is certainly more frightening.","It is played at the edge of the field by lamplight, at a hand's width, so the watchers must come close. Nobody, player or watcher, is to answer the Voice. Whoever answers brings the cider next year. The Voice may say anything it likes to the crowd except the true name of somebody dead. We are not cruel, only careful.","THE PLAYERS\nTHE SHEAF, a child in a straw coat, who stands in the last stand and says nothing until the end.\nOLD REAPER, with a wooden sickle.\nTHE BINDER, his wife, who has the sense.\nTHE VOICE AT THE GATE, never seen; played from behind a hung fleece by whoever has the best ear for other people.\nTHE VILLAGE, which is everybody else.","SCENE ONE. THE FIELD. Evening.\n(REAPER comes on with his sickle and finds THE SHEAF standing very still in the stand.)\nREAPER. There she is. Last of the year. I'll have her down before supper.\nBINDER. You'll not. Not on your own.\nREAPER. I've cut the whole field on my own.\nBINDER. And it grew back by morning, every stalk of it, and the bread tastes of less each time.\nREAPER. Bread's bread.\nBINDER. Bread was bread.\n(REAPER swings at THE SHEAF. THE SHEAF does not fall. He swings again. THE SHEAF folds its arms.)\nREAPER. She's a stubborn one.\nBINDER. The last stand is never cut by one. Everybody knows that.\nREAPER. Then fetch everybody.\nBINDER. (turning to the watchers) Everybody's here. Look at them, standing about with their hands in their shawls.\nREAPER. They'll want feeding first.\nBINDER. They always do.","SCENE TWO. THE GATE. Night.\n(The fleece is the Hedge. REAPER and BINDER sit with their backs to it, eating. THE SHEAF stands where it stood.)\nVOICE. (behind the fleece, in an old woman's voice) Son? Son, come out to the gate. I've brought your supper, and it's hot.\nREAPER. (half rising) Mam?\nBINDER. (her hand over his mouth) Your mam's been a year in the churchyard with a stone on her tongue. Sit down.\nVOICE. (in the BINDER's own voice, exactly) Husband, leave her. Come out. I'm out here. That's not me in there with you.\n(REAPER looks from the fleece to his wife, and back.)\nREAPER. (muffled) Which of you is it?\nBINDER. Which of us has her hand over your mouth?\n(REAPER thinks about this, and sits down.)\nVOICE. (to the watchers) You there, in the blue shawl. Your boy's out here. He says he's cold. Won't you say something to him? Only a word.\n(Here the watchers say nothing, and the Voice goes along the crowd, coaxing, for as long as the player can bear it. The Voice must never be answered. If it is, the play stops, the one who answered is told what they owe, and we begin the scene again.)\nVOICE. (at last, softly) Nobody? Nobody at all?\nBINDER. Not tonight, thank you.","SCENE THREE. THE CUT. Before dawn.\n(THE VILLAGE comes forward round THE SHEAF, all of it, as many as there are. Everybody who has a blade holds it up, and everybody who has none holds up a finger. The tune is hummed, low, sixteen beats, and on every beat every hand makes one cut in the air.)\nALL. (under the breath) Cut it low, and cut it slow, the last of the year is let to go.\n(On the sixteenth beat THE SHEAF kneels down. Then, for the first time, it speaks.)\nTHE SHEAF. Thank you. I was so tired of growing back.\nREAPER. Will you grow back tomorrow?\nTHE SHEAF. No. Not this one. This one you let end.\n(THE SHEAF stands up out of its straw coat, and is only a child again. BINDER plaits the coat into the sheaf-figure, and the whole field carries it to the Hedge-gate and hangs it there till spring.)\nVOICE. (behind the fleece)\n(The Voice says nothing more. It has nothing to say to people who are all together. That is the end of the play.)","AFTERWARDS. Nobody claps, because clapping carries. The players hum the tune once more through, and the child who was the Sheaf is given the first slice of the hearth bread and goes home asleep in somebody's arms. This year Hodge Lorkin answered the Voice in Scene Two, because it did his father very well. He has promised the cider for next year without being asked. U. D."]},
{"id":"lib_c_eight_keepers","title":"The Eight Keepers, and the Sparrow Who Asked","author":"Constance Farthing, who taught the small voices their letters","genre":"fable","year":"409 SR","origin":"Whisper-Grove, the Cloister road","where":"cloister","how":"gift","at":"lib_moot","quest":"shelver","style":"parch","summary":"Nine bedtime fables for a children's choir: eight animals who each keep one thing in the world, and a sparrow with no job at all.","text":["For the small voices of the charitable choir, Cloister road. These are told one a night before hymn practice, and on the ninth night the Sparrow. They are very old and I have not improved them, except to take out a bear, who frightened the youngest.","THE BADGER WHO KEPT THE ROOTS\nIn the beginning everything grew every which way: up and down, and sideways into next week. So the Badger went under the ground and told every root, one at a time, which way was down. He is telling them still. If you put your ear to the earth in spring you will hear a sort of humming. That is the Badger going round, saying down, and down, and down.\nMoral: somebody has to say it every time.","THE SALAMANDER WHO KEPT THE HEARTH\nFire did not used to know its own size. A candle might burn down a barn, and a bonfire might not warm a cup. So the Salamander sat in the middle of every flame and told it how big to be: the size of a hand for a hand, the size of a house only for a house.\nMoral: mind the size of things.","THE TORTOISE WHO KEPT THE WEIGHT\nWhen you put down a cup, it stays where you put it. That is because the Tortoise is holding it down for you, and holding down the table, and the house, and the hill the house stands on. It is slow work, and he does not complain.\nMoral: say thank you to your cup.","THE HERON WHO KEPT THE HOURS\nThe Heron stands in the river of the hours and does not move, so that the hours can go past him in their proper order, one and then the next. If he ever stepped out, they would all pile up against each other like logs at a weir, and next Nullday would happen on top of breakfast.\nMoral: stand still when it is your turn to stand still.","THE COCKEREL WHO KEPT THE MORNING\nEvery day the Cockerel calls the light up over the edge of the world, and every day it comes. Nobody has ever thanked him, because he is noisy.\nMoral: a thing can be useful and still be annoying. This is true of choirs.","THE HARE WHO KEPT THINGS GOING\nThe wind, the rivers, a ball thrown in the yard: they all go because the Hare keeps them going, and she never sits down to do it. That is why you cannot catch a hare.\nMoral: do not try. She is busy.","THE MAGPIE WHO KEPT WHAT WAS REMEMBERED\nThe Magpie loves bright things, and the brightest things of all are the things we remember: the way to your gran's door, the words of the third verse, the names of everybody you love. She keeps them in her nest so that nobody loses them for good.\nMoral: if you forget something, it is not gone. The Magpie has it. Ask her nicely.","THE OWL WHO KEPT THE RESTS\nBetween every note of every song there is a little rest, and the Owl keeps those. A rest sounds like nothing, but it is not nothing. It is our nothing: the good quiet, the quiet of a house asleep. Outside the edge of the world there is another quiet, which belongs to nobody, and it would very much like to come in. It cannot get past the Owl. She keeps all the gaps filled with our own quiet, the way you fill a doorway by standing in it.\nMoral: not all quiet is the same quiet.","THE SPARROW WHO ASKED\nThe Hedge-sparrow was very small and had no job at all. She went to each of the Keepers in turn and asked: When do you sleep?","The Badger said, When somebody comes to say down for me.","The Salamander said the same, and so did the Tortoise. The Heron said it without moving his beak. The Cockerel said it loudly. The Hare said it running past. The Magpie said it and then forgot she had, and the Owl said it very softly, in a rest.","Who is coming? asked the Sparrow.","Nobody answered. Nobody knew, and it is rude to make a thing up for a sparrow.","The Sparrow thought about this for a whole afternoon, which is a long time for a sparrow. Then she went back under the ground to the Badger and said, Show me how you say down.","And the Badger laughed, which nobody had ever heard him do before, and began to teach her.","Moral: little ones should make themselves useful. That is what I tell the choir, at any rate. The children have decided it means something else, and they will not tell me what."]},
{"id":"lib_c_hand_on_it","title":"Hand on It: A Tale of the Eleventh, in Four Counts","author":"Magda Rusch, of no House","genre":"folk tale","year":"418 SR","origin":"Cinderhold, Tier Eleven; a Bellows chapbook, carried west by a Deep Guild fitter","where":"quarry","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"tales","style":"paper","summary":"A deaf old striker, the granddaughter who swore to be his ears, and the day the Great Beat missed a fall.","text":["This is told the Eleventh way. Three counts of story, and then the fall, when you shut your mouth. Where it says Fall, wait for it.","Kolb struck plate on the Eleventh for forty years. He swung the bar. He kept the count.\nFall.","He could not hear the Beat any more. Forty years of it had taken his ears. He felt it in his boots instead.\nFall.","His granddaughter Grete carried water to the crew. She was eleven, and of no House. She talked between the falls, like everybody.\nFall.","The crew had a word for Kolb now. They said it between the falls, behind his back, though he could not have heard it to his face. The word was cold.\nFall.","It was the whistle. On the Eleventh everybody listens for it. A pipe that has run cool for a week remembers all at once, and before it remembers it sings, high and thin, F-sharp. Then you have four counts to get behind basalt. Mouth open. Eyes shut.\nFall.","A deaf man does not hear the whistle. A deaf man on a crew stands there while it sings. And whoever goes back for him is the second man burned.\nFall.","The foreman, Ruprecht, said it to Grete's face, because he could not say it to Kolb's ears. Your grandda's off the crew at end of shift. I'm sorry for it.\nFall.","Grete said: I'll be his ears.\nFall.","Ruprecht laughed. A child for ears, on the striking floor. Then he saw she meant it, and stopped laughing, and said: Who'll swear for that?\nFall.","Kolb had read every word off their mouths. He went to the furnace mouth. He took the ingot out bare-handed and held it.\nFall.","He held it through a whole count of four, with the crew watching and the smell going up, and he said: She's my ears. Hand on it.\nFall.","Nobody called him cold after that.\nFall.","So it went. Grete at his elbow, with the water and with her hand on his wrist. They had a sign between them. For the whistle: the flat of her hand, hard, once. Then you run.\nFall.","It went that way all spring and all summer. Twice the whistle came. Twice the flat of her hand came down on his wrist, and twice old Kolb was behind the basalt before the younger men.\nFall.","Then, on a Driftday at the back end of the year, the Great Beat missed.\nNot stopped. Nobody alive has heard it stop. It missed one fall, the way your heart does when you miss a stair in the dark.\nAnd there was no","There was no fall.","Every soul on the Eleventh froze where they stood. Mid-word, mid-swing, mid-breath. A body raised under the Beat does not know what to do in the gap where a fall should be. It holds you like a hand. Four counts. Sometimes more.","And in the gap, high and thin, a pipe began to sing.","Grete heard it. Grete was frozen with the rest, with her mouth open on a word and her hand on her grandda's wrist, and the hand would not come down.","Kolb did not hear the whistle. Kolb had not heard the Beat in eleven years. There was nothing missing in his ears, because there had been nothing in them to miss.","He looked at the crew standing like a row of nails. He looked at the child at his elbow with her mouth open. He looked where she was looking.","He picked Grete up under one arm. He took Ruprecht by the collar with the other hand. And he walked behind the basalt like a man going home for his supper.","One.\nTwo.\nThree.\nAnd the pipe remembered.","Fall.","The Beat came back. It always does. Five of the crew were burned, and they lived. Ruprecht has a scar from his ear to his collarbone, and on the Eleventh that is a rank, and he wears it like one. Nobody died. On the Eleventh, that is worth a date.\nFall.","Ruprecht came to Kolb after and said it between the falls, loud, so the whole crew heard: He kept his hand on it. There is nothing higher you can say of anybody in Cinderhold.\nFall.","Kolb could not hear it. He looked at Grete. She took his wrist and tapped it out with her fingers, four little taps, one to a count.\nFall.","That is what four taps on the wrist mean on the Eleventh now. Not the whistle; the whistle is still the flat of the hand. Four taps with the fingers mean: he kept his hand on it.\nFall.","Tell it between the falls. It is short on purpose."]},
{"id":"lib_d_yewdale_herbal","title":"A Herbal of the Resonant Reach, with Corrections","author":"Barnabas Yewdale, physician, of Greenroot; annotated in pencil by Prue Gorse, herb-woman, late of Greenroot","genre":"herbal","year":"391 SR; annotated 418–419 SR","origin":"Printed at Oakhaven; annotated at Oakhaven","where":"greenroot_chapel","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"craft","style":"parch","summary":"An old physician's herbal of orchard, Flume and hedgerow, and the Greenroot herb-woman who has spent two years telling him in the margins exactly where he is wrong.","text":["A HERBAL OF THE RESONANT REACH. Being the Plants of Orchard, Flume and Hedgerow, their Virtues and their Seasons, set down for the Use of Households. By Barnabas Yewdale, Physician, of Greenroot. Printed at Oakhaven, 391 SR.","In pencil, across the title page: Prue Gorse, late of Greenroot, her book now. Yewdale was my grandmother's doctor, and she said he never once washed his hands. He is right about willow and wrong about most of the rest, and since the quarry he is wrong about all of it. I have marked him. Read the margins first. P.G., the back-end of 418.","From the preface: The Reach is the kindest country in the Octave to a physician, for here the law of Growth is our partner. A wound in the Reach wishes to close. A bone wishes to knit. The physician's whole art in this province is to stand aside and let the country do its work, and to add, at the proper season, the herbs set down hereafter.","Margin: Stand aside. That is exactly what you must not do any more. The country does its work now whether you want it or not, and its work is more. A wound closes over the splinter in it. Two cut fingers bound together will heal into one finger. Clean everything twice before you let it close, and then watch it, because it will not stop when it ought.","SILVER WILLOW. The bark of the silver willow, which grows along the Flume below the lower lock and in great plenty in the Spectral Woods. Peel it in spring, dry it in the shade, steep it a quarter hour. For fevers, the aching of the joints, and the headache that follows a long singing. Bitter. The bitterness is the virtue.","Margin: Right. Still right. The only page in this book I would sell you. Bettany puts it in her tonic with honey and raw egg, and when there is no egg, which is always, she puts in more honey and says nothing. Peel it from the north side of the tree. Do not ask me why. My mother did.","SWEET REED-PITH. The white pith of the reed that grows in the margin of the Flume, called by children sweet-reed. Boiled in milk, it is given to Cantors in the orchard schools before a long tending, and to infants for the croup.","Margin: Boiled in milk. There has been no milk worth the name since the Cascade; it turns before it is out of the pail. Chew it raw. It eases a throat, and I will tell you something Yewdale never knew, because in his day nobody needed to: it steadies a person who is frightened of their own quiet. You keep chewing, and you keep making a small wet sound, and a small sound is what we all live in now. Give it to the night-hummers. Give it to anyone who has to sit up with the dying.","RESONANT MOSS. The green moss of the humming stones, which shivers when it is touched and takes the note of whatever it grows on. Laid on as a poultice, it draws a wound together in a night. The Reach's chief gift to physic.","Margin: In a night. It drew Jem Cutler's thigh together round a nail he had not told anyone was in it, and he walked on that nail till midsummer. Do not lay moss on anything you have not opened, cleaned and looked into with a lamp. I would strike this page out entirely if the Grafters did not buy the stuff from me by the sack.","KNITBONE, or comfrey. The root grated and bound about a broken limb, which it knits in half the common time.","Margin: Nothing in the Reach needs help knitting. Burn it.","LILAC MOON-MOSS. A moss of the drowned stones of Whisper-Grove, which gives a soft lilac light in the jar. I know it only by report from the physicians of the Woods, who prize it above gold for deafness and the ringing of the ears.","Margin: I had a pinch of it off a Woods pedlar the summer before the Grove went quiet, and it still glows in its jar on my sill, which is more than can be said for the pedlar. It is good for ears, the best thing there is. A carter's boy came to me deaf from a powder-flash on the quarry road and it gave him his hearing back in two days, and the first thing he heard was his mother crying at my door, and he did not know what the sound was. Nobody will come up the Grove road selling it again. When my jar is done, it is done.","LIME FLOWER. The blossom of the lime, as grows by every chapel in the province, taken as a tisane for sleep and for a fretful heart.","Margin: The chapel lime here still flowers, and the bees still go to it. Nobody sleeps anyway.","WHITE POPPY. For pain past bearing, given in drops, and at the last, in larger drops, by the physician's own hand and no other's.","Margin: The Grafters have their own drops for this now, the long drops, and they carry them in a brown bottle for the end. I do not know what is in them. I know what they are for, and I am told the surgeon at Oakhaven gives them gently, which is more than I will say for his Office.","On a blank leaf at the back, in her hand: MUTE-DROPS. Yewdale has no page for these, because no physician in his day would have dreamed of them, so I give them one. The Grafting Office's mute-drops: three on the tongue, and a quarter hour. They do not take the pain. They take the voice. A sawboy once left a bottle on my step by mistake. It smelled of bitter almond and wet slate and something I have no name for, and I poured it into the Flume, and the eels came up to look. It is not a herb. It is not physic. They say it is a kindness to the whole street. I say a medicine that is kinder to the street than to the patient is a policy, and I will not have a policy in my book.","Below it: BURL. Neither of us has a page for this. People come to my door asking for a herb for the burl, and there is none. I sold two, the first year, a sorrel wash and a bitter salve, because a woman wept on my step and I could not bear to send her off with nothing. I am ashamed of both. If a child's fingers are putting out white hairs toward the hearth, do not come to me. Keep the hands warm and dry and out of the damp, and go to whoever in your town still sings.","On the inside of the back board: To whoever has this book after me. Yewdale wrote that the physician's whole art in the Reach was to stand aside. Mine has been to stand in the way, mostly of things that wanted to grow. I leave it with the willow page folded down. It is the only page in forty years I never had to correct, and it is bark off a tree, and you peel it on the north side."]},
{"id":"lib_d_ears_after_dark","title":"Ears After Dark: A Field Guide for Those Who Sit the Crossings","author":"Tilda Dunnock, night-hummer of the Tannery Row crossing","genre":"field guide","year":"419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"watchhouse","how":"quest","at":"underhedge","quest":"ilse","style":"paper","summary":"An old night-hummer's guide to the sounds of an Oakhaven night, arranged like a bird-book, for the new ones the Watch keeps sending her, who keep standing up.","text":["I have sat the Tannery Row crossing every night since the Hedge closed, which is two years and a season, on the same stool and in the same shawl, and the Watch has asked me to write down what I know for the new ones, because the new ones keep standing up. Do not stand up. That is the first thing. A woman standing at a crossing in the dark is a woman about to go somewhere, and everything out there knows it.","This is a guide to listening. I have set it out the way the old bird-books are set out, by what you will hear, and it is short, because most of what you need to know is: hum, and do not answer.","WHAT YOU OUGHT TO HEAR. Learn the town first, so that you know when something is missing from it. A hush-lamp whistles high and thin and never quite steady. There are twenty-two within hearing of my stool, and I know them the way you know your children's coughs. A cradle creaks on the Row at the second hour and again at the fourth. The tavern shutters tick as they cool. The oaks chime when there is wind, and since the quarry, sometimes when there is none. Under all of it, in your teeth more than your ears, the bore. Write your own crossing down, every sound and its hour. You will want the list later.","HOUNDS. You will not hear a hound. You will hear what it has eaten. A hound comes the way rain comes onto a slate roof, a soft quick patter that is not quite in the right place, and inside the patter are sounds that do not belong to the hour: a child laughing where there is no child, a cartwheel where no cart is moving, a man shouting run. Worst and surest of all, a bell. Nobody in the Reach has rung a bell since the Synod took them for the bore, and the hounds still have them. If you hear a bell, it is not a bell.","Before any of that, listen to the roosting birds. A hound goes through a hedgerow like a hot iron through wax, and the birds go still in front of it, one bush at a time. If you hear the quiet walking toward you, it is not quiet. It is something with the birds' silence carried in front of it like a bow-wave.","What draws them: music, first and always, and the louder and the dearer the better. A scream will draw them. A name said aloud draws them faster, and I do not know why, and I would rather not. Keep names indoors after dark.","What to do when you hear one: nothing. Hum lower, not higher, and keep it in your chest. They come to a song, not to a murmur; a murmur is only the town breathing. If one comes close it is not your hum that will save you. It is glass, and you will not have any, so pray the Watch has.","BEDS. In the morning, look for where the dew is not. A hound lies up in an oval a little longer than a big dog, and where it has lain the grass is dry and flat, and no sound carries in it for a day. Drop a pebble in. If you do not hear it land, go round. Birds will not land on a bed. Children will want to stand in one, to see what it is like. Do not let them.","VOICES. This is the part the new ones ask about, and the part I like writing least. You will hear people you love. At the Hedge, mostly, at the dead hour, low and warm and a little tired, the way they sounded at the end of a long day. They will know things. They will know the name you had as a child. They will ask you to come and help them, or they will say that they are cold.","I will put it as plain as I can. Never answer a voice you cannot see. Not to say no. Not to say you are sorry. Answering tells it where you are, and whatever you say, it keeps. A word you give it on Rootday it will give back to you by Nullday, in your own mouth.","A trick works once. If you and your sister have a whistle that means come home, and you use it where it can be heard, it is its whistle now. Make another, and do not use that one either.","I know a man who listens to his wife at a crossing. Everybody knows him. I will not name him, because none of us has the heart. He does not answer. He only listens. I put him in so that you will understand I am not telling you what is easy.","SILENCE. Silence comes in three kinds, and you must learn the first two by heart, so that you are never mistaken about the third.","The first is the Hedge's silence. Wool and thorn swallow sound. Stand near the Hedge and your own voice comes back to you small and close, as if you were talking into a pillow. That is a good silence. That is a silence somebody made, on purpose and by hand, for you.","The second is the silence a hound leaves. It is dry, like the bed. It sits in one place, it does not move, and it wears off by the next night.","The third I will not write about. My mother said a thing written down is a thing invited, and I have sat out too many nights to start sending invitations now. I will tell you only how you will know. You will notice that you cannot hear something you ought to: the reed in the lamp, the cradle at the second hour, your own breath. And it will not frighten you. That is how you will know. When you notice it, hum. Hum anything, and hum it badly. Say your own name into your shawl.","WHAT WE FIND IN THE MORNING. Once in my two years I went to relieve a crossing at the far end of the town at first light, and I found the stool, and the shawl folded on it, and the lamp still burning, and the reed in the lamp gone still. Nobody on that street had heard a thing. That is the whole of the entry. I have put it here so that you will not stand up.","That is all I know. Sit down. Keep the shawl on. Hum. When the shift changes at the dead hour, say it to the one who comes to take your stool: all's well, all's quiet, keep to the Narrow. Say it when it is not true. It is not for them. It is so that there is a voice at the crossing."]},
{"id":"lib_d_fellers_book","title":"The Fellers' Book: On the Cutting of Iron-Bough","author":"Jethro Nettleford, feller-master of Oakhaven; with a postscript by Tomas, timber-cutter","genre":"manual","year":"406 SR; postscript 419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"clearing","how":"pile","at":"clearing","style":"parch","summary":"A gruff old feller-master's lessons for apprentices on knocking, felling and naming the timber that rings like bronze, and what his last apprentice had to add after the Hedge went up.","text":["Iron-bough is not timber. Get that into your head on your first day and you will keep your fingers longer than I kept mine. Timber is dead wood. Iron-bough is a tree that has not yet agreed to be dead, and the whole of the fellers' craft is the getting of that agreement.","I have felled in the Reach for forty-one years and trained eleven apprentices. Nine are living. Two have beams named after them in the Greenhall roof, which is the only memorial a feller gets, and which neither of them would have chosen, being careless men.","ON KNOWING THE GROVE. A grove knows its own. Walk into a stand of iron-bough as a stranger and the trees go quiet round you, a ring of quiet that widens as you walk, and every feller in the valley lifts his head and says: somebody is in the north stand who oughtn't to be. So you do not cut a grove you have not walked. An apprentice walks his master's grove every evening for a month before he is let near it with an edge, until the trees chime round him as they chime round anyone else. Then he is furniture to them, and he may begin.","ON THE KNOCK. Before you cut, you knock. Strike the trunk with the back of your axe at shoulder height, and listen. A sound tree gives you a clean bronze note that hangs in the air as long as a held breath. A buzz in it means worm. Two notes at once means the heart is split, and the tree will twist as it goes. A dead note, a knock like a fist on a door with nobody home, means hollow, and you leave it, because a hollow iron-bough falls where it likes. Chalk the note on the bark. Your master will check it. He will be right and you will be wrong for about four years.","ON THE SINGING DOWN. No feller in the Reach puts an edge to an old tree until a Cantor has sung it down. This is not piety. I have no piety. It is practical. The felling-song tells the sap to go down and the tree to sleep, and a sleeping tree falls true, splits clean, and does not throw its crown back at you. I have seen a tree felled awake. It took Jago Holcombe's arm off at the shoulder and then lay there ringing for an hour as if it were pleased with itself. Wait for the Cantor. Pay her in cider. Stand still while she sings and take your hat off, and if you laugh I will put you in the Flume.","ON THE FELL. Cut the mouth on the side you want her to fall, a third of the way through, then the back-cut a hand higher. Do not cut through the hinge; the hinge steers her. When she begins to go, step back along the line you have already cleared, and never run, because a man running cannot hear what the tree is doing.","ON RINGING-WOOD DAY. We fell for the year at the first hard frost, when the sap is lowest, and the whole of Oakhaven comes out to hear it. Each trunk rings as it falls, and a big one rings for as long as it takes to sing the Sheaf Song through, and the children run under the note as if it were rain. Then the beams are named. Every beam in a Reach house has a name, cut small on the end-grain where it sits in the wall: the name of the tree, or of the feller, or of somebody's grandmother, or a joke. The beam over my own bed is called Not Tonight. My wife named it.","ON THE WOOD AFTER. Iron-bough goes on growing a little after it is cut, a hair a year, which is why Reach doors stick in the spring and why you must never trust a joiner from outside the province. A good beam rings faintly for years whenever the house is struck. Your grandchildren will know the beams you cut by their notes. That is all the fame a feller ever gets, and it is better than most.","Mind your hands. Mind your master. Wait for the Cantor. J. Nettleford, Feller-Master, Oakhaven, the back-end of 406.","POSTSCRIPT, in a different hand, written larger. Jethro is dead seven years and cannot argue, so here is what has changed. I am Tomas. I was his twelfth and his last; he had me one winter.","Everything he says about the knock is still true, and the knock will now get you killed. The wood rings louder since the quarry, and a bell-note in the open brings hounds the way a supper-bell brings boys. So: carded wool on the blade, bound tight, four strokes to a bundle before it beats flat. Cut in a gust, when the whole grove is chiming anyway and one more note is lost in it. If a hound comes up, stop. Call the job off. Leave the tree a day to forget the hour you were at it. Never run with a log. A log knocks against everything you own.","There is no Cantor to sing them down now. The orchard-cantor was taken the year of the Hedge, and you do not ask a Cantor to sing above the Narrow for a tree when she has a whole town to sing small for. So we lay a palm flat on the bark and say sorry, quietly, and I do not know if it helps. They fall awake. They throw their crowns and kick the saw. I have lost three fingers to it, and the fourth grew back wrong, and I keep it in a glove, and I will not show it you.","We held Ringing-Wood Day last back-end in the lee of the Hedge, with wool on every blade. Nobody came out to hear. Nobody named a beam. The timber goes up the quarry road on the Synod's quota to shore a headframe, and a headframe does not want a name.","Jethro wrote that your grandchildren will know your beams by their notes. Mine are holding up a hole in the ground. I have thought what I like of myself since Firstfruit. Knock first anyway. It is the only honest thing left in the job."]},
{"id":"lib_d_narrow_larder","title":"The Narrow Larder: Receipts for a Lean Year, with Remarks","author":"Philippa Oatley, who kept the cookshop by the market, Oakhaven","genre":"cookery","year":"419 SR","origin":"Oakhaven","where":"tavern","how":"shop","at":"moor_bookseller","price":1,"style":"paper","summary":"An Oakhaven cook's receipts from the Fracture winter and the shut years since, for bread that will not stop rising, pies with no egg, and suppers nobody else needs to hear.","text":["Hetty says I am to write it down before I die, so that somebody in this town can still make a pie that does not go off like a mortar. I said I would, if she put it on her shelf where people could see it and not under the bar with the strong cider. She has promised. If you are reading this under the bar, take it out.","THE FIRST LEAN YEAR. Everybody under twenty thinks the lean years began with the Hedge. They did not. They began on the morning the sky rang, in 412, and the winter after was the worst eating this valley has had in anyone's memory. Milk turned between the pail and the dairy door. Cheese would not take. The cider went flat in the barrel, a quarter-tone flat, and the old men said they could taste it, and they could. The harvest went into the barns heavy and came out of them grey and tasting of nothing. Then the wagons came up the Greenroot road with grain, and the Governor said we would eat, and we ate, and nobody in this house asked what it cost. I will not start asking in a cookery book.","RECEIPTS OF THE FRACTURE WINTER, for when it comes again.","Hedge pottage. Nettles, cut young and with gloves; sorrel; a handful of barley if you have it, and an onion if you are a Governor. Boil. Do not salt it, because there is no salt. There will be salt later, and you will weep over it.","Acorn cake. Iron-bough acorns, which nobody ate before 412 and everybody ate after. Shell them and leach them in the Flume in a net for three days, or they will turn your mouth inside out. Pound them in a mortar. They ring in the mortar, so put a cloth over it. Bake flat on a hot stone. It tastes of a floor, but a clean floor.","Eel boiled twice. The Flume eel is green, and so is its broth, and the first water is not for drinking. Pour it off, fresh water, and boil again. Wrap what is left in dock-leaf. Children will not eat it the first time. They will eat it the third.","WHAT WE EAT NOW.","Bread. Since the quarry, bread wants to rise and does not know when to stop. Punch it down. Punch it down again. Never leave dough to prove overnight. I have seen a loaf come up out of its crock and across a table like a sleeping cat, and the woman next door had to take her oven door off with a crowbar. Bake in a lidded crock with a stone on the lid, and listen. Good bread is quiet. Bread that creaks is not done growing.","Flour. The fields regrow overnight now, which sounds like a mercy until you taste the third cutting. First-cut flour is for bread, second for porridge, and the third for the paste you hang wallpaper with. Keep the Last Sheaf's flour for the hearth bread and nothing else. The last stand is still uncut in the north field as I write this, so you know the time of year, and they are saying the Church will want it put off. We shall see.","Mutton. There is none. The flocks went into the Hedge for their wool, and then they went into us, one a month, and I cooked a good many of them myself, and I am not sorry. If anyone in this town sells you mutton pie, ask him whose. If he says it is lamb, ask him whose lamb.","Eggs. There are none. The hens went quiet first, the autumn after the Hedge went in. I will not say more than that, and what was left of them went in the pot. To bind a pie without egg: a spoon of honey and a spoon of flour, beaten with a little cider. To glaze it: milk, if you can get milk that has not turned, which you cannot, so do not glaze it. Nobody in Oakhaven has looked at a shiny pie in two years and we are none the worse for it.","Bettany's tonic, which she gives for weakness, is honey, raw egg and willow-bark. When you ask her where the egg comes from she looks at you as if you had asked her where babies come from, and she of all women should know.","Moot-curd. Aged beside a humming stone, and it tastes faintly of static, the way the air does before thunder. It is the one thing in this book that has got better since 412. I do not know why and I have stopped asking it.","COOKING QUIET. Felt your lids. Wooden spoons, and never iron on iron. Take the kettle off before it sings; a kettle that sings is a kettle calling. Grind before dusk. Chop on a folded cloth. If you must crack a bone for the marrow, do it under water in a bucket. Nobody needs to hear your supper but you.","THE LAST RECEIPT, FOR THE SHEAF. When the last field is cut, every house in the Reach bakes hearth bread from the new flour, one round loaf, and it is broken by hand and never cut, and you give the first piece to whoever is oldest at the table, and the heel to the youngest, and the crumbs to the hearth. That is the whole receipt. You do not need me for it. Your grandmother did it, and so will you.","If the Church will not let us keep the Sheaf this year, bake the bread anyway, after dark, and break it with the shutters to and the lamp low, and pass it round, and eat it. That is all lean-year cookery has ever been. You eat anyway, and you eat together, and you eat quietly. The rest is only salt."]},
{"id":"lib_d_telling_the_bees","title":"Telling the Bees","author":"Ennis Crowle, beekeeper, of the Flume road","genre":"manual","year":"418 SR","origin":"the Flume road, by Oakhaven","where":"lyra_house","how":"quest","at":"flume_road","quest":"ilse","style":"parch","summary":"A beekeeper of the Flume road writes down, for the first time in her family's memory, how to keep straw skeps in the Narrow and how to tell the bees the news.","text":["My mother kept bees on the Flume road, and her mother before her, and none of us ever wrote anything down, because a bee-woman's craft goes from mouth to ear, close, at the straw, the way everything that matters goes. But there are fewer mouths every year, and fewer ears, and the young ones who come to me for a swarm do not know how to tell. So I have written it in the plainest words I have, and if the bees think the less of me for it, they may say so.","SKEPS. Coil the straw and bind it with split bramble, never twine. Set the skep on a stone stool in the lee of a wall, with a straw hackle over it for the rain. A good hive hums low and steady, like a kettle thinking about it. Learn your own hive's hum the first week, because everything is in it. A hive that has lost its queen howls: a thin, ragged, high note that you will hear from the road, and that makes the dogs uneasy.","Since the quarry, the bees work too fast. Comb draws itself out overnight. I have had a skep fill in a week that ought to have taken a summer, and brood come out of season in the back-end. Take the honey early and often, or the comb will grow through the straw and split the skep from the inside.","TELLING. The bees must be told. Every birth and every death in the household, and every stranger who comes to stay. If they are not told, they go. You come out one morning and the straw is empty and still warm, and they are off in the wood, hanging in a clump on a branch like a bruise, waiting for somebody who will tell them things. My grandmother said the bees are the house's ears. I think it is simpler than that. They live with us, and they want to know what we know. So would you.","How to tell. Go at dusk, when they are all home. Stand close, closer than is comfortable, with your mouth near the straw. Knock once, softly, two knuckles and one tap, the way we knock in Oakhaven. Then murmur the news. Not loud. A bee minds a shout worse than a hound does. Say it plainly. A girl born to the house, and her name. Or the name of who has died, and that they are gone and will not be coming to the hives again. If there is more than one dead, tell them in the order of the years, oldest first, so the bees can keep them straight. Tell a stranger by name, and where they came from, and that they are to be let past. Then wait. When the hive has had it, the hum settles lower, the way a room changes when somebody has made room on the bench.","In my mother's day you sang it. There was a telling-song, four lines, which I will not write here, because nobody may sing it now, and a song written down and never sung is a sad thing to leave lying on a page.","SINCE THE HEDGE. When they planted the Hedge, they took every fleece in the Reach for the wool, and I gave my skeps' winter wrappings with the rest. That first winter a hive's hum carried a quarter mile on a still night, and the hounds came down the Flume road to it twice. I lost two skeps, and very nearly my own self. So now I wrap them in fleece again, bought dear, and I set them in the lee of the reed-banks where the water talks over them.","A hum is a sound, and a sound brings hounds, and there is no help for that. But a hive that has not been told goes, and a hive that goes leaves you with nothing at all, and I would rather the hum.","HONEY. Reach honey is the Cantors' honey. Warmed in the hand and stirred with a drop of oil, it is what they take for a torn throat, and every Cantor in the Reach has a torn throat now. I give it free to any Cantor who comes up the road. So should you.","MY OWN TELLING. When I die, somebody go down the Flume road and tell them. Go at dusk. Knock once. Tell them Ennis has gone and will not be coming to the hives again, and that they are to stay for whoever comes next. They will know already; bees always know. Tell them anyway. It was never for them that we did it."]},
{"id":"lib_d_reed_and_lamp","title":"Reed and Lamp: On Cutting Flume-Reed and Making Hush-Lamp Whistles","author":"Dorran Sedge, keeper of the river gate, Oakhaven","genre":"manual","year":"418 SR","origin":"Oakhaven, at the river gate","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"craft","style":"paper","summary":"The river-gate keeper who once tended the Cantors' reed-beds explains, at length and against his wife's wishes, how to cut Flume-reed, why a cut reed dies in a day, and how a hush-lamp whistles.","text":["I kept the reed-beds at Reedside for the Conservatory before I kept the river gate, which is near thirty years with my hands in reed one way and another, and Mabbie says that if I am going to talk about it every supper I may as well write it down and talk about something else at supper. So.","First, what is gone. The orchard wind-reed is gone. For three hundred years the Cantors planted it in rows and pruned it to pitch, until a whole valley of it would sing a chord when the wind came off the hills, and I tended those rows from a boy. On the morning the sky rang, every wind-reed in the Reach went to something like spun sugar in an hour, and the next wind took it. I was at the river gate that morning. I heard the orchards stop. I will not write more than that.","What is left is Flume-reed, the wild cousin, that grows in the wet along the channel, under the river gate and down past the lower lock. It was never planted, never pruned, and never taught a note in its life, and my belief, which the Conservatory has never asked for, is that this is why it lived. The orchard reed had been told what to be for three hundred years. When the world went a quarter-tone wrong it had nothing of its own to fall back on. Flume-reed had never been told anything. It just went on being reed.","CUTTING. Cut at dusk, when the reed has been drinking all day. Cut low and on the slant, one clean stroke, with a knife you keep for nothing else, and lay the reed in wet moss at once. Three from a bed in a day and no more, or the bed sulks and gives you nothing for a week. Cut quietly. A reed-knife makes no sound if you know your business, and if you do not know your business the hounds will teach it you.","WHY A CUT REED DIES IN A DAY. Every cutter is asked this and every cutter has his own answer. Mine is that a cut reed is a reed waiting to be told what it is. While it is green it will hold any pitch you give it. Give it one before this time tomorrow and it will keep that note the year round, till the next Reed-Hanging, and longer if you oil it. Leave it untold past the day and it gives up waiting. It goes pale and light and brittle, and it will not take a note again for love or money. A dry reed is good for kindling and nothing else. Do not keep dry reeds about the house. Mabbie says they look like bad news, and she is right.","TUNING. You tune by the slot and by the length. Cut the lip with a fine knife, a sliver at a time, blowing across it between cuts, until the note sits where you want it. A reed is tuned against the Root note, a hundred and seventy-four, which any Cantor will give you and most old men can hum. For a First Reed you want the child's own note, and the Cantor finds that for you; I have cut the reeds for half the children on the river street. For a lamp you want nobody's note at all.","THE HUSH-LAMP. A hush-lamp is a lamp with a reed in its chimney. The heat goes up, the draught draws across the reed's lip, and the reed speaks for as long as the flame burns: high and thin and never quite steady. The unsteadiness is the whole of the art. A steady note is a thing that can be learned and followed. A wavering one is only the house breathing.","To make one, you want a brass collar to hold the reed above the throat of the chimney, a hand's width over the flame and no closer, or it scorches. Set the slot to face the draught. Seal the collar with beeswax; Ennis Crowle's, from the Flume road, is the best, and she will tell you so herself. Tune a lamp-reed a little sharp of the Root, and never to any living person's note. A lamp that sounds like a person is a lamp that something may come and talk to.","Trim your wick right. Too high and the reed shrieks. Too low and it stops, and a lamp that is burning with its reed stopped is worse than a dark one, because you will look at the flame and think you are safe. Listen to your lamp before you sleep. If you cannot hear it, get up.","OIL AND FROST. Oil your reeds once a month, colza or a drop of lamp-oil on a rag. A reed cracks in the dry and splits in the frost, and a First Reed that splits is a grief to a house. If it splits, the note is not lost. Any old body who knew the person can hum it near enough, and a reed tuned near enough is still a comfort. A reed that is a little wrong is a comfort, sometimes, in a way that a right one is not.","REED-HANGING. In Reedmonth, when the reed comes up, every house takes down last year's reeds and hangs new ones, tuned to each child in it. The street used to be loud with tuning for a day; you could not hear yourself think on the river street for the whistling. These last two years we have done it with our mouths an inch from the reed and the doors shut, and you would not know from the street that anything had happened at all. Every door on the river street has a new reed over it all the same.","People ask me why I bother, when nobody past the thorn will hear a reed of ours again, with the Hedge eating every note before it is halfway down the lane. I tell them a reed does not have to be heard to be right. It only has to be given its note.","Mabbie says that is enough about reed. It is never enough about reed. D. Sedge, at the river gate."]},
{"id":"lib_d_tank_and_envelope","title":"Tank and Envelope: A Handbook for the Riggers and Tank-Tuners of Envelope Ships","author":"Ondine Abara, yard-mistress of the drift-yard below the Long Mole, Port Cantabile; this copy with pencil notes signed P.","genre":"manual","year":"410 SR (fourth printing, 415 SR); pencil notes 417–419 SR","origin":"Port Cantabile","where":"p16k_kestrel_below","how":"pile","at":"p16k_kestrel_below","style":"paper","summary":"A Rust Coast yard-mistress's sharp-tongued handbook on persuading drift-glass to lift and keeping an old envelope ship in the air, in a copy a young engineer has argued with in pencil.","text":["This book is for the rigger and the tank-tuner of an envelope ship: sixty feet or thereabouts, oak and brass, a sailcloth envelope over her, lift-tanks slung beneath her keel. It is not for the courier-cutters, which have no envelope and no manners, nor for the great Consortium freighters, whose engineers will not read a book written by a woman who learned her trade on a slipway. You know who you are. Your ship is older than you, and patched like a quilt, and you love her more than is sensible.","ON DRIFT-GLASS. Everything begins with the glass. Drift-glass is Velocity glass tuned to lift. It is pale green as sea-ice when you hold it in your hand, and packed in a brass tank with a reed-valve at one end it burns a soft cobalt and wants, above all things, to move. The whole art of the envelope ship lies in persuading it that the way it most wants to move is up. Her core is another matter: Fortis-cut lodestone in an iron cage amidships, which takes the weight off her and asks nothing of you. Leave it to the Fortis men. The core makes her light. The glass makes her go. You do not command drift-glass. You persuade it. Captains do not understand this, and it is no use telling them.","ON LISTENING TO A TANK. A tank is full of song. When she is content she hums a low chord that you feel through the deck in your heels. Learn that chord in your first week aboard, as you would learn your mother's voice, because everything you need to know is in it. A sour note in the chord means the glass is unsettled: damp in the valve, a knock in the cradle, or salt. Salt above all. Salt gets into a tank and makes her moody, and a moody tank lifts in sulks and starts, and you will spend your nights with a tuning-hammer putting her right.","Pencil, in the margin: She likes to be told what you are going to do before you do it. Not in the book. True, though. P.","ON THE TUNING-HAMMER. Felt head, brass shaft, never iron. Tap the cradle, never the tank. A tap on the tank itself is an insult, and she will remember it for a week.","Pencil: Mine. Not the captain's. He uses it on the galley stove. P.","ON THE ENVELOPE. The envelope keeps what the tanks breathe out, gives her the last of her lift, and gives her most of her temper in a wind. Close-woven sailcloth, doped every season. Patch inside and outside both, in long laps, and stitch with the grain. A patch is no disgrace. An unpatched hole is.","Pencil: Better than sailcloth for a weep: weaverling silk. Do not ask where it came from. Deep Woods, 419. P.","ON BALLAST. Iron in the keel-sling. It is what keeps her from flying like a leaf in a squall. Every new captain wants to throw it over to make height, and every old rigger has watched what happens next.","ON LIFT. Do your figures on a slate, and do them twice, and when the captain does not like the answer, do them a third time and give him the same answer. A soul is a hundredweight and a half with his boots on, a child half that, and in the whole history of skyships nobody has ever come aboard with less baggage than he said he had. Water is the heaviest thing you carry that you cannot do without. The long gun is the heaviest thing you carry that you can.","Pencil: Eighteen. Not twenty. I said so. P.","ON A WEEPING TANK. Drift-glass does not burst. If a tank is holed, it weeps: a slow cobalt vapour, and the lift goes out with it. You will hear it before you see it, because the chord goes thin and climbs. Clip the hole, seal the clip, and get her down while you still choose where. A clipped tank is good for forty miles if you are lucky and four if you are not. A whole new tank wants a drift-bench and a yard, and somebody who knows how to tune one. There is a yard below the Long Mole, and I am somebody.","Pencil: Forty miles, then Cantabile. He will not like it. P.","ADDED TO THE FOURTH PRINTING: ON FLYING IN THE NARROW. Since the Screaming Year, riggers have asked me what the hounds make of a ship. At height, nothing on the ground hears her. Below a hundred feet a sour tank carries further than you would credit, and a hound will follow a chord as it follows a song. Keep her sweet when you are low. Moor high. Do not shout orders over the rail; learn hand-cant, as the deaf bosuns do, and hire one if you can. And if at the dead hour you hear your mother calling you from the rigging, it is not your mother, and you are not to go up.","ON THE WINCH AND THE LINE. A winch drum is only as strong as the deck-beams it is bolted through, and the beams are only as strong as the hull they hold together. If whatever hangs on the end of your line is heavier than your ship can lift, it is your ship that will go down to it, not the other way about. Keep an axe beside every drum. Everybody on the Coast knows the saying: the line is not a promise; the line is a rope.","There is no pencil note here. Somebody has underlined the last sentence, once, very hard, so that the lead has gone through the paper.","A LAST WORD. A ship is not a house. She is a set of arguments with the air that you win every day by a little, and lose all at once if you stop making them. Riggers come and go. Ships, if you are good at your work, stay. Be good at your work.","Pencil, on the back board, smaller than the rest: She hums sweeter when I sleep on the tanks. Mags says that is only my snoring. Mags cannot hear my snoring. P."]},
{"id":"lib_d_grafting_handbook","title":"Handbook of the Grafting Office (Oakhaven Works Issue)","author":"The Grafting Office of the Iron Synod; this copy annotated in ink in an unknown hand","genre":"manual","year":"417 SR","origin":"the Organum; issued at the Oakhaven Works","where":"surgery","how":"pile","at":"surgery","style":"office","summary":"The Synod's regulations for surgeons who cut the burl-sick, in the Office's flat hand, with a working surgeon's replies in the margin.","text":["HANDBOOK OF THE GRAFTING OFFICE. Issued under the authority of the Office for the use of Surgeon-Grafters and their attendants in provinces subject to law-rot. Oakhaven Works Issue, 417 SR. This handbook is the property of the Office. It is not to be lent, copied or annotated.","Margin, in small neat ink: Annotated.","1. Of the Grafter. The Surgeon-Grafter is an officer of the Synod and not of the household. He attends the patient on behalf of the street. His first duty is to the silence of the street, his second to the law of the province, and his third to the patient.","Margin: Read it backwards.","2. Of attendance. Inspections are made every second Rootday. The Grafter is accompanied by two attendants, styled sawboys, by a brazier of banked coals in an iron cage, and by an escort. Harness is to be felted and hooves booted. The Grafter will wear the mask, which renders the breath at the level of the Narrow, at all times in the patient's house.","Margin: Not for the children. They have enough to look at.","3. Of examination. The affected part is to be examined by lens and by pressure, and the sound of the pressure attended to. Living tissue gives. Tissue gone to wood answers. The chest is to be examined by trumpet below the collarbone for the dry sound, which signifies roots in the lung and places the patient beyond the remedy of the saw.","Margin: It sounds like wind in barley. You will not forget it the first time, so do not try.","4. Of the mute-drops. The patient must not be permitted to cry out. Administer the mute-drops a quarter hour before the first incision, and remind the family that silence is a mercy extended to the whole street. The drops do not relieve pain, and the Grafter is not to represent to the family that they do.","Margin: Somebody must hold the head. The Office does not say who. In this town the Cantor is usually there before you. Let her.","5. Of the instruments. The hush-saw, felted at the grip, with a blade thin enough to flex. The sap-iron, heated at the brazier and applied to the cut surface to stop the bleed, which in this province is green and slow to cease. No instrument is to be laid on stone or permitted to fall.","Margin: The sap-iron is the one loud thing in the morning. Warn them before it, not after.","6. Of the sap-mark. Where the patient is found past remedy by the saw, the Grafter will chalk the outer door in green: a circle, with one line struck through. The mark signifies past cutting. The household so marked is to be visited monthly and kept comfortable, and at the last the long drops are to be administered by the Grafter's own hand.","Margin: That is all it means. Write it on the door small, and tell them what it means before you go, so that they do not have to ask the street.","7. Of the register. The Grafter keeps a register of marked households, giving the name, the house and the date of marking. The register is the property of the Synod. It is to be surrendered without delay to any officer of the Church or of the Arbiterium presenting a warrant under seal.","Something was written in the margin here and scraped away with a knife, more than once, until the paper is thin enough to see the lamp through.","8. Of children. Children are to be inspected as adults are. Early signs in the hands are to be recorded, and are not to be left to the judgement of the family.","Margin: Unusual is also a word. So is recheck next month.","9. Of conduct. The Grafter will not hum, sing, pray aloud or converse with the patient beyond the questions required. The Grafter will not express to the family any opinion as to the justice of the procedure.","Margin: No. But I may go slowly. Nothing here says how fast.","10. Of this Handbook. The Grafter will keep this Handbook about his person and consult it in all cases of doubt.","Margin: I do. In every case. And then I consult the margin."]},
{"id":"lib_d_rudds_almanac","title":"Rudd's Back-End Book: Signs of Weather for the Downs and the Reach","author":"Absalom Rudd, beacon-keeper of the sheep-walk","genre":"almanac","year":"419 SR","origin":"the Bellwether, Wethercombe","where":"p22_bellwether","how":"pile","at":"p22_bellwether","style":"paper","summary":"The old keeper of the fold-fires sets down the weather-signs his father taught him, marks which of them have lied since the sky rang, and admits to one he has no sign for.","text":["I kept the fold-fires on three hills for forty back-ends, and a beacon-keeper who cannot read weather is only a man setting fire to good furze. These are the signs I go by. My father gave me most of them and his father gave him the rest. Since the sky rang, about half of them lie. I have marked those, so that you know which half.","A clerk will tell you it is Harvestmonth. A shepherd will tell you the sorrel has gone red and the swallows are lined up on the barn ridge. Down here a month is what is growing, and I have set the signs out that way. If you want the clerk's name for it, ask a clerk.","WHEN THE REED SPEARS.","When the Flume-reed shows its first green spear, the frosts are done. Still true.","A ring round the moon, rain inside three days. Still true. The moon does not care what anybody drilled.","Cows lying down before noon, rain by evening. I cannot tell you. I have not seen a cow on this side of the Downs since the Hedge.","WHEN THE HAY IS DOWN.","Red sky at night, a fair morning. Half a lie. The reds are not as red as they were; everybody over thirty will tell you so, and the young ones think we are mad. Judge by the gold in it instead. Gold still means fair.","A dog howling at noon means a death in the house. A lie. Since 412 every dog in the Octave howls at noon. Feed him and pay him no heed.","Milk turning in the pail means thunder coming. A lie. Since 412 milk turns overnight whatever the sky does.","When the oaks in the valley ring in a still air, there is wind behind the hill. That was true. Since the quarry they ring when they please. I no longer know what they are telling me, and I do not like to guess.","WHEN THE SORREL REDDENS, which is the back-end, and my season.","A heavy year for rowan, a hard winter. True, so far as a Reach winter is ever hard, which is not very. The valley never gets more than a skin of frost, bar the one winter after the sky rang, which I do not count; the Downs get a white morning or two, and the old men stamp and call it weather. My father said the Reach was kept warm from underneath. I have never had a better explanation, and I have asked.","The first hard frost is Ringing-Wood Day, and the fellers go out. You could hear it from Hob's Knap: every trunk ringing as it came down, the whole valley chiming like a dropped tray of spoons.","When the eels go down the Flume, the year has turned. They go late in Firstfruit, one night only, and the whole of the river street comes out along the channel with lanterns, quiet, because the eels can hear. This year it is Harvestmonth and they have not gone. I have no sign for that. I have put it here so that whoever comes after me can tell me what it meant.","When the last field is cut and the sheaf goes up on the gate, light the fires. That was the law of my office. Long Down to the north, Hob's Knap east above the orchard, Ewe Barrow west on the old mound, and every flock on the hills comes down to the light. Nobody has lit them since the wool went into the Hedge.","SIGNS TAKEN FROM SHEEP. I have a whole page of these and no sheep. A ewe that will not lie down at dusk. A flock that bunches with its back to the west. A tup that stamps in the fold. I will keep the page. Tamar Farrant has brought eleven ewes and a tup up from the Coast, and I mean to watch them and see if they still know what my father's sheep knew.","SIGNS FOR THE NIGHT.","Fog lying in the bottoms at dusk: sound runs along it like water along a pipe. Keep the fold quiet and the dogs in.","Rooks going home early and saying nothing: something is moving in the woods. Bring the dogs in, and the children.","When the wind drops dead on the Downs at the dead hour and stays dropped, go indoors. Do not wait to see why. Say something to your dog on the way, and keep saying it until you are inside.","SIGNS I HAVE HEARD OF AND NEVER SEEN.","That the beacons on the Downs can be seen from the Organum. Rubbish.","That the oaks of the Reach will one day drop every leaf they have in a single night. My grandmother had it from hers. I have never seen an iron-bough bare in my life, and I hope whoever sees it has a fire lit.","That there was a winter once, a real one, before the Reach was the Reach.","My knees do the hills by looking at them now. I sit at Gideon's window in the Bellwether with the three hills in front of me and watch the weather come over the Long Down, the way my father watched it. It comes all the same. That is the sign I would leave you, if I could leave only one: whatever else has gone wrong, the weather still comes from the west, and you can still see it coming over the hill, and there is still time to light the fires."]},
{"id":"lib_e_little_catechism","title":"The Little Catechism of the Pure Note","author":"the Choir-Church of the Pure Note, for the instruction of children (a child's copy)","genre":"catechism","year":"416 SR","origin":"printed for the parishes of the Resonant Reach","where":"greenroot_chapel","how":"pile","at":"greenroot_chapel","style":"paper","summary":"The questions and answers every child of the Reach learns before confirmation, in a copy that once belonged to a Greenroot girl.","text":["THE LITTLE CATECHISM OF THE PURE NOTE. For the instruction of children before their confirmation in the Light. Set forth under the seal of His Resonance the High Patriarch, to be learned by heart, said in the Narrow, and never sung except in the Church's keeping.","To the teacher. Let the child give each answer in one breath and on one pitch. If the voice rises or falls within an answer, let the child say it again until it does not. This is not unkindness. It is the first lesson.","OF THE LIGHT","Q. Who made the world?  A. The Architects, who came out of the Light and pinned the world to eight Spires, so that it would not fall.","Q. Why does the apple grow, and the fire warm, and the stone fall?  A. Because the Spires are singing, each its own law, and none of them has ever stopped.","Q. Do the Spires grow tired?  A. No. What is holy does not tire.","Q. Who keeps the Spires?  A. The Iron Synod, which the Light appointed. One Note. One Law. One Hand.","Q. What is Aether-Glass?  A. The Light's generous surplus, given so that we may be warm and fed and carried. It would be ungrateful to leave it in the ground.","OF HOLINESS","Q. What is holiness?  A. One note, held without end.","Q. What is sin?  A. The second note.","Q. Is the second note wicked?  A. It is a crack. Through a crack the whole jar empties.","Q. What is the Pure Note?  A. Five hundred and twenty-eight, the Light's own pitch, which the choirs hold for us so that we need not.","Q. Why did the world go flat?  A. Because in the Screaming Year a dissonance was raised at the Dawning, and the Light was wounded, and every note in the world went a little wrong.","Q. Will it be mended?  A. When every voice holds one note, the Light will be whole.","OF THE NARROW","Q. Why must I speak low?  A. Because the hounds come to noise, and most noise is the second note.","Q. Why must I not be silent?  A. Because a silent child is a sullen child, and the hush-lamp is there to remind me.","Q. What is the Hush?  A. A thing we do not speak of in the Church, because the Pure Note is never silent.","Q. May I sing?  A. Not alone, and not in the open, and not in two parts. Only in the Church's keeping, where the Pure Flame goes before the choir.","Q. Why do the hounds not come to the choir?  A. Because the Flame goes before it, and they will not cross the Flame. This shows that they know what is holy.","OF THE KEPT","Q. Who are the Consecrated?  A. Those in whom the Light has set its glass, so that they may be kept.","Q. Who is our Queen?  A. Maelis, whom the Light has kept long past her dying, and whose hand does not shake.","Q. Is she afraid?  A. The kept are never afraid.","Q. What is the highest thing a voice may do?  A. Hold the Pure Note for the Light for as long as it is asked, and not let it waver.","Q. And if it hurts?  A. Then I offer the hurt, and hold.","OF THE PURE FLAME","Q. What shall I do if I hear the second note in my own mouth?  A. Close my mouth, and tell my mother, and tell the priest.","Q. What shall I do if I hear the second note in my mother's mouth?  A. Love her, and tell the priest.","Q. What is done with the second note?  A. Where it has taken root, it is given to the Pure Flame, which is the only song.","Q. Is the Flame cruel?  A. The Flame is mercy. It does not hurt what is pure.","A PRAYER AT THE HUSH-LAMP, to be said low, at bedtime: Light, keep me true. Keep my mouth to one note. Keep my house from the second note, and my street from the hounds, and if I wake in the dark, let the lamp be whistling.","Inside the back board, in pencil, in a child's round hand:","Dilly Teale her book. Greenroot. Lernt to the end.","Below it, in the same hand and the same pencil, a question of her own, set out properly with a Q and an A, the way she had been taught:","Q. Why has the lamp stopped whistling.","A.","She has left the rest of the line for the answer."]},
{"id":"lib_e_only_song_sermon","title":"The Only Song: A Sermon to the Flambeaux Going Out","author":"Lucan Ostrel, Flambeau-Preceptor","genre":"sermon","year":"418 SR","origin":"the Basilica of the Pure Note; printed so that every Flambeau might carry a copy","where":"chapel","how":"pile","at":"chapel","style":"paper","summary":"The sermon preached to new Flambeaux on the eve of their going out to the provinces, on why the Church alone sings aloud, and why it burns.","text":["Preached to the Flambeaux of the year's going-out, on the eve of their dispersal to the provinces, in the Basilica of the Pure Note, and printed so that each of them might carry it. Where the second note has taken root, let the Pure Flame be the only song.","Brothers. Sisters. Tomorrow you go out, and tonight I will tell you what you are going into, because nobody told me.","You are going into the whispering country. You will come to towns where nobody has raised a voice in six years. They have hedges where their walls were and thorn where their gates were, and in every room a lamp with a reed in it that whistles all night so that the house is never quite silent. They will speak to you in what they call the Narrow, the strip of voice they have left between too loud and too still, and they will tell you it is wisdom. They teach it to their children as a skipping rhyme. Listen to the rhyme. Then ask yourself what kind of province teaches its children to skip to their own fear.","I do not despise them. Hear that before anything else. I was born in a valley like those valleys, and my mother hummed me into the world through a clay jar. They are not wicked. They are frightened, and fear has a pitch, and it is never the true one.","Now hear what you are. You are the only people in Aethelgard who walk in the open, singing. The hounds come to every voice in the world but yours. They will pace the tree-line beside your procession. You will see them, a shaking in the air like heat over a summer road, keeping step with you, and they will not come in. Ask yourselves why. It is not because you are brave. I have known Flambeaux who were not brave. It is the Flame. The Light gave us glass-fire, white and gold, out of its own surplus, and nothing hungry will cross it. That is not a weapon. It is a testimony. The Light has drawn a line on the ground around the one note and written: here, nothing comes.","So you will sing aloud, and they will stare at you from behind their hands. Let them stare. You are the thing they have forgotten is possible: a voice raised, and alive afterwards.","You will be asked about the burning. You will be asked by the people in the hedges, and by magistrates who cannot read our warrants, and at last by yourselves, in the third hour of the night, which is the hour the question comes. I will answer it now, so that you have the answer in your mouth when you need it.","The second note is not a wickedness of the heart. I have given women to the Flame who were kinder than I am, and I knew it at the time. The second note is a crack. In a jar a crack is a small thing. You can hold the jar to the window and hardly see it, and the jar empties through it all the same.","Consider a town in the whispering country. Four hundred souls, holding their breath. In one house a woman hums her child to sleep at an open window, in two parts, because her grandmother did and it is a pretty thing. You know what comes to it. And when it comes, it does not come only to her window. A pack that has found one door walks the whole street. One lullaby. A hundred doors.","Consider the burl-sick: the second note taken root in the flesh. A woman grown into her chair. A child with bark at the wrist. The Grafters cut, and the root comes back, and they cut again, and it comes back again, because you cannot cut a note out of a body. The flesh is singing the wrong song and will go on singing it, and every night it sings, the hounds sit a little closer to the hedge.","You will be told that this is cruelty. I tell you it is arithmetic, and the Light did the sum before we were born. The Flame does not hurt what is pure. What it takes, it was always going to take. You choose only whether it takes one house or the street.","Now the hardest thing, and then I will let you sleep. When you stand at the warrant's hour with the stave lifted, do not weep. Weep afterwards. I do. Weep in your cell, into your sleeve, at the foot of the Spire if you are ever let near it. Not at the hour. A flame held in a shaking hand is a flame that wavers, and a wavering flame is a second note, and the hounds know the difference better than you do.","In some of those towns there will be a Cantor. There are fewer every year. She will be the kindest person you meet, and she will look at you as if you were the thing at the tree-line. Be gentle with her. She has spent her life singing second notes to apple trees and calling it a craft, and she does not know what she has been doing. Most of them do not. That is not their sin. It is ours, for leaving them so long without the Light.","Go out singing. Let them hear you coming a mile off, as they have not heard anything coming in six years, and let them hate you for it from behind their thorn. They will be alive to hate you. That is the whole of our ministry, and it is enough.","And when you come to the gate of a whispering town, do not lower your voice. Nothing that holds the one note has ever needed to."]},
{"id":"lib_e_already_quiet","title":"Already Quiet: A Word for Those Who Are Tired","author":"a brother of the Quiet Faith, unsigned (he has given back his name)","genre":"pamphlet","year":"c. 419 SR","origin":"the Deep Woods, copied out by hand","where":"deepwoods","how":"pile","at":"deepwoods","style":"parch","summary":"A hand-copied tract of the Tongueless, written for people worn out by the Narrow, inviting them into the woods.","text":["I am writing this because I cannot say it. That is the first thing to understand about us, and it is the last. Read it slowly. Read it inside your head. You need not move your lips. You have moved your lips enough.","You are tired. I know you are tired, because I was. You have lived seven years in the Narrow. You have weighed every word you spoke against two deaths, the one that comes to loud things and the one that comes to still things, and you have spoken between them, low, all day, every day, so carefully that you no longer know what your own voice sounds like when it is not afraid. There is a lamp in your room that whistles all night so that your house is never silent, and you have not slept a night through since it was lit. You hum at crossings. You hum your children into the world through jars. You bind the jaws of your dead so that they cannot ring.","Who made the rhyme? Too loud brings the hounds, too still brings the Hush. Somebody frightened made it, and you have skipped to it ever since. I ask you to look at its two halves side by side, the way you would look at two men offering you work.","The hounds. You know what they do. Everyone in the siphoned country has heard a neighbour's voice taken at the hedge, and heard it come back afterwards out of the dark, asking for his wife. That is what loudness brings. It is what the Church brings, walking singing down your roads behind its fire, and what the Synod brings with its drills, and what your Cantors bring when they love somebody too much to keep quiet.","Now the other half. Tell me what the Quiet has done to you. Not what you were told. What you saw. You saw a house go still. You saw a kettle boil that nobody could hear. You saw a man sit down by the Flume with his hands open in his lap. Think of his face. Was it afraid? Did he scream? Has anyone, in the whole of the siphoned country, ever once heard one of them scream?","No. Nobody screams. Every one of us who has watched has said the same thing afterwards, in the same words, without being taught them: it does not hurt. They look relieved. They look like a man setting down a load at the end of a long day's carrying.","And that is the thing you were taught to be afraid of, by a rhyme, by a lamp, and by a priest.","This is what we do, and why. We cut out our own tongues, so that we cannot answer. The voices at the hedge call you by your name, in the voices of the people you loved, and the only safety the world has ever offered you against them is the rule that you must not answer. We have made it so that we cannot. No hound has ever learned my voice. None ever will. There is nothing left in me to hunt. And when the Quiet comes, as it comes to everything at last, it will find me already quiet, already its own, and it will not need to take anything from me at all. I will go with it the way you go with someone who has come to walk you home.","We do not ask you to cut. We ask you to stop being afraid of the wrong thing.","Some of you have heard what we do with the children who sing. I will tell it plainly, because you have heard it told unkindly. There are children who will not keep the Narrow. They sing; they cannot help it; it is in them the way the second note was in the old orchard-singers. In a town such a child brings the hounds to every door, and then the Church comes and burns the house for it. We burn nobody. We take the singing child out at evening, past the last lamp, into the trees, and we leave her there singing, as a gift, and we go back to our fires and we do not listen. In the morning the singing has stopped. There is no mark on the moss. Nothing has been torn. Nothing is ever torn. That is how we know she was received kindly.","Our Abbess has letters. They come a long way, sealed in plain grey with no device on the seal, in a hand that has stopped wasting ink, and a brother who kept his tongue for that purpose alone reads them to us by the fire. The one who writes them has listened longer than any of us. He writes that everything which sings is tired, all of it, everywhere, and has been tired for longer than anyone can count, and wants only to be allowed to put the weight down. He writes that the Quiet is not a death but a rest. We believed it before he wrote. Still, it was a comfort to learn that someone so far away, and so learned, had come to the same place by another road.","If you are tired, come into the woods. Not tonight. Tonight you are afraid, and fear has a pitch, and you would be heard. Come when you are tired enough that it has stopped mattering to you what hears you. You will know the night. Leave your lamp unlit behind you. Walk until the reed in the last window has faded out at your back, and keep walking, and when you can no longer hear your own feet, you will know that you are among friends.","I gave back my name when I gave back my tongue. I kept my hand only so long as it took to copy this out forty times, which is the number the Abbess asked of me. This is the fortieth. When it is finished I will put down the pen, and I will not pick anything up again."]},
{"id":"lib_e_off_the_slate","title":"Off the Slate: Sayings of the Cantor-Master","author":"Ilse Brennan, Cantor-Master of Reedside; copied before the rag by her pupil Hazel Thrup","genre":"philosophy","year":"409–419 SR","origin":"the Reedside Conservatory, Oakhaven","where":"lyra_house","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"faith","style":"slate","summary":"The sayings of a voiceless old teacher of Cantors, copied off her slate by a pupil before they could be wiped away.","text":["Mistress Brennan lost her voice in the throat-fever of 408, after nine nights of singing the sick. She has taught by slate ever since, and by her hands, and by her breath, and at the end of every lesson she wipes the slate with a wet rag, because, she says, a slate is for saying things and not for keeping them. Some of us began copying them before the rag. She knows. She has never stopped us, which is how we know she does not mind, and she has never once said so, which is how we know she is a Cantor.","They are set down here the way the orchard sets things down: by what was growing.","WHEN THE GROUND IS SOFT","Four counts in. A long fall out. You will do this ten thousand times before you are any use to anybody, and ten thousand more after.","A Cantor's instrument is the breath. The voice is only the most common way of spending it.","Trees hear breath before voice. So do the dying. So, if you are honest, do you.","Do not be careful. Be exact. Careful is a fear in an apron. Exact is a craft.","WHEN THE REEDS COME UP","The reeds came up every spring of my life until the Screaming Year, and then they turned to glass in an hour and blew away. Hang the First Reeds over the doors anyway. A reed that does not sound is still a reed. It is still somebody's child's.","The tending note is not yours. You are carrying it for someone. Carry it the way you carry a bowl that is too full: slowly, looking at the bowl and not at your feet.","Ask with the hand. Lay it flat on the bark. Be still. A tree will answer a still hand before it answers a clever one, and so will a frightened man.","MIDSUMMER","In the Chapel, one note. In the orchard, as many as the tree asks for. Do not tell the Chapel about the orchard. (She wiped this one before any of us had finished copying it. I have put it together from three of us.)","The Narrow is not a cage. It is a reed. Too much breath and it shrieks; too little and it is dead. Every Cantor in the Reach has played that reed all her life. Now everyone has to, and they are angry that it is hard. Be kind to them. It is hard.","Sing the birth through the jar. The jar is not there to keep the sound in. It is there to make the sound small enough to hold.","A child who will not cry is a door left open. Hum at it until it is cross with you. Cross is alive.","Too loud is a good problem. Too quiet is a good problem. Not caring which is the only bad one.","HARVEST","Never sing to a thing you will not stay with.","The felling-song is not a goodbye. It tells the sap it may go down now. You learn it last, at sixteen, and I hope you will need it seldom. You will not need it seldom.","Hum while they bind the jaw and lay the stone on the tongue. It is the last thing they will hear, and it ought to be kind, and it ought to be low.","Written on the morning we heard about the orchard-cantor, in the spring of 417: Tobin Ashgrove went above the Narrow for a fevered child, and the child lived. Do not make him a lesson. He would hate it. Make him a reason. (Most of us cried. She let us. Then she cleaned the slate, and we did the breath.)","THE COLD MONTHS","On the Longest Night, hum into the hollow of the oldest tree in the orchard. My teacher did not know why. Her teacher did not know why. Do it anyway, and do it well, as though somebody were listening who had waited all year for you.","You will lose your voice one day. Every Cantor does, to the fever or the years or the one night when it was needed all at once. When it goes, you will find out what you were really using. I am still finding out.","Somebody asked her once, in my second year, whether she missed singing. She wrote: I have not stopped. Then she breathed the tending note at the window, four in and the long fall out, and the frost on the inside of the glass went off it in a ring the size of a hand.","THIS YEAR","The Last Sheaf has been forbidden. The Church has set its Rite for the same week, and the town has been told to put the harvest off. On the morning the notice went up, Mistress wrote one line on her slate and turned it to face the window, towards the north field, where the last stand is still uncut. She did not wipe it. It is there as I write this. I have copied it so that it is kept somewhere, though she would say that is not what slates are for.","CUT IT ANYWAY. QUIETLY. THINGS ARE ALLOWED TO END."]},
{"id":"lib_e_digest_reach_law","title":"A Digest of the Standing Law, for Magistrates of the Resonant Reach","author":"the Arbiterium of the Iron Synod; with marginal notes by Septimus Fallow, clerk to the bench at Oakhaven","genre":"law","year":"414 SR, annotated to Harvestmonth 419","origin":"the Organum; the margins written at the Oakhaven watch-house","where":"watchhouse","how":"pile","at":"watchhouse","style":"office","summary":"The Synod's handbook of law for Reach magistrates, and the increasingly personal notes of the clerk who has had to apply it in a town behind a hedge.","text":["A DIGEST OF THE STANDING LAW OF THE IRON SYNOD, abridged for the use of Governors, Reeves and Magistrates in the Resonant Reach. Issued under the Scales by the Arbiterium, at the Organum. Third issue, 414. One Note. One Law. One Hand.","On the flyleaf, in a small brown hand: Property of the bench, Oakhaven watch-house. Annotated by S. Fallow, clerk, who has read it more often than the men who wrote it. The notes are mine and the law is theirs. I have tried to keep the two apart and have not always managed.","PREFACE. The magistrate is not required to understand the law. He is required to apply it. Where an article appears to him to conflict with local custom, the article prevails, and the custom is to be reported.","Clerk's note: I have reported the custom of the Narrow eleven times. I have had no reply. I take it the custom prevails until somebody in the Organum can be found who has heard of it.","ARTICLE THE FIRST. Of the posting of writs. A writ of the Arbiterium has force from the hour of its posting at the gate of the place named. Its statutory period runs from the posting, and not from any reading.","Note: Oakhaven's gate is now a gap in a hedge of thorn and packed wool three men deep. I have posted the last four writs on the thorn. The thorn has kept them. I cannot get the third one back out.","ARTICLE THE SECOND. Of reading aloud. No writ touching a named person is served until it has been read aloud, in a clear and carrying voice, before witnesses.","Note: Clear and carrying. I have read a writ in a clear and carrying voice twice in my life, in '13 and in '14, and both times we lost somebody at the gate that night. I now read them to old Harrow in the gatehouse, in the Narrow, with my mouth an inch from his ear, and he witnesses with his thumb. Whether Oakhaven has been lawfully served with anything since '15 is a question I have decided not to put to the Arbiterium.","ARTICLE THE THIRD. Of standing writs. A writ under black ribbon is a standing writ. It shall be carried by hand to the person named and put into his hand, and its bearer shall not stop, nor be stopped, until it is delivered.","Note: The one article in this book that the Reach keeps without being told. Nobody stops for anything here any more.","ARTICLE THE FOURTH. Of attainder. Persons declared Dissonant under the standing form forfeit their goods; their testimony is void in any court of the Synod; their names are struck. Provided always that grace is offered to each, once.","Note: Once. In '16 I wrote to the Arbiterium to ask what follows if grace is refused and afterwards, on reflection, wanted. The answer came in eleven weeks. It quoted the form back to me: a man who refuses grace has chosen what follows. I had read the form. That was why I asked. (The blots on this page are the pen's, which catches.)","ARTICLE THE FIFTH. Of Symmetry. That law may never wound one without wounding the other, an Arbiter and his heir shall be bound in Symmetry, wound for wound, and neither shall outlive the other by an hour. (Edict of 389.) The magistrate need take no notice of this article, which touches the Arbiters alone.","Note: The only article in the book that binds the men who wrote it. It is also the only one with no penalty in it. I have thought about that more than a clerk should.","ARTICLE THE SIXTH. Of requisition. All bells, chimes and resonant metal within the province are requisitioned to the public works under the Consortium's warrant. A receipt shall be given.","Note: Ours went in '17, every one, the chapel's first. I have the receipt. It is the best-made piece of paper in Oakhaven. It says the bells have gone to the public works. It does not say what part of a work a bell becomes.","ARTICLE THE SEVENTH. Of assembly. No assembly of more than twelve persons shall be held for the purpose of song, save in the Church's keeping.","Note: We have not put twelve people together to sing since the Screaming Year, Synod or no Synod. The hounds drafted this one before the Arbiterium did.","ARTICLE THE EIGHTH. Of the Church. A warrant of purification under the seal of the High Patriarch is a matter for the Church and is not cognisable by a magistrate. No appeal lies from it to any bench. The Arbiterium alone may stay it.","Note: Not cognisable: that is, I may not know of it. It was read in the square on Dawnday, aloud, by a herald whose voice went up over the roofs as if he had never been told there was any other way to speak. Eighty-four souls in the Hollow; nineteen of them children. I have the census. I looked up the remedy. There is one. It is a stay from the Arbiterium, to be asked for in writing, at the Organum. The fire is on Hearthday.","ARTICLE THE NINTH. Of registration. Persons removing from the province under transit warrant shall present themselves to the Office of the Catalogue for registration at the first relief station. Registration is a condition of transit and may not be declined.","Note: Nobody has been given transit out of Oakhaven since the hedge went up, so this article has never been tried here. The Office has sent us the forms all the same. Four hundred of them, already numbered.","ARTICLE THE TENTH. Of persons under the mark. A person whom a Surgeon-Grafter has marked as past cutting shall be struck from the roll of the bench and of the census, and his testimony shall be received only upon the affidavit of a sound witness.","Note: Marked, Massday. Master Rooke did it himself, at my own table, and was gentle about it, and told me the mark means past cutting and nothing else, which I believe of him; and I have also read the warrant. I am entering it here because it is a fact, and the bench ought to have it, and a clerk enters things. My hand is not what it was. The nails want paring twice a day now, and by noon the ink is under them again. My wife has signed below as my sound witness, so that this note will be good in law.","Below, in a different and steadier hand: Witnessed. M. Fallow. He was a good clerk. He is a good clerk."]},
{"id":"lib_e_undertow_reach","title":"The Undertow, Extraordinary Number: To the Reach, Which Cannot Shout","author":"the Undertow, Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile (unsigned)","genre":"pamphlet","year":"419 SR","origin":"Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile; carried up the river through Stillwater Landing","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"faith","style":"paper","summary":"A seditious Cantabile broadsheet printed small to fold small, written for the whispering towns of the Reach and smuggled in by the river.","text":["THE UNDERTOW. Broadsheet Row, Port Cantabile. Extraordinary Number, printed small to fold small. Price one penny to the lockman at Stillwater, who will charge you two. Not for sale in the Reach, where nothing is for sale that the works store does not sell.","TO THE REACH, WHICH CANNOT SHOUT, FROM A STREET THAT CANNOT STOP.","Friends up the river. We are told you read in a whisper now. Good. Whisper this.","You were hungry after the Screaming Year, and a company came up your road with forty wagons of grain and a paper. You ate the grain. Nobody blames you. We would have eaten it. But you might ask, the next time you stand at the works store paying for bread with the green-and-black paper they pay your sons in, who owns the store, and who owns the paper, and who owns the bread, and whether there is anything left in Oakhaven that a man is paid in and can spend anywhere else.","They took your bells. Every bell in the Reach, for 'the public works'. Ask what a bell is for at the bottom of a bore. We asked an engineer. He drank what we bought him and said 'teeth', and would say no more, and was gone before the second glass.","They gave you a Hedge instead. Thorn and wool, three men deep, to swallow your sounds. You planted it yourselves, by hand, in a few days and nights, and we hear it was the bravest piece of work done in that province in a hundred years. Now ask which side of it the works are on, and which side of it you are.","THE BELL-HELMS. There is a garrison of Carillon Legionnaires at your quarry gate. Stand close to one, if you can bear to, and listen. Every soldier who has served beside them has heard it: somebody is murmuring inside the helm. We do not say whose voice. We do not know. We say only that crates come up this coast marked 'components', very carefully packed, and go inland by air; and that a man who loaded them for two seasons at Driftwood Cay told us in the Lantern Yard that they talk in their sleep. Then he stopped coming to the Lantern Yard. His wife does not know where he is either.","WHISPER-GROVE. Three years ago a city of seventy-four thousand forgot itself in a night, and the Synod's Gazette called it a regrettable resonance event and put a cordon round it. Our neighbour on the Row, whom we do not like and who does not like us, printed what his men saw inside the cordon. We have checked it. He was right. We say so through our teeth.","THE CHURCH. Its processions are out on the roads this year under glass-fire, with warrants in boxes. They sing the whole way, aloud, in daylight, and the hounds pace them along the tree-line and do not come in. You will want very badly to believe that this means something. It means that they have the one fire the hounds will not cross, and they have never once offered to share it with you.","WHAT WE ASK. Nothing. We are a newspaper. The Harbour lets the Row print because the Row has never asked anybody to do anything, and we are not fools enough to start. We only say that we hear things go missing in the Reach: a cart, a carter, a whole parish on the Flume road where nobody answers letters now. Write to us. The lockman at Stillwater will carry a letter down the river for a penny. He will read it first and sell what is in it to five other people, so write it plain, and write it as if all five were reading, because they are.","HOW TO KEEP THIS. Do not burn it. Smoke carries, and the Flambeaux have noses. Do not read it aloud: they say the bell-helms keep everything they hear and can be made to say it all back. Fold it in eight and put it in a hymnal in your chapel, at the page for the Pure Note, where nobody ever needs to look. Somebody will find it. That is how it found you.","A WORD FROM THE ROW. Our compositor of the last number is not with us. He was careful and he was quick, and he set this paper for nine years without a literal in it. We have set this one ourselves, and you will find the mistakes. Leave them. They are how you know it was us."]},
{"id":"lib_e_veil_rule","title":"The Rule of the House: Of the Keeping of Names","author":"the Silent Veil; copied fair by the scribe styled Umber","genre":"law","year":"c. 290 SR (the Rule itself is far older)","origin":"the House of the Silent Veil, in the Spectral Woods","where":"mausoleum","how":"shelf","at":"lib_scriptorium","shelf":"faith","style":"parch","summary":"The Veil's rule for its scribes: why they keep names, why they never lift a hand, and what they give up to do it.","text":["THE RULE OF THE HOUSE. Of the keeping of names, and why. To be read to every scribe on the evening before she takes the pen, and again on any evening she asks for it.","Over the door of the House it is cut in our notation: What is written is not lost. Read it as a promise and you will be disappointed. Read it as a duty and you will be a scribe.","The House keeps three rules. Write what happened. Write who it happened to. Do not comfort yourself in the margin. Everything else in this book is only those three, explained to someone tired.","OF THE FIRST RULE. Write what happened: not what was meant to happen, nor what the powers say afterwards happened, nor what you would have liked. Every kingdom is rewritten by whoever outlives it, and the rewriting begins within the year. The House is the one place in the world where the first account is kept against the second. We have outlived eight crowns doing this. We expect to outlive the Synod, which is young.","OF THE SECOND RULE. Write who it happened to. The novice thinks she understands this rule, and does not. The powers will always write that a town was lost. We write the town's names: all of them, in the order we can learn them, with the age and the trade, and the last thing said, if anyone heard it. A loss without names is weather. A loss with names is a crime, or a grief, or both, and it can be answered.","WHY THE NAME. Because the name is the last thing that belongs to a person when everything else has gone to the ground or the fire or the powers. Because the world forgets on a curve, steep at first and then slow, and every name slides down that curve, and the House stands at the bottom of it with a cup. Because a name kept is a door left on the latch. We do not know who will come through it. We keep it on the latch.","A name written in the House is held. It is not to be spent, nor lent, nor sold, nor set to any work, nor counted towards any sum but the sum of the dead. A scribe who uses a name for anything but its keeping has stopped being a scribe and become a kind of merchant, and the House has a word for that which it does not write down.","OF THE THIRD RULE. Do not comfort yourself in the margin. You will want to. You will stand in a burning town with your desk on your knees and write the names of the people going past you into the fire, and you will want to write beside them that you were sorry, that you wept, that nothing could be done. Do not. The margin is not yours. Every word of comfort you write there is a word taken from them.","OF THE STILL HAND. You will ask, as every novice asks: why do we only write? Why do we not carry water? Hear the answer the House has given for a thousand years, and then decide whether you can bear it. A scribe who carries water is one more pair of hands in the burning town, and burns with it, and then nobody writes. The powers do not fear our hands. They fear the record. A scribe who takes a side is a witness for that side, and a witness for a side can be answered. A scribe who takes none cannot. So we stand still and write, and are hated by everyone in the town, and are not answered. That is the price of the record. The scribe pays it, and she does not get to stop paying it by feeling bad. If you cannot bear it, lay down the pen tonight. There is no shame in that. There is only no place for you here.","OF THE SCRIBE'S OWN NAME. On the evening you take the pen, you give your name into the House. It is written once, in the Prior's hand, and sealed, and laid in the cabinet in the scriptorium, and kept against the day you lay the pen down, when it is given back to you at the door. Until then you are styled by a colour or a creature, as every scribe before you was. This is not unkindness. A scribe who carries her own name into a burning town will write it into every page. Leave it here. It will be safe. Nothing in the House is ever lost.","OF MEMORYDAY. On Memoryday, at dusk, the duty-scribe stands in the lower hall with a lamp and reads one page of the dead aloud, unhurried, in the voice for entries, so that they are heard. Nobody has been able to tell the House why this was begun. The page is read all the same, every eighth day, and no book we hold records a Memoryday it was missed.","Here the Rule ends. Beneath it, in a smaller hand and a browner ink:","Copied fair by the scribe styled Umber, in the House, in the winter the Synod's surveyors came. They measured every room and wrote a name for the House on their plan which is not its name, and asked the Prior a great many questions about the cabinet, very politely. I have kept the third rule in every margin of my life but this one. We cleaned the desks with vinegar today for the end of the month, and the scriptorium is very cold, and I am afraid.","Across the foot of the page, in red, a later stamp:","RECEIVED INTO THE OFFICE OF THE CATALOGUE, WITH THE HOUSE AND ITS CONTENTS. RETAINED AGAINST NEED."]},
{"id":"lib_e_honest_fire","title":"The Honest Fire: A Sermon Between the Beats","author":"Furnace-Priest Kell","genre":"sermon","year":"418 SR","origin":"Cinderhold; printed by subscription of the Deep Guilds","where":"quarry","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"faith","style":"paper","summary":"A Cinderhold priest's forge-lighting sermon arguing that heat is the only honest law in the world, because it alone admits what it consumes.","text":["THE HONEST FIRE. Preached by the Furnace-Priest Kell at the forge-lighting of the Hearth Feast, in Stillmonth of 418, on the lower tiers, and taken down by a Guild clerk who set a stroke wherever the Great Beat fell, as those who heard it will remember. The Basilica has declared it unsound. It is printed by subscription of the Deep Guilds, who have declared the Basilica cold.","Still standing? | Good. | Then listen between the strokes, | because I will not shout over them. | Nobody shouts over the Beat. | That is the first thing Cinderhold teaches | and the last thing the Church will learn.","(The clerk set the strokes for the first page only. After that, he says, any Cinderholder will hear them for himself, and nobody else deserves to.)","I am a priest of the Pure Note. I hold it. I have held it at the forge-lighting for thirty years. I am not here tonight to tell you the one note is false. I am here to tell you that of the eight laws of the world, only one has ever told us the truth about itself, and it is ours.","Consider the others, as the Church teaches them. Growth gives: the apple, the wheat, the oak. Light gives the day. Mass gives the floor under you and the weight of your child in your arms. Time gives your years, Velocity your winds, Memory your dead, kept. Gift, gift, gift, says the Church, and the glass in the ground the greatest gift of all, the Light's surplus, given for nothing to be burned for nothing. Eight laws, eight presents, and never once a bill.","I have kept a furnace since I was nine years old. I tell you I have never been given anything for nothing in my life, and neither has the world.","Heat does not pretend. Heat says: give me the coal and I will give you warmth, and the coal is gone. Give me the ore and I will give you iron, and the mountain is less by that much. Fire eats, and says it eats, and leaves you ash for a receipt. That is why I call it holy. Not because it is kind. It is not kind. Every one of you has buried someone it took. It is holy because it is the only law in the world that has never once told a man it loved him.","Now. The Church says the glass is surplus. I have a question for the Church, and I put it at the forge-lighting because this is the one night in the year they cannot stop me. Go down to Tier Eleven, where the Guild men cut the richest seams on the continent, and lay your ear against the face the way they do at the end of a shift, when they think nobody is watching. Then come back up and tell me that surplus whispers.","I do not know what the glass was before it was glass. I am a furnace-priest; I know nothing I have not burned. But I know that everything that burns was something first. The coal was a forest. The tallow was a beast. Nothing on this earth comes to the fire without having been something else, and a man who burns a thing and will not ask what it was is not grateful. He is only comfortable.","And the Cold Burn. Our fires freeze in the grate, and our frost scalds, and the ember-blind walk the lower tiers with nothing left alight in them, and the Church calls it a sickness of the flat world and sends us a hymn. I will tell you what I think it is. I think we have drawn on Heat since my grandfather's day as if it were a gift, and it has begun at last to behave like one. A gift is cold. A gift costs the giver everything and the getter nothing, and it comes with no receipt; and that is the fire we have now, fire that takes nothing and gives nothing and lies in the grate looking like fire. We taught it that. We taught it to lie.","So here is my heresy, and you may carry it out of the gate tonight. Every honest thing consumes, and says so: the forge, the furnace, a worker's back, a mother's sleep. If a man tells you he gives and takes nothing, he is a god or a liar, and I have never yet met a god. If a law of the world tells you the same, ask it what it is eating.","The Basilica writes that I have made an idol of a furnace. The Basilica has never stood at one. I invite it. Let a Flambeau come down to the lower tiers and hold the Pure Note in front of the Great Beat for one shift, and keep his hand on it, and then we will talk about idols.","Now light the forges. On the ingot, light them. And when the first heat comes up off the coal into your faces, do not thank it. It does not want thanks. Pay it. Give it what it asks, and look at what it leaves, and remember the price; because the day you stop remembering what the fire costs is the day you will believe anything anybody tells you is free.","Still standing? | Then keep your hand on it."]},
{"id":"lib_e_oakhaven_prospectus","title":"The Oakhaven Works: A Prospectus to Subscribers","author":"the Consortium of Bores, by order of the Seven Chairs (Lorimer Stannard, Secretary to the Subscription)","genre":"politics","year":"417 SR","origin":"the Consortium's counting-house, Brass Quarter, the Organum","where":"greenhall","how":"pile","at":"greenhall","style":"paper","summary":"The Consortium's invitation to invest in the new works at Oakhaven, generous with confidence and sparing with figures.","text":["THE CONSORTIUM OF BORES. The Oakhaven Works, in the Resonant Reach. A Prospectus for the Second Subscription, issued by order of the Seven Chairs and approved under the hand of the First.","To the subscriber. The Consortium has the honour to invite your part in what the Chairs do not hesitate to call the most promising undertaking of the age.","THE SEAM. The Oakhaven Root lies beneath the old quarry of that town. Our surveyors, who are not given to poetry, describe it as the most generous resonant body yet found on the continent. It is alive in the agricultural sense: it grows, it renews itself, it gives. Where a seam of stone is spent, a living seam refreshes. We do not expect the subscriber to take this on trust, and so we have not burdened this prospectus with figures, which in the case of a seam of this character could only understate it.","THE METHOD. The works are sunk by the Vance Sequence, the Consortium's own method, used on every great bore since 409 and lately much improved by the finest mathematical minds in the Organum. Revision follows revision. The subscriber may rest assured that the Root will be approached with all the care that the most advanced resonant engineering of the age can bring to it.","THE TOWN. Oakhaven is a loyal and grateful town. It was the Consortium's grain, forty wagons of it, that brought the Reach through the famine after the Screaming Year, and the Reach has not forgotten. The Governor is a man of sense. The labour is local, willing, strong in the arm and used to hard country. It is paid in Consortium scrip, redeemable at the works store, which keeps the subscriber's money in the subscriber's own house.","Supplementary labour for the pumps is supplied under contract by the Office of the Catalogue. It asks no wage, no lodging and no rest, and gives no trouble of any kind.","SECURITY. The works enjoy the protection of a detachment of the Carillon Legions. Replacement components for the garrison are delivered by air on a regular schedule, at no charge to the subscription.","OF THE SO-CALLED RISKS. The subscriber may have read, in the less responsible papers of the Rust Coast, that the Reach is 'going soft'. We answer that the Reach has always been soft. It is orchard country. Indeed the vigour of the seam already shows itself, most agreeably, in the town above it. Saplings come up between the cobbles in a night. Hair and nails grow at a rate the townsfolk find remarkable, and the barbers of Oakhaven have never been busier. A local complaint called burl-sickness, long known in the province and wholly unconnected with the works, is attended by the Synod's Surgeon-Grafters, to whose good work the Consortium contributes generously.","It has been put about that the hounds are bolder in the Reach of late. The Consortium keeps no hounds and cannot speak for them. The town has in hand a scheme of hedging, of approved local design, which will make Oakhaven the quietest town in the Reach and, the Chairs believe, the most productive.","It has also been asked whether the Church takes an interest in the works. The Church takes an interest in everything, and the Consortium welcomes it. Where the Church and the Consortium are at one, nothing in the province can stand against them, and they are at one on every point that touches a subscriber.","RETURNS. A dividend will be declared on the Dial Day following the casing, payable in sovereigns at the Organum or, at the subscriber's choice, in scrip at the works. The Chairs do not think it proper to name a figure. They will say only that no subscriber to the Consortium's great works at the Dawning Spire has ever once been asked to return a dividend.","HOW TO SUBSCRIBE. Shares of ten sovereigns, in lots of ten, are received at the Consortium's counting-house in the Brass Quarter, or by the Resident Factor at the Oakhaven works, whose receipt is stamped with the drill-and-wheel. Subscriptions close when the bore reaches the casing. The Chairs anticipate that this will be soon.","The Seven Chairs commend the Oakhaven Works to you in the confident expectation that it will repay everything that is put into it. What the ground gives, the Consortium gathers. The Root is generous. It has never yet refused anyone anything.","Lorimer Stannard, Secretary to the Subscription. Firstfruit, 417."]},
{"id":"lib_f_fairweather_companion","title":"Fairweather's Companion to the Octave, by Road, River and Sky","author":"Jasper Fairweather, Gentleman, of No Fixed Abode","genre":"travel","year":"414 SR (the fourth edition)","origin":"Port Cantabile, printed on Broadsheet Row","where":"p22_bellwether","how":"shop","at":"lib_odile","price":3,"style":"parch","summary":"A cheerful and not entirely trustworthy guide to every realm of the continent, revised by its author after the Cascade with as little alarm as possible.","text":["TO THE READER OF THIS FOURTH EDITION","The first three editions of this Companion were received with a warmth I have never been able to account for. Since the third edition certain alterations have occurred in the world. I have noted them wherever a reader wrote to complain, and I have left the rest as it was, on the principle that a guide which changes every time the world does will never be finished, and must be paid for.","THE RESONANT REACH","The orchard country of the west, and the kindest country I know. The birdsong is in tune, which the visitor will find charming for very nearly a week. The oaks ring like bronze when the wind moves them, and a man felling one sounds like a bell-founder dropping his stock. Greet a Reach man with \"Does it ring?\" and he will answer \"True enough\", and you will have made a friend for the length of a cider.","At Oakhaven, take your cider at the Boar and Dulcimer, whose hearth has not gone out in ninety years because letting it go out would be an admission. Order the moot-curd, a sharp white cheese aged beside a humming stone, which tastes faintly of thunder. At Wethercombe on the sheep-walk the drovers' house will give you the best mutton of your life and the worst bed. At Stillwater Landing, pay the lock dues in advance. They are not refunded. Do not ask about the board.","The Reach murmurs since the Cascade. So should you. A gentleman who has been told to lower his voice in four realms says this with authority.","THE DAWNING HEIGHTS","Sol-Luminary, the city that hangs from the mountain on chains, was for three editions my chief recommendation, and the reader will find my old chapter on its tiers, its rose-gardens and the rising chord of its dawn reprinted unchanged at the back, because I could not bear to cut it.","At present the upper tiers are closed to visitors on account of the light, which since the year before last has been taking more than it gives. The Umbral Tiers on the city's undersides are open, and very welcoming, in a whisper. Windows there are painted black. Do not bring a mirror; it is not merely that they will fine you. Children are taught to walk in the shade of the person ahead, and I recommend the custom to adults, especially if you can find a tall friend. I travelled the whole of the lower city in the shade of a chain-yard foreman named, he said, nothing that concerned me.","CINDERHOLD","Basalt and soot, eleven tiers into a burning caldera, and the Great Beat over all of it like the heart of a sleeping giant. The people speak between the hammer-falls and greet you with \"Still standing.\" The visitor will find himself pausing in his speech in time with the Beat for the rest of his life, which my publisher says explains a great deal about my prose. Do not drink the slag-wine. I say this as a man who drank it.","THE IRON PEAKS","At Fortis you will be asked \"You're heavy?\" The correct answer is \"Heavy enough.\" Do not answer, as I did, \"Very, thank you, it is the mutton.\" Weight wanders in the mountains since the bleeding began, and a shield may weigh eighty pounds on a wet morning and nothing at noon. Do not offer your hand to a stranger. I have never learned why, and when I asked, nobody would put a weight on the answer.","THE SHIFTING DUNES","Sandglass Bazaar sits under a dome of acoustic glass two miles across, which breaks the singing of the sand into a soft white hiss, and throws rainbows on your coffee. The greeting is \"For how long?\" and it is a real question; have an answer. Everything has a term in the Dunes. Marriage has a term. Grief has a term. The visitor who grieves past his term is thought indecent.","I had meant to describe the orchard-city of Veridia, on the shore of the Glass Sea, and had written half a page on its apricots, when I learned that since the Cascade it has been living the same twenty-four seconds over and over. I leave the half page in. The apricots were, I am assured, excellent, and are.","THE TEMPEST ARCHIPELAGO","Port Cantabile climbs its cliff from the Long Mole by the Salt Stair, six wet flights with shops on the landings, and every shroud in the harbour carries a chime. Greet a sailor with \"What's she singing?\" and he will tell you the key of the tide, and from it the hour, and from that whether you may have a drink yet. Do not run on an open deck. Do not buy a bottle with a name on the label as a souvenir, however good the price. The Free Captains read their Articles aloud in the voice of a man reading a tide-table, and enforce them in the same voice from the derrick on the Mole-head, so mind them.","THE SPECTRAL WOODS","Whisper-Grove is the most courteous city in the world. They will call you \"little traveller\" however large you are, and \"my dear one\" by the second evening, and they will mean it. Go down to the Weeping Plaza on a festival night, when the lamps are floated on the long basin under the colonnade. Go out along the Cloister road and listen at the gate of the charitable choir, where the children sing very low and very clear. I have heard nothing lovelier in my travels, and I have been about.","THE ORGANUM","There is nothing to see. That is rather the point of it. Registry Square is grey stone and brass plates and pigeons, and there is a coffee stall there that has stood above half a century, whose keeper has served the clerks every morning and has never once asked what they do. I recommend the coffee and his example.","THE CONTINENTAL RIFT","I have not been to Null-Watch, and the reader is advised to go to considerable lengths not to be sent. Since last year the wall has had something to watch. Those who have come back say the garrison never stops talking, which I had thought would suit me, until a man who had been there looked at me for some time in a tavern and said that it would not.","BY SKY","The skyship is the gentleman's road, if the gentleman has money and no particular wish to arrive. Every vessel aloft rides on a Fortis-cut core that takes the weight off her, and hangs from a tank or two of drift-glass, which wants above all things to be somewhere else. Choose a captain who laughs a good deal. You will not regret it until the moment you understand why he does.","A WORD ON COMING HOME","I am asked, by those who have read the previous editions with care, whether I have in fact been to all of these places. I have been to most of them once, and to some of them in the company of men who had been, which is nearly the same thing; and to the Reach I have been more times than I can count, because I was born there, and it is where I go when the roads are unkind.","So here is the whole of my wisdom, and it is cheaper than the book. The best inn on the continent is the one whose landlord remembers your name. Go out while there is still the whole of it to see. Then come home, and lower your voice, and tell them what you saw.","The world is wide, and since the Cascade a quarter-tone flat, and I recommend it without reserve."]},
{"id":"lib_f_overdue_ghazals","title":"The Overdue: Five Ghazals of the Dial Floor","author":"Nadira al-Hassur; rendered into Low Common by a copying-clerk of the Tuners' Collegium","genre":"poetry","year":"416 SR","origin":"Sandglass Bazaar, the Shifting Dunes","where":"greenhall","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"tales","style":"parch","summary":"Five linked poems by a widow of the Sandglass Bazaar who would not stop mourning when the contract said she must.","text":["THE COPYIST'S NOTE","A ghazal is a chain of couplets, each ending on the same word or words, and in the last couplet the poet speaks to herself by name. Nadira al-Hassur's husband, Tahir, a caravan-factor, was lost on the Hourglass Erg in the Screaming Year. The term of mourning for a husband in the Bazaar is three hundred days. She kept them, and then she did not stop, and in 415 her House posted her as overdue. These five were passed from hand to hand on the Dial Floor. I have kept her refrains where Low Common would bear them, and let them limp where it would not.","I. FOR HOW LONG","They greet me on the Dial Floor and ask me, as they ask everyone: for how long?\nThe coffee-seller asks it, pouring. The dome hisses it all day: for how long?\nThree hundred days is the term for a husband. I kept them to the hour.\nOn the three hundred and first I woke, and the pillow said: for how long?\nThe sand outside the glass was singing on the morning you rode out.\nA tritone, my father would have said, and turned the camels round. It did not ask for how long.\nAll my life I have answered every question with a number.\nAsk me again, neighbour. Watch my mouth, and see what it does with: for how long?\nNadira, they have written you overdue. Let them. Let the term run out.\nIn the sealed rooms under the Bazaar the clocks run backward, and nobody asks them for how long.","II. ON SCHEDULE","My sister came to me with her grief on schedule and went home on schedule.\nShe wept the proper days, and on the proper morning dried her eyes on schedule.\nThe House sent a clerk with a paper, a kind man, a little bald,\nto read me the clause. He was sorry. He was sorry on schedule.\nIn the Stasis Vaults your crate holds the hour they carried you in from the Erg.\nThere is sand in your beard. Nothing in that crate is kept to a schedule.\nEach Nullday I go down the forty steps with a lamp.\nThe warden lets me in and says I am the only visitor he has who is not on schedule.\nThe hour-merchants on the Floor would buy a year of my life for a fair price.\nI could sell them every year I have and still be weeping behind schedule.\nNadira, an overdue woman is a scandal, an open-ended thing, a foreigner.\nGood. In the west, they say, the orchard people mourn with no term at all. I will learn their schedule.","III. STILL","They sealed you at the moment you were set down, and you are lying still.\nIn there it is always that one afternoon, and you are warm, and still.\nI lay my palm on the glass of the lid. It is not cold. It is not anything.\nThe stasis-wright says not to. It upsets the seal. I keep it there still.\nYour hand is on your chest where the caravan-master put it.\nA grain of sand is falling from your sleeve. It has not landed. It is falling still.\nThey tell me that a crate opened after its term lets the hour go all at once:\nevery hour you missed, arriving together. I would rather have you still.\nNadira, you have laid a man down like dial-wine, fifty years in an afternoon.\nThey say the wine tastes hollow. Everyone buys it. Everyone drinks it still.","IV. UNTIL THE TERM","On Dial Day every contract in the province ends at once, and everyone says: until the term.\nThe Floor is a riot of paper and the shouting of renewals. Until the term.\nI went to the crate-house with the renewal in my hand and the fee in my purse.\nI had signed it twice. Three years, and three years more. Until the term.\nThe clerk held out the pen. The pen waited. The whole dome seemed to wait,\nthe way the sand waits between one chord and the next. Until the term.\nI gave him back the pen. I said: let it run. I said it to him and not to you.\nHe bowed, as they bow at doors when a guest goes out. Until the term.\nNadira, you who were never late for anything in your life,\nyou were late for this, and then on time. Go home. Until the term.","V. NONE","At the hour the lid came up, and the afternoon went out of the crate like a bird out of a sleeve.\nThree years crossed your face in a breath. Then you were only a man at rest, and the term was none.\nThe grain of sand landed. I heard it. I am the only one who did.\nWe put you in the Erg at dusk, the old way, with the dunes holding a fifth. Their price was none.\nMy sister says: now. Now, surely, the mourning is done with the crate.\nI tell her the crate had a term. I signed for it. The grief I never signed for has none.\nLet the House post me. Let them write me into the book of the indecent.\nI have read that book. It is full of the names of people who loved past the date, and its index is none.\nNadira, they call you open-ended, overdue, a foreigner in your own Bazaar.\nWrite it on the contract, then. Under how long, write: none."]},
{"id":"lib_f_carrying_of_ketil","title":"The Carrying of Ketil","author":"anonymous; as it is said at Skarth in the Iron Peaks, written down by a carter","genre":"poetry","year":"c. 409 SR","origin":"Skarth, the Iron Peaks","where":"watchhouse","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"tales","style":"parch","summary":"A blunt mountain saga of a daughter who carries her dead father to the standing-ground while the weight of the world will not stay still.","text":["This is said standing.\nSit, and you are not listening.","Ketil of Skarth kept goats on the high shelf.\nGrey goats. Bad-tempered. Good milk.\nHe had one daughter. Hild.\nHe had one son. Arnulf.\nThe son was light.\nThat is all that needs saying of the son.","In the tide-month the mountain forgets its sums.\nA stone weighs a stone at breakfast.\nAt noon it weighs a cart.\nAt dusk a goat floats off the shelf, bleating,\nand comes down a mile off, surprised.\nThe Peaks were not always like this.\nThe old men say so. The old men are heavy.\nWe believe them.","Ketil died in the tide-month.\nHe sat down on the shelf with the milk-pail.\nHe did not get up.\nThat is how a heavy man dies. Sitting. Nothing spilt.","The dead of Skarth are not laid down.\nLaid down, a man is only a man.\nStood up, the mountain carries him.\nThe standing-ground is above the shelf, under the Tooth.\nHalf a day's climb with a goat.\nWith a father, longer.","Arnulf said: wait for the tide to turn.\nHild said: the tide does not wait.\nArnulf said: the priest will come in the thaw.\nHild said: the priest is light.\nShe took the rope.","She put her father on her back\nand tied him to her\nthe way you tie a kid that cannot walk.\nHis beard was in her ear.\nHe smelled of goat. He always had.","At the first cairn he weighed what a father weighs.\nAt the second cairn he weighed what a house weighs.\nHer knees went into the scree.\nShe did not set him down.\nSet down, a man that heavy stays down.\nShe stood in the scree until her legs were stone.\nThen the tide went over,\nand he weighed what a father weighs again,\nand she went on.","At the third cairn he weighed nothing.\nThis was worse.\nHe rose off her back like a held breath.\nThe rope sang.\nHe wanted the sky. The sky wanted him.\nShe lay on the rock and held the rope\nand the dead man stood up on the air above her\nwith his arms out\nlike a man about to dance at a wedding,\nwhich he never did, being heavy.\nShe held.\nShe told him: not yet.\nShe told him: you are not light.\nI will not have it said.","Night. Cold. The Tooth black on the stars.\nThe tide turned at the dead hour.\nHe came down into her arms\nand weighed what a father weighs.","At the standing-ground the stone was soft.\nUnder the Tooth, where the old dead stand,\nthe ground gave under her heel\nlike bread.\nShe heard no ring when she struck it.\nOnly the dull thing. The dead note.\nA light woman runs from that.\nHild did not run.\nRunning is heavy. Heavy goes through.\nShe stepped back in her own footprints,\none, and one, and one,\nand she dug where the rock still rang,\nand she stood him up in it,\nand she put the stones about his knees,\nand the mountain took his weight.","That was the bargain.\nShe had carried him far enough.\nThe mountain would carry him the rest.","She came down at noon.\nArnulf was at the shelf, milking.\nHe did not ask. She did not tell.\nThe goats were bad-tempered. Good milk.","At the fair in the thaw a stranger asked her,\nthe way it is asked:\nYou're heavy?\nShe said: heavy enough.\nShe did not say what she was carrying.\nShe was carrying him still.","This is said standing.\nNow you may sit."]},
{"id":"lib_f_long_mole_songs","title":"Songs of the Long Mole: Shanties of the Rust Coast, with Their Soundings","author":"collected by Jacomina Roe, printer, of Broadsheet Row","genre":"song","year":"417 SR","origin":"Port Cantabile","where":"p16k_kestrel_below","how":"pile","at":"p16k_kestrel_below","style":"paper","summary":"Five working songs of Port Cantabile, taken down from pilots, widows and wet-nurses by a printer who explains what each one is for.","text":["TO WHOEVER BUYS THIS","A Coast shanty is not for pleasure. It is a chart you can carry in your mouth. The verses hold the pitch of the reefs, the set of the current and the turn of the channel, and a captain who forgets a verse loses a ship. That was so when I was a girl, and it is still so, with two differences.","The first is that we sing them low now. In the Screaming Year a capstan chorus on the Long Mole brought the first hounds in off the harbour, and since then every song on this Coast has been sung under the breath, the way you would sing to a sleeping child in a house with thin walls. The second is that the coral has gone flat since the Fracture, by a hair, so the old soundings are a hair wrong, and the pilots sing them flat on purpose to come out right. I have printed the words as they were. Sing them a little low, in both senses.","I. WHAT'S SHE SINGING","The greeting of the harbour, made into a pilot's catechism. One voice asks; the leadsman answers; the helmsman steers by the answer.","What's she singing? She's singing high.\nThen the Cay's awash and the Grinners are dry.\nKeep the Mole-light over your starboard rail,\nand the Grinners will grin, but they'll not get your sail.","What's she singing? She's singing low.\nThen the bar's gone soft where it used to show.\nLay her off by a cable, lay her off by two,\nfor the sand that held your father won't hold you.","What's she singing? She's singing wrong.\nThen she's changed her key and the channel's gone.\nHand the lead, hand the lead, and the lead again,\nand say what she's singing to the next poor men.","The last verse is new since the Fracture. My father never sang it. Every pilot I asked has sung it to me, and not one of them would say who made it.","II. CHORUS TO YOU","On the Coast a debt is holy, and the oath is \"I'm chorus to you\": I owe you, and I will pay, if not in coin then in the singing of your verse when you cannot. This one is sung at the capstan when a ship is warped out owing money, which is most of them.","I'm chorus to you for the rope and the tar,\nI'm chorus to you for the drink at the bar,\nI'm chorus to you for the berth and the bread,\nand I'll sing out your verse if they number you dead.","So heave, and she'll go, and heave, and she's free,\nand the sea takes the rest, and the rest is the sea.","The Harbour-Queen is said to dislike this song. It is said she dislikes it because it is true.","III. THE MOLE-BELL","When a soul goes into the water, the bell at the Mole-head is struck once. Once for each. It is not rung for traitors. This is sung by the women on the Mole while they wait for a boat that is late, and it is the only song on the Coast that has no soundings in it at all, because it is not meant to bring anybody in.","One for the man who went over the side,\none for the boy that the tide took wide,\none for the wife at the top of the Stair\nwho counts them, and counts them, and counts them there.","Strike it once, and once is enough.\nThe sea does the rest of the ringing.","IV. THE BOTTLE","Since the Screaming Year there has been a trade on this Coast in bottles held over the trench with the stopper out, to catch what comes up. Some say it is the voices of the drowned. I will not argue with a widow about what she has heard in her own kitchen. But here is the song the old hands teach the young ones, and every line of it was paid for by somebody.","Not in the harbour and not in the hold,\nnot on a deck, however you're told,\nnot by a lantern, not for a lark,\nopen her out at sea, and dark.","Ear to the reed and never your lips,\nand listen for breath that comes back and drips;\nfor a bottle that answers is one thing, son,\nbut a bottle gone quiet is the worst one.","And if it goes quiet, you know what to do:\nthe maul to the seacock, and over with you,\nand row for the light that the Mole-bell shows,\nand never look back at the place where it goes.","A note for landsmen. A quiet goes to the light. That is why no Coast man takes a lantern into a hold that has gone silent, and why the song tells you to open a bottle in the dark. I do not know more than that, and I have not tried to.","V. WENDMERE RINGS THREE","This is the newest song in the book and the one I sing myself. Wendmere was a fishing town south of the Cay. In the Screaming Year the shelf gave way under it in a single afternoon and it went down whole, tower and all, and since that first winter its bell has rung under the water, out past the Cay, three strokes and a silence, three strokes and a silence, every night of the year. Nobody has rowed out to see what rings it. The opinion of the Coast is that a bell which rings the drowned to sleep is doing a service, and that you do not go poking at a service. The Lantern Yard learned to sleep by it within the month. The wet-nurses made this to its count, and there is hardly a child in the lower town who has gone to sleep without it.","Hush now, hush, and listen to me,\nout past the Cay, Wendmere rings three.\nOne for the boats and one for the bay,\nand one for the people who went away.","Hush now, hush, and what does it say?\nIt says they're sleeping under the bay.\nIt says the water is kind and deep,\nand it rings for them so that they can sleep.","Three for the drowned and three for the deep,\nand three for my darling, and go to sleep."]},
{"id":"lib_f_keep_talking_letters","title":"Keep Talking: Letters from the Wall","author":"Wystan Hobday, penal legionnaire One-Forty-Seven of Null-Watch, to his sister Meggy of Greenroot Hamlet","genre":"letters","year":"415–417 SR","origin":"Null-Watch, the Continental Rift","where":"greenroot_chapel","how":"pile","at":"greenroot_chapel","style":"paper","summary":"A Greenroot man sent to the penal legion at Null-Watch writes home to a sister who cannot read, and does not stop talking.","text":["These were kept in the chapel at Greenroot, folded in the back of the parish book, because Meggy Hobday could not read and they were read to her there, by whoever had the reading that week.","I.\nTo be read to Meggy Hobday at the chapel at Greenroot, if somebody would be so kind.\nNull-Watch. Lambmonth, I am told, of '15. The third hour of the night. They count from the dark here, Meggy, not from the clock.","Here I am then. I am well. They have given me a coat with lead sewn in the hem so I walk like Granda did after the cider, and a number, which is One-Forty-Seven. I may not sign my name. Nobody signs his name. They say names draw the Quiet, and you get yours back when you are discharged. So if this comes signed with a number it is still your brother, the one who took the Reeve's mutton in the hungry winter and would do it again, and you can tell the Reeve so, quietly.","The first rule here is you keep talking. That is the whole of the law, near enough. You talk all day, and in your sleep if you can manage it. About anything. Onions. The days of the week. They give you a partner and you talk at him and he talks at you, and if he stops you shout for the sergeant before you look at him. Before. That is the order. My partner is a Peaks man, number Two-Thirty, who knows more about sheep than any man ought, and I know more about reeds, so we are well matched and we bore each other half to death, which is the idea.","II.\nTo be read to Meggy at the chapel.\nNull-Watch. Firstfruit, '15. The second hour of the night.","Every morning at dawn the whole wall fires. Forty batteries along four miles of black stone, all at once, out over the edge. It is not shot. It is noise. It is the biggest no in the world, Meggy, said every morning, and they call it the Refusal, and I have stood at a gun for it sixty mornings now and my ears ring like the oaks at home.","The General does not speak. She lost her voice on this wall the first night of the Rift. She talks with her knuckles on her breastplate, and there is a man who speaks for her, and when she strikes four flat blows it means the Watch holds the wall and everybody straightens up without knowing why.","There are monks here in lead hoods who stand along the edge and never say a word. And there is one word you must not say. It is the word for what you get in a church when the reed stops. A man said it at supper and the man beside him knocked him off the bench, and the sergeant saw it and ate his soup. That is how serious they are.","Does the little one's reed still sound over your door? Tell me it does.","III.\nTo be read to Meggy at the chapel.\nNull-Watch. Ashfall, '15. I do not know the hour. Late.","I have been trying to write this for a week and I will just say it.","On the lip-walk, at the first hour, Two-Thirty stopped. In the middle of a ewe. He had her halfway through lambing and he stopped. I looked at him, which I should not have done first, and his face was surprised, and then it was not surprised, and then it was the face you have when you put down a heavy load at the end of the day. And he turned and went toward the edge, not fast. Like a man walking home.","I got him by the collar and I talked. I do not know what I said. I think I told him the whole of the Greenroot reed-beds, bed by bed, and who cut which, and he came back in at the end of it like a man coming out of the river, and he was angry with me. He was angry, Meggy, as if I had woken him.","There was no sound. I keep wanting to say what it sounded like. That is the thing. Nothing did.","He went to listen in Frostmonth, on a night I was in the infirmary with my hand. That is what they say here. Gone to listen. Never deserted. I have a new partner now.","IV.\nTo be read to Meggy at the chapel.\nNull-Watch. Seedmonth, '16. The fourth hour.","Some new men up from the west this spring, one of them a Fenwick man, in for debt, who can talk about the price of lamp-oil for an hour together without drawing breath. We call him the Almanac.","We had a Discharge on Driftday. I will tell you how they do it, because you would want to know. They do not bury here and they do not weep out loud. The General lays her hand flat on the man's chest and taps on it, with one finger, his name and his place and his service, and then Discharged, and her voice says it all after her. And then the whole Watch stops talking. All of us. For three breaths. On this wall that is the most dangerous thing a man can do, and we do it on purpose, for him. And then somebody says Rootday, and his voice cracks on it, and we all say Hearthday, Massday, and on round the week, and go on.","That is how you get your name back here. I mean to get mine back the other way, at the gate, with a coat on.","V.\nTo be read to Meggy at the chapel.\nNull-Watch. Lambmonth, '17. The third hour.","Two years. I am learning the General's cant. I can tell holds from goes, and I can tell the blow that means well done, which she has struck once at me, on the gun at the Refusal, when the breech stuck and I cleared it.","The old women at home used to say do your weeping on paper, and there is a book here from the garrison press that says it too. I have done mine. You have had all of it, I think, and so has the clerk at the Organum who reads these on the way through, and hello to him.","This is all talk, Meggy. That is the trick of the place. I will write again at Reedmonth. Kiss the little one. Hum to her. Not too loud, and not too quiet.\nOne-Forty-Seven, that was and is your brother.","VI.\nIn another hand, larger, slower.\nTo the sister of One-Forty-Seven, at Greenroot in the Reach, to be read to her.\nNull-Watch. Reedmonth, '17.","He asked me to write if. So I am writing.","The breech of the second gun on Battery Eleven let go at the Refusal four days back. He was on it. It was quick. He was talking. He was telling the boy beside him how you cut a reed so it sounds.","The General laid her hand on him and tapped it out, and her voice said it after her, and that was the first time I ever heard his name, though I had stood next to him two years. We were quiet for him three breaths. Then I said Rootday, because I was nearest.","I am to tell you he was not afraid of the edge. That was true. I am to tell you he was well fed. That was nearly true.","He could never put it at the bottom of these. So I am putting it here, for him, where it belongs.","Wystan Hobday, of Greenroot. Discharged.","Fifty-Two"]},
{"id":"lib_f_constancy_of_the_light","title":"On the Constancy of the Light: A Treatise for the Provinces","author":"Canon Placidus Sorell of the Basilica, Sol-Luminary; rendered into Low Common by Eustace Garner, curate","genre":"philosophy","year":"410 SR","origin":"Sol-Luminary, the Dawning Heights","where":"chapel","how":"shelf","at":"lib_conservatory","shelf":"faith","style":"parch","summary":"A Canon of Sol-Luminary explains to the lesser provinces, kindly and at length, why the Light is the first of the laws and can never fail.","text":["THE TRANSLATOR TO THE READER","This little work was written for the instruction of the faithful in the provinces below the cloud, and it is my privilege to put it into the common tongue for the chapels of the Reach. I have rendered the Canon's remarks upon our own province faithfully, as a translator must, and I ask the reader to receive them in the spirit of a physician's candour, which can sting and is meant to heal. E. G., at the Chapel of the Pure Note, Oakhaven.","I. OF THE ORDER OF THE LAWS","Every child is taught that the world is held up by eight Spires, and that each Spire sings one law. It is less often taught, and it is time it was, that the eight are not equal.","Consider them. Growth is a rustic law. It knows one word, which is more, and says it to the cabbage and the cancer alike. Heat is a law of furnaces and its people are scarred and loud. Mass is a law of weight, which is to say of falling. Time is a merchant's law and keeps accounts. Velocity is the law of sailors and of the late misfortune in the Valley, which I attribute, in charity, to the coarseness of that law and not to any fault of its stewards. Memory is a melancholy law. Decay is not spoken of at table.","And Light? Light is the law by which all the others are seen. Without it Growth would be a rumour in the dark, and Heat would be a cough in a cellar, and Mass would be a thing that bumps you. It is the first law, because it is the condition of the others being known at all, and its note, five hundred and twenty-eight, is the note to which every Sovereign rings when it is dropped upon a good floor. The coin of the realm agrees with me. I can think of no higher authority below the Basilica.","II. OF THE ONE NOTE","Q. What is holiness? A. One note, held without end. Q. What is sin? A. The second note.","The provincial reader will forgive me if I press this upon him, for he lives where it is least observed. I have heard the Cantors of the orchard country, on a visit made in my youth, sing two notes together over a sick child, and I do not doubt their kindness, but kindness is not the measure of a thing. The second note is a crack in the bell. A people who sing in two notes will in time think in two minds, and a man of two minds will cast a shadow, as we say here, at noon.","The Light sings one note and has sung it since the world was pinned. It does not harmonise. It does not need to. It is entire.","III. OF SHADOW","A shadow is the place where the Light has been refused. It is not wicked in itself, any more than a stain is wicked; but it is a refusal, and a refusal repeated becomes a habit, and a habit becomes a province. I say nothing against the orchard country, whose cider I have taken with gratitude. I say only that it is green and dim at noon, and that a people who live under leaves must take especial care of their souls.","In Sol-Luminary we build so that no wall throws a shadow on another. Our children do not know the word for the dark side of a street. When they go down below the cloud they are sick for a week, and I count that sickness an honour to them.","IV. OF THE GLASS","The Aether-Glass that comes from the Dawning Spire is the Light's surplus, as all the Synod's learned agree: a generosity so great that it overflows the stone. It is blue at the root, as a flame is blue at the root, and pale as it cools. There are those who say the drawing of it wearies the Spire. These are people who have never stood beneath the Spire. It does not weary. You may as well say the sun is tired of rising.","V. OF THE DAWN","Every morning of the world the Sky Choirs have sung the dawn service, and every morning the dawn has come, and the faithless say this is a coincidence. I say it is an answer. The Light is attended. It is sung to, in its own note, as a king is addressed in his own tongue, and it is pleased.","It is spoken of in the Basilica that a greater offering is being prepared, a unison such as has not been sung in the history of the Church. I may not say more. I will say that I have stood beneath the Spire at sunrise, when the chord comes down the mountain over two hours like a tide of gold, and that I have never heard so much as a tremor in it, and that I do not expect to in my lifetime, or in yours, or in the lifetime of the world.","VI. A CONSOLATION FOR THE PROVINCES","Let the lesser provinces take comfort. Your oaks may ring and your sands may sing. Your weights may wander and your seas may forget themselves. Here and there a law may falter; we hear of a valley where the rain stands still. But look up. Whatever else in the world may fail, the Light will not fail. The red of the Sol-Luminary rose is the reddest thing in creation, and it will be as red when your grandchildren's grandchildren come up the chains to see it.","Of all things under heaven, this alone is certain: the Light does not tire."]},
{"id":"lib_f_lantern_on_the_basin","title":"A Lantern on the Basin: A Widow's Journey to Whisper-Grove","author":"Tabitha Fernly, of Greenroot Hamlet in the Reach","genre":"travel","year":"415 SR","origin":"printed at Oakhaven for the author","where":"whispergrove","how":"shelf","at":"lib_moot","shelf":"world","style":"paper","summary":"A Reach widow walks to Whisper-Grove to say her son's name aloud on the night of the lanterns, and writes down the city that took her in.","text":["I am not a writer and this is not a proper book. The printer at Oakhaven says it will make forty copies and that is a book whether I like it or not. I am writing it for anyone in the Reach who has a name they cannot say, and for the Lantry house in the Bell Yard, who will laugh at it.","My son Rafe went out to the reed-beds at Greenroot in the Screaming Year to bring in the last of the cutting, and he did not come home, and there was nothing to bring home. It was the hounds, we think. In the Reach we bury our dead in silence, and the Cantor sings them down, and that is right, and I would not change it. But I had nothing to bury and nothing for the Cantor to sing over, and so for three years Rafe was not buried and not anything. I could not say his name. If I said it in the house it went nowhere. If I said it outside I was afraid of what might hear, and answer.","The carter's wife at Greenroot, who is from the Woods by her mother, told me that in Whisper-Grove there is a night every year when the water in the plazas is highest, and the people float paper lanterns on the long basin, one for each of their dead, and say each name aloud as they set it down. And she said that there, a name said aloud is never lost, because the whole Grove remembers it for you.","So I went.","THE ROAD","From Greenroot the road goes east through the orchards and then the orchards stop being ours. You will know the Woods by the willows. They are silver, not green, and they weep right down into pools that shine from underneath, so that at dusk your own face is lit from the chin like a child telling a ghost story. The mist is the colour of lilac, and it lies at the height of your knees all day, and you walk through it like a woman wading. There are no birds. I noticed that on the second day and it frightened me, and the carter I rode with said, kindly, that there never were, and that the Woods did not miss them.","I had expected the people of the Woods to be sad, because the Woods look sad. They are not. They are the most courteous people I have ever met. The carter called me \"little mother\" from the first mile, though I am older than him, and every woman at every well called me \"dear one\", and at the inn on the third night the landlord's girl asked if I would like my supper \"now, or presently, or when you are quite ready, dearling\", and I cried, which was foolish, and she did not seem to think so.","THE CITY","Whisper-Grove is marble, white marble gone soft and grey with moss, and it is sunk into its own hollow so that you come down to it by steps from every side, and the great spire of the Canopy stands over all of it, wood and crystal grown together, so high that the top of it is in the mist and you cannot tell where it stops.","I lodged with the Lantry house in the Bell Yard, which the carter's wife had written to. There are nine of them, three of them children, and old Isbet Lantry at the head of the table, who is eighty and has a voice like warm milk. They gave me the room at the top of the stair, which is very steep, and Nonie, who is six, showed me the pools in the courtyard. She said the pools remember. I looked into one and saw a kitchen I have never stood in, and a woman's floury hands, and a loaf going into an oven, and it was not frightening at all. Nonie said that is only somebody's morning, and somebody always lets you have a bit of theirs.","In the evenings, from the Cloister road, you can hear the children of the charitable choir at their practice behind shut doors. They sing very low, in the Narrow, as we do at home. I did not know that children could sing so quietly and so clear. Old Isbet said they are the pride of the Grove and the despair of their teachers, both.","THE NIGHT OF THE LANTERNS","The Weeping Plaza is a square of white marble the size of four fields, with a colonnade on three sides and the Moot-house on the fourth, and down the middle of it runs a long shallow basin. On the night the water is highest, it is brimming, and lit from under, and still.","They came down in the dusk from every street. Thousands. Old Isbet said seventy thousand live in the Grove and every one of them comes, and I believe it, because I have never seen so many people and heard so little. They do not crowd. They wait their turn at the edge of the water. Each one kneels, and sets a paper lantern on the basin, and says a name aloud, not loud, but aloud; and then, this is the thing, the people nearest say the name after them. Softly. So that it is said by more than one mouth, and held by more than one.","I had made my lantern at the Lantry table, badly, and Nonie had mended it. When it came to me I knelt down at the water with my knees wet through and I could not do it. Three years. My mouth would not.","Old Isbet put her hand on my back, and waited, as if we had all night. Then I said it.","\"Rafe Fernly.\"","And the Lantry house said it after me, all nine of them, and the strangers beside them said it, and the strangers beside those, a little way along the basin, so that I heard my son's name go away from me along the water in other people's voices, kindly, the way you pass a child along a line of hands to keep him out of a river.","The lantern went out on the basin with all the others. There were so many that the whole plaza was lit, and the mist over it, and the colonnade threw its shadows up into the fog.","COMING HOME","I am home at Greenroot now. I have said his name four times since, in my own kitchen, and nothing has answered, and nothing has heard, and that is right.","I will go back to the Grove next year for the lanterns, and I will take this book with me. I will give one copy to the Lantry house, who will laugh at how I have made them sound grander than they are, and say their stair is not so steep. And I will leave one at the Moot-house, on the shelf where they keep the books that travellers bring, so that the next woman from the Reach who has a name she cannot say will know where to lodge, and what to do, and that she will be welcome.","In Whisper-Grove nothing is ever forgotten. I have left him there, where he is safe."]},
{"id":"lib_f_bastion_instructions","title":"Instructions to Persons Visiting the Bastion","author":"Keld Holm, Clerk of Visitors, for the Martial Compact of Fortis","genre":"travel","year":"416 SR","origin":"Bastion Fortis, the Iron Peaks","where":"p16h_depot3","how":"shop","at":"lib_odile","price":2,"style":"office","summary":"The rules every carter, factor and pilgrim is handed at the gate of Fortis, written by a clerk who means every word and wastes none.","text":["Issued at the Lower Gate to every person not of the Compact. Read it before the second terrace. There is no third copy.","1. You will be asked: \"You're heavy?\" Answer: \"Heavy enough.\" Do not answer \"Yes\". Do not answer \"Very\". Do not tell the sentry what you ate. He is asking whether you are yourself. Say that you are.","2. Do not offer your hand to anyone. In the Bastion a hand offered to a stranger is a claim on his weight. If a man of the Compact offers you his, he has decided something about you. Take it.","3. Do not call any person light. Not in jest. Not of a child. Not of yourself, in the tavern, at the end of a long day. It is the worst thing that can be said here, and it is not said.","4. Weight wanders in the Peaks. Your pack may weigh twice on the fourth terrace what it weighed at the gate, and nothing at all by noon. Hold it by the strap, not the buckle. If it rises, let it go up a little and bring it down slowly. Do not chase it. The Compact does not refund carriage, and does not pay for goods that have floated.","5. Iron goes sideways in the mist. On the outer stair, carry your knife inside your coat. A dropped knife does not go down here. It goes over.","6. Do not run on any terrace. Running is heavy, and heavy goes through. If the ground before you feels soft, or wrong, or like bread, stop. Step backward, slowly, in your own footprints, or in the footprints of the person ahead. Then tell a sentry. He will thank you. He will not thank you twice.","7. You will hear tapping in the lower levels: the Sounders, testing the floor with their rods. This is a good sound. If the tapping stops, turn round and go back the way you came. Do not ask why. Do not wait to see. They will tell you if they want you to know, and they will not want you to know.","8. The Weighing Hall, on the third terrace, is open to visitors on every day but the Weighing itself, which falls on the first Massday of Sheafmonth. Uncover your head. When a soldier of the Compact dies his kit is weighed, and the weight is cut in the wall beside his name. Do not touch the wall. There is no room left at the height of a hand. The new names are cut higher now, and the masons use a ladder.","9. The dead of the Peaks are buried standing, so that the mountain can carry them. You may see this done. Do not remark on it. Do not ask whether it is uncomfortable for them. A visitor asked that in the spring. He was not answered, and he left by the next cart.","10. Prices are fixed and are not discussed. Every scale in the Bastion is checked against the Marshal's weight, so the weight you are charged is the true weight, whatever your pack did on the stair. Sovereigns are taken. Chimes are not. Promises are not.","11. Visitors may not go on the Wall. If you find yourself on the Wall, you are no longer a visitor, and these instructions no longer apply to you. Others will.","12. If the bell on the Wall is struck in threes, go indoors, to the nearest door that opens, and stay there. Any door in the Bastion will open to a stranger when that bell is struck. That is the oldest law in Fortis, and it has never once been broken.","13. If a member of your party is lost within the Bastion, go to the Clerk of Visitors. The Compact will weigh what he carried, if anything is left, and cut the weight in the wall of the Weighing Hall with his name and the year, in the visitors' row by the west door. He does not have to be of the Compact. He has only to have been heavy. We have done it for carters. We have done it for a pedlar's boy. It costs you nothing. Do not offer to pay.","14. Persons who say that these instructions are short are reminded that they are the weight they need to be.","15. On leaving. Your pack will be lighter on the road down. This is the mountain, and not your purse. Count your purse anyway.","Heavy enough.\nK. Holm, Clerk of Visitors."]},
{"id":"lib_f_ruddock_timber_run","title":"Ledger and Day-Book of a Timber Run, Stillwater to Fortis","author":"Rowland Ruddock, timber factor, of Stillwater Landing","genre":"travel","year":"416 SR","origin":"Stillwater Landing, and the road east","where":"flume_landing","how":"shelf","at":"lib_bonded","shelf":"world","style":"paper","summary":"A Stillwater timber factor keeps his accounts on a run of ringing iron-bough to the Bastion, and finds one entry he cannot price.","text":["Hearthday, 2 Seedmonth, at Stillwater.\nBought of the Timber Compact cutters, forty logs of iron-bough, felled at the first frost, seasoned, chalked for Fortis. Paid: 20 sovereigns.\nPaid the keeper his lock dues, in advance, not refunded. Paid: 1 sovereign.\nPaid the keeper for news of the river. Paid: 1 sovereign. He sold the same to the Compact's man before me and the Bores factor after, and told me so, which I suppose is what I paid for.\nHired Huw and Dickon to raft, and Bartle to pole. Wages advanced, 2 sovereigns each. Paid: 6 sovereigns.\nTook my nephew Seb, fifteen, because his mother asked me, and because she asked me loudly.","Stasisday, 4 Seedmonth.\nOut through the lower lock at first light. The basin carries; we said nothing until the cutting. Every lashing oiled so it will not creak. Every log wrapped at the ends in old fleece, so that if two touch they knock and do not ring. Paid for fleece: 4 pence. The rule on the raft is the rule of the trade: no log touches another, and no man raises his voice, and nobody whistles, and that includes you, Seb.","Driftday, 6 Seedmonth.\nAt the Drop the raft took the weir crooked and the big log at the head, the best of the forty, came out of its fleece and struck the sill. It rang. I have heard iron-bough ring all my life and I have never heard it ring like that, over water, at dusk, like the Chapel bell at the Landing.\nThere were hounds on the east bank inside the count of twenty. Huw did not wait for me to tell him. He cut the head log loose with the hatchet and kicked it off down the race, and it went away down the river ringing every time it touched a stone, and the hounds went with it along the bank, and we lay flat on the raft and let the current have us the other way.\nItem: one log, the best, given to the river. Value 3 sovereigns. Entered here for the record, but not added, since I paid for it already at Stillwater and do not mean to pay for it twice. It bought something. I am not sure the ledger has a column for what.","Rootday, 9 Seedmonth, the railhead at Ironford.\nThirty-nine logs onto the flatcars of the Iron Tramway. Freight: 8 sovereigns. The yard clerk would not take Chimes, though I had them. Paid him besides, for his consideration, which is what he called it: 1 sovereign.","Dawnday, 13 Seedmonth.\nThe Tramway goes round the Valley of Velocity by the long loop, and from the cars you can see down into it. It is raining in the Valley. It has been raining in the Valley, the brakeman says, since '98, the same rain, hanging in the air like beads on threads, and the light goes through it and does not come out the same colour. Nobody runs on the cars. The brakeman told Seb so, and then told him again, and then showed him a man's boot nailed to the brake-house wall and did not say whose.\nSeb bet Bartle a penny he could throw a stone into the Valley. He threw it. It went in at the edge and it is going still, I suppose, faster all the time. Bartle says the wager is not decided until it lands. Paid Seb's wager anyway, to have peace: 1 penny.","Rootday, 17 Seedmonth.\nInto the Peaks. Grey rain, grey rock, and a mist that pulls at anything iron, so that Dickon's knife went out of his belt sideways and over the drop like a bird. At the second halt the axle under the fourth car began to groan, and the brakeman said the tide was on, and we watched the logs on that car sink the springs flat, as if each one weighed a house. Nobody touched them. In an hour the tide went over and the springs came back up and the logs were logs again.\nA Peaks woman at the halt asked Huw \"You're heavy?\" and he said \"Middling\", and she looked at him the way you would look at a man who had spat on your doorstep. Seb has learned to say \"Heavy enough\" and says it to everybody, including the mules.\nFood and lodging at the halts, the whole road. Paid: 2 sovereigns.","Driftday, 22 Seedmonth.\nSeb asks about the Sounders, who walk ahead of the work-gangs on the line tapping the stone with iron rods as tall as themselves. He walked behind one for a mile this morning, at a respectful distance, listening. The Sounder let him.","Nullday, 24 Seedmonth.\nNo train runs on Nullday in the Peaks. We sat up in the halt-house all night with the hush-lamp and talked, the five of us, about nothing, so as not to be still. Seb asked what the dead note sounds like. Nobody knew. Dickon said if you have heard it you do not come back to say.","Massday, 27 Seedmonth, at Fortis.\nDelivered thirty-nine logs to the Compact's quartermaster on the fourth terrace. They weighed them on Fortis scales, checked against the Marshal's own weight, and the logs weighed a fifth again what they weighed at Stillwater, and I said so, honestly, and the quartermaster said the weight is the weight, and paid by it, 2 sovereigns the log.\nReceived: 78 sovereigns.\nI asked her what the Bastion wants with ringing wood, when it has the whole mountain. She said they use Reach iron-bough for props in the deep levels, because a prop of iron-bough rings when the load comes on it, and a Sounder can hear a ringing prop three levels off. Your wood tells us when the mountain moves, she said. It is the only timber in the world that will.\nPaid Huw, Dickon and Bartle the balance of their wages, 3 sovereigns each: 9 sovereigns.","Rootday, 3 Lambmonth.\nSeb has asked to stay. The Sounders take any boy who can stand still and listen, and the Captain of them tells me he can. I said his mother would have my hide. He said his mother would have it anyway, for the boots. I bought him boots fit for the deep. Paid: 2 sovereigns.\nI have written to Joan. I have not posted it.","ACCOUNT OF THE RUN\nPaid out: 50 sovereigns and 5 pence.\nReceived: 78 sovereigns.\nBalance in hand: 28 sovereigns, less 5 pence.\nLeft at Fortis: one nephew, Sebastian Ruddock, fifteen, at his own asking.","I have looked for the column to put him in, and there is not one.","I go home lighter, which up here is an insult, and down there will only be the truth."]},
{"id":"lib_g_lamp_book","title":"The Lamp Book of the Bell Yard","author":"the Fellowship of Lamp-Makers and Lighters of Whisper-Grove; kept by Osmer Lowry, warden","genre":"chronicle","year":"396–415 SR","origin":"the lamp-makers' loft, Bell Yard, Whisper-Grove","where":"whispergrove","how":"quest","at":"whispergrove","quest":"shelver","flyleaf":["Osmer Lowry","Kitto Lowry"],"style":"parch","summary":"The ordinances, rounds and yearly remembrances of the Grove's lamplighters, kept by an old warden for the nephew who will have the book after him.","text":["Osmer Lowry, warden of the Fellowship of Lamp-Makers and Lighters, his book; which is to say the Fellowship's book, while he keeps it. After him it goes to Kitto Lowry, his sister's boy, as soon as Kitto can trim a wick without saying a word his mother would not like.","THE ORDINANCES OF THE FELLOWSHIP, as they were read to me when I was bound, and as I have read them to every boy and girl bound since.","First. A lamp of the Grove is lit at dusk and not before, whatever the Moot says about winter afternoons. The pools light the faces; the lamps light the way. Until the pools come on, the willows give light enough, and oil is dear.","Second. Every lighter knows his round by heart and by count, so that he could walk it blind; and every lighter walks his round the same way every night, at the same pace, so that a child who is lost at dusk has only to stand under a lamp and wait, and the lighter will come by within the hour and know her.","Third. No lamp is left half-lit. A lamp that is begun is finished, or it is put out and begun again. A lamp half-lit is a promise half-kept.","Fourth. The lamp at the Moot-house door burns all night while the Moot is sitting. The lighter of that round sits up with it on the steps, and may listen, and may not speak, for in that room he is not a householder, only the lamp-man.","Fifth. On the night the Grove's water is highest, the Fellowship gives paper and wax and wick for the basin lanterns to anyone who asks, at no charge, and no lighter is to ask whose name the lantern is for.","Sixth. Whoever lights the last lamp of his round says, Goodnight, Grove, not loud. Nobody knows when this began. It is an ordinance because a warden once tried to strike it as foolishness and the Fellowship sat in the loft until morning and would not let him.","THE ROUNDS. There are nine rounds in the Grove, and the Ashlane is the longest because of the steps. The Bell Yard round is the oldest and the shortest: eleven lamps on iron arms, from the Moot-house corner down past the joiners' shops to the turn, and back along the other side to the plaza, so that the lighter ends where he began. By custom the warden keeps it for himself. I have kept it since 396, and my father before me.","REMEMBRANCES. A warden writes a line a year, or more if the year deserves it.","396. Made warden, nine hands to two. The two were right; I was too young.","398. New oil up from the Reach, rape oil, cheaper than willow-oil, and it smokes. The Moot complained of the smell. We mixed it half and half and told nobody, and the Moot stopped complaining. Write that down, Kitto: the Moot complains of what it knows.","401. Kitto bound to me at nine years old. He asked me whether lamps were afraid of the dark. I told him a lamp is the only thing in the Grove that is not, and that is the whole of the trade.","404. Kitto burned off both eyebrows on the Ashlane round and lit the rest of the round anyway. Fined a penny for language. Paid it himself, and did not argue, which is more than I did at his age.","407. The water highest in thirty years. We gave out four thousand papers and ran short of wick by the ninth hour of the evening. A little girl came for a lantern for her grandmother and could not say the name for crying, so her brother said it for her, and the people on the steps said it after him, as they do. Ordinance Five. I did not ask.","409. The colour of the flame, written here so the book remembers it if I do not. At the Moot-house door on a still night the flame is the yellow of a pear in Sheafmonth, with blue at the root.","412. The Screaming Year. The Fellowship's book ought to know, so I write it. Since the sky went wrong in the south, the flame is not the colour it was. Same oil. Same wicks. Every lighter over thirty says it, and the young ones look at us as though we were simple. It is paler. A pear in Firstfruit, not in Sheafmonth. The Moot has ordered the lighters to go quietly on the rounds, no calling and no whistling, because singing in other places has begun to bring things. We have always gone quietly. A lighter who has to be told to be quiet has never lit a lamp.","413. The Moot asked whether the lamps ought to be left dark, so as not to be seen from outside. The Fellowship met in the loft and voted to keep lighting, eleven hands to none. I said to the Moot, A lit street is a street where somebody is expected home. They let it stand.","414. My knees. Kitto has the Bell Yard round, though it is the warden's by custom, and I walk behind him and tell him what he is doing wrong. He does nothing wrong. I tell him anyway. That is also custom.","415. Kitto made journeyman at Canopy, fourteen hands to none, and the old ones banged the table. He says he means to marry. I have told him a lamp-man's wife sees him at dusk and at dawn and never in between, and he says that is twice a day more than most men manage.","415, later. I have taken the Bell Yard round back for the winter while Kitto does the Ashlane, which wants young knees. Eleven lamps down, eleven back, and the plaza at the end of it with the pools coming on. I find I minded watching more than I mind the knees. It is the best walk in the Grove and I would not give it to anybody.","Kitto. When this book is yours, keep the Ordinances, and add to them if you must, and never strike the sixth. Light the lamps whether or not anyone is coming. Somebody always is."]},
{"id":"lib_g_midwife_daybook","title":"The Day-Book of a Midwife of the Ashlane","author":"Grisel Imrie, midwife, of the Ashlane","genre":"journal","year":"414–416 SR","origin":"the Ashlane, Whisper-Grove","where":"whispergrove","how":"quest","at":"catacombs","quest":"shelver","flyleaf":["Grisel Imrie"],"style":"paper","summary":"Two years of births in the Ashlane, set down by a brisk and loving midwife: the hard nights, the naming on the water, and the count she means to finish one day.","text":["Grisel Imrie, midwife, of the Ashlane, her day-book, begun in Stillmonth 414 because the last one is full. If found, return it to the house with the blue door at the top of the steps. If I am out, I am at somebody's bedside. Leave it on the step and the cat will mind it.","For whoever comes after me and was not raised in the Grove: we keep our names on the eighth day. The child is carried down to the nearest pool and its name is said over the water three times, once by the mother, once by whoever stands for the father, and once by the midwife, so that the Grove has heard it from three mouths and will not let it go. The Church's man says the custom is harmless. Nobody asked him.","3 Stillmonth 414. The cooper's wife at the top of the lane. A girl, seven pounds and a bit, the fourth hour of the night, and she came out shouting at the cold, which is right and proper. I sang her down low, the way we all do now, and put her to the breast, and the shouting stopped, and her father sat on the stairs and wept. There are no hounds in the Ashlane and there never have been. But since the Screaming Year every father in the Grove listens to his own child as though it were a bell rung in the street, and I do not blame them.","11 Stillmonth. Said on the water: Petronel. The mother said it, the grandmother stood for the father, who is away with the willow-cutters until spring, and I said it third. The pool showed the grandmother a kitchen none of us knew, which she says is a good sign. I said that every sign is a good sign after a safe birth, and she said that was not how signs worked, and we were both right.","Thawmonth. Nine this month and every one of them whole. I am too old to write that without touching wood, so I have touched the table.","Reedmonth. A first child at the fuller's, a boy, eleven hours of it, and the mother so frightened of being heard that she would not push. I said to her, dear one, you may shout at me all you like. Whatever is out there is listening for singing, and nobody in the history of the world has ever mistaken this for singing. She laughed, and then she pushed. Said on the water on the eighth day: Bede. A good plain name. He will grow into it.","Firstfruit. Hot. The pools steam at dusk. I have told every mother due this month to sit with her feet in a basin and her opinions to herself, and none of them will do either.","14 Longsun 415. The fuller's girl again, her second lying-in, and it was two. The first came roaring. The second came blue and still, and would not take a breath, and I did everything there is: the cold water and the warm, the rubbing, my own mouth over its face. The old women say a child that will not cry is a door left open. I do not hold with sayings. I hold the child upside down.","For a long minute it would not. Then the first one, in her mother's arms across the room, roared again, very loud and very rude, and the second one opened its eyes and roared straight back at her. Would not be outdone. In thirty-one years I have never seen anything so like a sister. Said on the water: Columb and Sabra. I said both names twice over, to make up for the fright, and nobody stopped me.","Dustmonth. A girl from the Cloister choir came to my door to ask whether babies can hear singing before they are born, because the choir-mistress had told them so. I said yes, but that mostly what they hear is their mothers' stomachs. She was disappointed in me. I told her to sing to her aunt's belly anyway, softly, because it would do the aunt good whatever it did for the baby.","Harvestmonth. Fourteen this month. I am tired in a way that sleep does not mend. I have taken a girl from the Bell Yard to carry the bag and learn. She is good with mothers and hopeless with fathers, which is the right way round.","Lambmonth 416. The lamp-man's boy from the Bell Yard has married, and his wife is expecting at the back end of the year, and they came to me together to ask what they ought to be doing. I said: nothing, and less of it. He is to keep the lamps lit on the stair. She is to eat.","Sheafmonth 416. Two clerks of the Office came up the steps with a great book for the register, very civil, with gloves on, and asked for every child I have brought since 400, with the date and the house and the voice. I asked what they meant by the voice. They said: the cry, whether high or low, strong or thin. I said that a cry is a cry, and that I do not write down how a baby sounds any more than I write down how it smells, which is lovely. They wrote that down, word for word. I gave them the names, because a name is not mine to keep back. I did not give them this book.","Sheafmonth, later. The works at the end of the Bell Yard go all night now, a grinding you feel in your teeth more than hear, and it comes up the lane when the wind is wrong. The mothers ask me whether it will harm the babies. I tell them babies have heard worse, and I believe it, and I do not like it either.","I have counted tonight, for no reason except that the lamp was good and the kettle was on. Since I was seventeen and held my first one for my mother: one thousand and ninety-one, and every one of them said on the water. When I am too old to kneel I mean to sit at the top of the Ashlane steps of a morning and count the ones who go by and say good morning, little mother. I expect it will take me the whole day."]},
{"id":"lib_g_grove_primer","title":"The Little Grove Primer","author":"set for the Moot schools of Whisper-Grove; written in by two children of the Carters' Yard","genre":"children's","year":"413 SR (new edition, with the Lesson for Quiet); written in until 416","origin":"printed at Whisper-Grove for the Moot schools","where":"whispergrove","how":"shelf","at":"lib_moot","shelf":"tales","flyleaf":["Blanche Clough","Wilmot Clough"],"style":"paper","summary":"The alphabet, a counting rhyme and the courtesies every Grove child learned, in a copy passed from an older sister to her little brother, with their pencil in the margins.","text":["On the flyleaf, in a careful round hand: This book belongs to Blanche Clough, of the Carters' Yard, Whisper-Grove, the Spectral Woods, the World. Under it, in pencil, much larger: AND NOW TO WILMOT CLOUGH BECAUSE BLANCHE IS TOO BIG FOR IT.","THE LITTLE GROVE PRIMER. Letters, Counting and Courtesies, for the Small Ones of the Moot Schools. New edition, with the Lesson for Quiet.","To the dame, or the mother, or the big sister: go slowly. A letter a day is plenty. Say each one aloud, softly, and let the small one say it after you. That is how the Grove has always learned anything.","THE LETTERS.","A is the Ashlane, steep as a stair; say good morning to all who are there. B is the Basin, long and still, with lanterns upon it, as many as will. C is the Canopy, taller than tall; it keeps the whole Grove, the great and the small.","D is for Dearling, and so are you. Beside it, in Blanche's pencil: NOT WILMOT. Beside that, in a worse pencil: IS.","E is the Evening, the lamps are alight; the lighter goes round, and the Grove says goodnight. F is the Fountain: look, but don't paddle. G is the Grove, and the Grove is home. H is your Hand: give it to whoever is smaller.","I is the Ink, and you will spill it. J is the Jug, and you will spill that too. K is the Kettle; ask before you touch. L is the Lamp-man: if you are lost, wait by his lamp. M is the Moot-house, where the grown people talk, and talk, and talk.","N is your Name. Say it clearly when you are asked, and ask other people theirs.","O is for Others; there are always others. P is the Pool, which remembers. You may look at what it shows you, but do not stare, for it is somebody else's and you would not like them staring at yours. Q is for Quiet, the gentle kind. R is the Rain on the willows.","S is for Singing, as soft as you can. T is for Thank you, said every time. U is for Up the steps, and no running. V is your Voice: keep your small one for indoors. W is the Willow, silver and long.","X is the mark that grown people make when they cannot write their names. You will never need it. Y is for Yesterday, which the Grove keeps for you. Z is the sound of a small one asleep.","COUNTING, for the plaza steps. One step, two steps, down to the plaza; three steps, four steps, hold your sister; five, six, the basin's brim; seven, eight, don't fall in; nine, ten, up again; and eleven lamps in the Bell Yard, lit one by one, and the lamp-man says goodnight when he is done.","COURTESIES. When you meet an old one in the lane, say: good morning, little mother; good morning, little father. When you are given a thing, say: you are kind. When you do not know a person's name, ask it. It is not rude in the Grove to ask a name. It is rude not to.","IF YOU ARE LOST. Do not run. Stand under the nearest lamp, because the lighter will come by. When a grown person asks you, say three things, clearly: your name, your street, and your mother's name. Then you will be home before your supper is cold.","THE LESSON FOR QUIET, new in this edition by order of the Moot. Since the Screaming Year there are things beyond the Woods that come to loud singing. They have not come here. We teach this so that they never need to. Skip it in the yard until you know it: Too loud brings the hounds, too still brings the Hush. So sing in the middle, and sing it soft, and never sing in a rush.","And this, which is harder. If somebody calls your name from the dark or the mist, and you cannot see their face, do not answer. Not even if it sounds like your mam. Go to the nearest lamp, and wait for the lighter, and he will take you home, and your mam will not be cross.","In the margin, in Blanche's pencil: Wilmot answered Mam from the privy and got smacked. It WAS Mam.","NOW WRITE YOUR OWN NAME, as big and as clear as you can, so that everyone will know whose book this is.","BLANCHE CLOUGH, in letters as straight as a fence. Below it, WILMOT CLOUGH, with the C the wrong way round and then crossed out and done again the right way, and the G very large, and a drawing of a lamp on its iron arm.","Well done, dearling. A child who knows her letters, and her name, and her street, can always be brought home."]},
{"id":"lib_g_basin_table","title":"Receipts of the Basin Table","author":"Edith Amory, of the Bell Yard; with additions by her daughter Rosamund","genre":"cookery","year":"405 SR, with additions to 415","origin":"the Bell Yard, Whisper-Grove","where":"whispergrove","how":"quest","at":"malting","quest":"shelver","flyleaf":["Edith Amory","Rosamund Amory"],"style":"paper","summary":"A Grove cook's receipts for the feast nights on the Weeping Plaza, with her daughter's notes in the back pages and one receipt that was never written down.","text":["Edith Amory, of the Bell Yard, her receipts. To Rosamund when I am done with them, and not before, and she is not to lend them to her aunt.","The Grove is not a hot-kitchen people. On the feast nights we eat out of doors, on the plaza steps, in the damp, and so we eat things that are good cold and better shared. Everything in this book can be carried in one hand, because on a feast night the other hand is always busy.","One rule before any receipt. Never cook a fish out of a remembering pool. It is perfectly wholesome, and it tastes of somebody else's supper.","LANTERN CAKES, for the night the water is highest. A plain dough of fine flour and butter, one egg to the pound, rolled thin and folded three times and rolled again, and cut in rounds the size of a child's palm, with a thumb-print in each for a spoon of plum or bramble. Bake them the afternoon before, because nobody has a minute on the day. They are for eating on the steps while you wait your turn at the basin. A child with a lantern cake in one hand will not drop his lantern in the water with the other. I have never known it fail, except with Rosamund, once.","STRAW-BOX BROTH. There are no fires on the plaza, by order of the Moot, on account of the colonnade. So the broth is made at noon: barley, leeks, the end of a ham, and a fistful of the cress that grows at the edges of the clean pools, the ones that show you nothing but your own nose. Bring it to a hard boil, then put the whole pot into a box packed with hay, a blanket over the top, and carry it down. At the ninth hour of the evening it will still take the skin off a tongue.","MOOT PASTIES. The Moot sits from the second hour of the afternoon until it stops, and it never stops. Make the pastry short and the filling dry, so they can be eaten on the benches without crumbs or noise: chestnut and mushroom, a little onion, a good deal of pepper. The householders say they come to the Moot for the business. They come for the pasties.","THE REMEMBERING LOAF, for Canopy, the first Memoryday of Ashfall. A round wheat loaf, glazed with milk. Before it goes into the oven, score the top once for each of the house who has gone, and say the name as you draw the knife, not loud. At supper the loaf is broken, never cut, and everyone at the table eats a piece of everyone. Light the candle in the window facing in, so that the light is for the house and not for the street. This year we scored it six times: my mother; my father; my husband's two brothers; the little one before Rosamund; and old Nan, who was nobody's but ate at our table for thirty years and is entitled.","MY CANOPY CAKE, for after the loaf. A dark cake: treacle, ground chestnut, dried pears chopped small, an egg, and the spice. I keep the spice in my head. A cook keeps one thing in her head or she is only a book.","Here the hand changes. The rest is in a younger, faster hand, with the letters leaning forward.","412. Mother says the pears are not as red this year as when she was a girl, and the plums have no bloom on them. I cannot see it. She says I will when I am old, and that this is the whole trouble with being old: you can see everything that has gone.","413. Lantern Night. Mother too tired for the steps, so I took the cakes down alone and gave them out along the basin. A small boy from the Carters' Yard asked whether he might have two, because his sister was too big for hers. I gave him two. His sister, who was not too big for anything, took one off him at once.","414. Mother died in Thawmonth, in her chair by the window, with her shawl on and her feet on the fender. I am told it is a good way to go and I am sure it is. The Echo will have caught her. She was never one to wander off.","Canopy, 414. I scored the loaf seven times. My hand went on the seventh, and I had to stop and start again. Broken, not cut. Everybody at the table had a piece of everybody, and that included her. I lit the candle facing in and sat up with it, which you are not meant to do, and nobody told me not to.","415. The Lantern Night. Water higher than any year I remember. I made her cakes and they were right. The broth was right. A woman from the Reach was on the steps, lodging with the Lantrys, a lantern on her knees she could not set down; I gave her a cake and she ate it without noticing, which is the best thing a cake can do.","Canopy, 415. I have tried her cake four times this year. The treacle, the chestnut, the pears: all right. The spice is wrong. It is not cinnamon and it is not clove and it is not the pepper off the Organum carts. She never wrote it down, and I never asked, because she was always going to be in the kitchen.","So when I find it, I will keep it in my head, the way she did, and not write it down. Whoever has this book after me will have to come to the Bell Yard and ask."]},
{"id":"lib_g_blue_ribbon","title":"Letters in a Blue Ribbon","author":"Cressida Tilney, copy-clerk to the Moot, and Corbet Ansty, journeyman pipe-voicer; bound by her","genre":"letters","year":"414–416 SR","origin":"Whisper-Grove and the Organum","where":"whispergrove","how":"quest","at":"wickhouse","quest":"shelver","flyleaf":["Cressida Tilney","Corbet Ansty"],"style":"paper","summary":"Two years of letters between a Moot clerk in Whisper-Grove and a Grove-born pipe-voicer working in the Organum, and a proposal she will only answer aloud.","text":["The Organum, Reedmonth 414. Cressida. I am here and I am alive and I have not been run over by a Justiciar, though it was close on the second day. You will want to know what it is like. It is loud. Not the way the Grove is loud on a Moot night, all of us murmuring at once; loud the way the inside of a bell is loud. The towers are pipes and the streets are ranks, and when the Synod speaks the whole city plays, and you feel it in your back teeth and in the soles of your boots. I am in the east rank under a master who has voiced pipes for forty years and thinks I am an idiot. You said you might write. Might is a word the Moot uses when it means no. Corbet.","Whisper-Grove, Longsun 414. Corbet Ansty. I am writing, which is not the same as might and not the same as yes. The Moot sat nine hours on Massday over the paint on the colonnade, whether white or the old grey, and I copied every word, and at the end they agreed to sit again. I enclose nothing. You may imagine a pressed willow-leaf if it helps. C. T.","The Organum, Firstfruit 414. C. T. Thank you for the willow-leaf. It is the finest I have ever imagined. You asked, or you did not ask but I will tell you, what a voicer does. A pipe is made to speak one note. It does not know which until you tell it, and you tell it by cutting the mouth, a hair at a time, and listening, and cutting again. Every pipe has one note it was made for, and when you have found it you know, because it stops arguing. My master says I cut too fast. He is right. I think it is because I am homesick. I miss the quiet. I miss the pools showing me somebody's breakfast. Will you do a thing for me? On the Lantern Night, set one for my grandfather, Old Ansty of the Cloister road. I cannot get leave, and somebody ought to say him. Corbet.","Whisper-Grove, Ashfall 414. Corbet. I set it. I said his name on the basin, and the people beside me said it after me, and so did the people beside them, a long way down the water, so your grandfather is now a public matter and will be discussed. It was a good lantern. I made it myself, and it went out straight and did not tip. Canopy was quiet. We scored the loaf at home and I lit the candle facing in. I have kept your letters, all four. Do not let that go to your head; I keep the Moot's minutes too. C.","The Organum, Stillmonth 415. Cressida Tilney. I have voiced eleven pipes since I wrote last and every one of them stopped arguing. I have got better at listening. So I am going to say it, as plainly as I can, in ink, because ink is what I have. Marry me. Corbet.","Whisper-Grove, Thawmonth 415. Corbet. No. Read the rest of this before you throw yourself off a tower. A thing written in a letter can be taken back; it is only ink, and ink is cheap, I buy it for the Moot by the jar. In the Grove a thing that matters is said aloud, on the water, in front of other people, so that it is heard and kept. You know this. You were named on a pool like the rest of us. So come home and say it on the basin steps, aloud, and I will answer you there, aloud, and the whole Grove may say it after us if it likes. That is not no, you fool. It is not yet. C.","The Organum, Seedmonth 415. C. My master will not give me leave until my journeyman year is out, which by his count is Sheafmonth of next year, and by mine is a hundred years. Will you wait? I have bought nothing yet, because I do not know what you would like, and because if I buy it I will look at it every night and cut my pipes too fast. Corbet.","Whisper-Grove, Dustmonth 415. Corbet. I will wait, and I will not be gracious about it. News, since you are homesick. The fuller's girl in the Ashlane had twins, and the second would not breathe until the first shouted at it, and the whole lane is telling it, and Grisel Imrie is pretending she is not proud. The lamp-men voted to keep lighting, which they do every year, and Old Lowry made a speech, which he does every year. The pools are high. I copy the Moot and I read your letters on the gallery at noon, where the light is good. Nobody up there can see what I am reading, which is just as well, because it is mostly the same letter. C.","The Organum, Lambmonth 416. Cressida. The city played for three hours yesterday because the Synod was speaking, and I stood in the rank with my hands over my ears like a boy and could not hear a single word of what it said. You never can. That is the thing about this place: everything is said aloud and nobody can make it out. In the Grove you can hear a person drop a spoon across the plaza, and you always know whose spoon it was. I have been away too long. Corbet.","The Organum, Firstfruit 416. C. T. My master has signed my papers. He shook my hand and told me I still cut too fast and that I will be a good voicer in about thirty years. I have bought a ribbon, blue, because you said once that you liked the blue ones on the Moot rolls, and I bought it without looking at it so as not to cut my pipes too fast. I will be home before Canopy. I will come down the Moot-house steps and stand on the basin's edge and say it, aloud, in front of the whole Grove, and you will have to answer aloud, Cressida Tilney, and everybody will hear. Corbet.","On the last leaf, in her hand: Bound by me, Sheafmonth 416, with the ribbon off my good hat, so that I shall have them all in one place when he comes, and can read him back every word if he tries to take any of it back. He is due before Canopy. I have been practising my answer up in the muniment store, where nobody can hear me. It is one word. I have had it ready since Reedmonth of 414."]},
{"id":"lib_g_plaza_and_canopy","title":"The Plaza and the Canopy, as the Old People Tell It","author":"Carrick Thackray, clerk to the Moot of Whisper-Grove","genre":"history","year":"411 SR","origin":"the Moot-house, Whisper-Grove","where":"whispergrove","how":"shelf","at":"lib_moot","shelf":"history","flyleaf":["Carrick Thackray","Joliffe Thackray"],"style":"parch","summary":"The Moot's own clerk writes down what the Grove believes about its great tree, its drowned-lamp basin and its quarrelsome Moot, giving both versions wherever the old people disagree.","text":["Carrick Thackray, clerk to the Moot, wrote this. For Joliffe, my grandson, who asked me how old the plaza was and would not take very for an answer.","The Moot asked me at its Sheafmonth sitting to set down a history of the plaza and the Canopy before the old people who know it are all gone, which is true, and was rudely put. I have done my best. Where the old people agree, I have written it plain. Where they disagree, which is everywhere, I have written both, and the reader may choose. I am a clerk, not a judge. The Moot pays me by the page and not by the verdict.","OF THE CANOPY. From any street in the Grove, if you look up past the roofs, you will see it: wood and crystal grown together into one thing, silver at the root and pale as ice where it goes up into the mist, so tall that nobody has ever seen the top. Nobody has ever seen it grow, and nobody remembers it shorter.","The old people say it was the first willow, and that the Grove grew up around it because people like to live near something that remembers. They say its roots run under the whole of the Woods. The scribes up at the House of the Veil say the roots come down through their own cellars, a long day's walk from here, and the scribes do not tell lies, though they do not tell much else either.","The old people say also that when one of the Grove dies, the Echo catches them, and the Canopy keeps them. That is why we keep Canopy, on the first Memoryday of Ashfall, with a candle facing in. My grandmother said that on a still night you could hear them if you put your ear to a root, saying their names over to themselves, the way children say their letters. I tried it when I was eight. I heard my own blood going. My grandmother said that was the same thing.","The Church says otherwise. The Church's man, who is a decent fellow and eats with the Moot on feast days, says that the Canopy is the Spire of Memory, one of the eight that sing the laws of the world, and that it sings the law by which things are remembered, and that the dead are with the Pure Note and nowhere else. I have written down both. I do not choose.","The pools are the Canopy's too, or so the old people say: it drinks from them, and dreams into them, and that is why a pool will sometimes show you a stranger's kitchen, or a stranger's morning. Children are taught to look and not to stare. It is somebody else's, and you would not want them staring at yours.","OF THE PLAZA. It is the size of four fields, all white marble, with a colonnade on three sides and the Moot-house on the fourth. The old people say the Moot-house was put on the fourth side so that the Moot should always have the whole Grove in front of it, and the whole Grove should always be able to see whether the Moot was awake.","It was laid, they say, in the time of the crowns, before the Reckoning, by masons who were paid in nothing but supper for forty years, because the Grove had nothing else to pay them in. That is why a Grove mason will still not take a fee for mending the plaza, only his supper, which the Moot finds very convenient and does not mention.","Down the middle runs the basin, cut long and shallow for the lamps. Here the old people disagree. Some say the first lantern was set by a woman whose son went out into the Woods and did not come back; she put a lamp on the water so he could find his way, and said his name aloud, and her neighbours said it after her. He did not come back. The lamps did, every year after, on the night the water is highest. Others say the custom is older than the basin, and that the basin was cut for it and not the other way about, and the scribes at the House take this side, and will show you nothing to prove it.","OF THE MOOT. Every householder may sit. Anyone may speak once on a question, and twice if they are very old. Nothing is decided until the lamp-man has lit the lamp on the Moot-house door, which is an old rule and a good one, because it means nothing is ever decided in a hurry. The longest Moot in the rolls sat nine days on whether the plaza steps should have a rail. It ruled that they should not, because a rail would spoil the steps for sitting on. Nobody has fallen off them since that anybody will admit to.","Above the hall is the gallery, where the light is best, and behind it the muniment store, where the rolls are kept, tied in blue. Below is the cellar, where the Moot keeps its cider in a barrel made by a cooper of the Ashlane. The barrel has not leaked in eleven years, and the cooper is insufferable about it.","Along the west wall the Moot keeps the town's books: anything a householder leaves to the Moot, and the books that travellers bring. Long ago two houses of the Ashlane fell out over whose family book should stand on the top shelf, and the Moot sat three nights on it, and ruled that the town's books should be shelved by size and not by name, so that no house's book stands above another's: the tall ones at the back, the small ones on the left. The clerk shelves them. I have shelved them for thirty-one years. It is the most restful work in the Grove, and it is the only ruling of the Moot that nobody has ever asked to have reversed.","OF THE HOUSE. The scribes of the Veil keep their House in the deep Woods and come down to us when there is something to write. For as long as the rolls go back, the Moot gave the House its vellum, and the House copied the Moot's rolls fair once in every generation and kept the copy, against fire. Since the Veil was taken into the Synod's Office the scribes come with grey ink, and are very polite, and do not stay for supper. We liked them.","That is the history, or as much of it as the old people would agree to let me write. The Moot has met on this plaza for longer than anybody can count. In all that time it has never once agreed about anything, and it has never once failed to meet. I think that is what a town is.","Joliffe. When you are a householder, go to the Moot. Sit at the back. Speak once a year. And when the Moot rises, put the books back the way you found them: the tall ones at the back, by size, not by name."]},
{"id":"lib_g_bistre_commonplace","title":"The Commonplace Book of the Scribe Styled Bistre","author":"Bistre, scribe of the House","genre":"journal","year":"c. 371–414 SR","origin":"the House of the Silent Veil, the Spectral Woods","where":"mausoleum","how":"shelf","at":"lib_scriptorium","shelf":"lives","style":"parch","summary":"Forty years of things one Veil scribe liked: receipts for ink, a prayer for Memoryday, sayings worth stealing, and small observations from a cold room.","text":["Bistre. The designation given me from the ink-cupboard by the Prior's hand, Stillmonth 371. The last Bistre was an old man who left me a cracked horn, a good penknife, and the habit of humming while he ruled a page, which the duty-scribe asks me every week to break.","Things worth keeping, copied as I find them. This is not a record. Records go on vellum. This is only what I liked.","THE INK OF THE HOUSE. Oak-galls, from the oaks along the Organum road, because there are none in the Woods. Crush them, do not grind them, and steep them nine days in rainwater. Never pool water: the old scribes say pool water makes an ink that remembers what it would rather have written. Green copperas, a thumb's weight to the pint, which turns it as black as the second note. Gum, until it hangs from the quill and thinks about it before it falls. Stir with a willow stick. Never with iron.","LAMPBLACK, for the pitched hand. The soot of the east stove, scraped on the last day of the month when the desks are done with vinegar. Work it with gum and one drop of honey, so that it runs in the long held strokes and does not break.","The three rules, which every scribe knows and which I copy here only because a commonplace book without them looks undressed. Write what happened. Write who it happened to. Do not comfort yourself in the margin.","A thing said by the Registrar of the Office to an apprentice, in my hearing, in the year 405: Rule the page before you write on it. A margin is not decoration. A margin is the place where you are not permitted to put your own opinion, and leaving it empty is the discipline of the whole trade. I copy it because it is good, and because I wish I had said it, and because I have been putting my own opinion in the margins of this book for thirty-four years and have no intention of stopping.","BEFORE THE PAGE ON MEMORYDAY, said under the breath on the stair, with the lamp. You who are on this page: I am not the one who forgets you. I am only the one who comes. Hear your names, and hold them a while longer. I will be back in eight days.","There are no birds in the Woods. I have looked for forty years. A novice from the Peaks asked me where they had gone. I said they had never come. She said that was worse, and she was right, and I have liked her ever since.","The pool under the House is highest on the night the Grove sets its lanterns. From the north windows you can see, a long way off through the willows, a glow on the mist where they are putting their names on the water. We set ours from the steps under the arch, one each, and say them in the flat voice, and the pool takes them round the bend and out of sight.","Vinegar day. The whole floor smells of it, and of tallow, and of wet wool. A novice asked me today to describe the smell to her, because she had just come and could not tell it from damp. I could not. I said she would know it in a winter. She asked what I meant. I said: you will know it the way you know your own name, which is to say you will never once think about it until somebody takes it away.","THE OFFICE'S GREY INK, which comes by the cask and is free. It does not bite the vellum. It sits on it, like oil on a pool. It is good for registers that are to be read within the year and poor for registers that are to be read in a thousand. I have written to the Office to say so, in grey ink, as required.","FOR AN INKY HAND. Sorrel juice, pumice, and patience. Nothing takes out the second knuckle. Wear it.","A thing the Prior said to a weeping novice, which I copy without his leave: The House does not ask you not to feel. It asks you not to write it down. Feel all you like. Feel it on the stairs.","412, the Screaming Year. My minium has gone brown on the page, and I will swear on the Rule that it is the same pot. The Prior says it is the light that is wrong, not the ink. A scribe from the south wing says the light is wrong everywhere and the ink knows it. I have written down both and I do not choose.","413. Sent down to the stacks by the roster, four of us, as the roster says. We came up quiet, as one does. The Rule lets me write this much and no more: it is not silent down there, and it is not sad. It is like standing in a crowded room where everyone is being introduced.","414. My hand shakes in the cold now. The duty-scribe has moved me nearer the east stove, which I resent and enjoy in equal parts. I have copied the three rules once more below, in the pitched hand, slowly, to see whether I still can.","Here follow the three rules in the old notation: small even marks, each line falling at its end, the held strokes long and unbroken.","Still can. Whoever has this book after me: it is not a record. Read it anyway. It is only what one scribe liked, in a cold room, for forty years, and I liked all of it."]},
{"id":"lib_g_pitched_hand_exercises","title":"Exercises in the Pitched Hand","author":"a novice of the House, his name scraped from the cover; corrected in minium by the scribe styled Woad","genre":"manual","year":"388 SR","origin":"the House of the Silent Veil, the Spectral Woods","where":"mausoleum","how":"shelf","at":"lib_scriptorium","shelf":"craft","style":"paper","summary":"A homesick novice's exercise book, learning the Veil's notation one mark at a time, with his master's red corrections and one long argument about the motto over the door.","text":["On the cover, a name in a boy's hand, scraped away with a penknife until the vellum is thin. Beneath it, in red: Novices do not write their names on House paper. You will have it back at the door. W.","EXERCISES IN THE PITCHED HAND. For the novice at the fourth desk, south side. Set by Woad.","THE FIRST LESSON: THE MARKS. The pitched hand does not write a word as it is spelled. It writes a word as it is said: how high, how long, and where the breath falls. The common hand keeps what a name means. The pitched hand keeps how it sounds in the mouth that said it. That is why it is ours, and why it is slow.","The line, which is a sound held. The hook up, which is a rise. The hook down, which is a fall. The dot, which is a rest; a rest is not nothing, and it is written. The tie, for two sounds that may not be parted. The stroke through, which is a breath taken. Five heights, ruled faint, and the marks sit on them like birds on a rail, if you have ever seen a bird, which here you will not.","Below, in the boy's hand, a page of marks copied over and over, line, line, hook down, dot, and in red at the foot: Your dots are blots. A rest is small. Make it small.","THE SECOND LESSON: HEAR AND WRITE. I will say a word at the door. Write what you heard, not what you know.","Bread. Willow. Water. Good morning.","In red, beside the last: You wrote good morning rising at the end, the way they say it in the villages, because that is how your mother says it. I said it flat. Write what you heard. Your mother is not in this room.","Beneath that, in the boy's hand, small: I know.","THE VOICE FOR ENTRIES. When you read an entry aloud, read it flat and clear and unhurried, the way a person reads a will. The voice for entries does not rise and does not fall. It is not yours to colour. The name has its own colour. Leave it alone.","On the facing page, in the boy's hand, a list: It is very cold. There are no birds. The stove smokes until the third bell. I miss the noise of the mill. I am not allowed to write this in this book. Beneath it, in red: No, you are not. I have read it, so it is done. Turn the page.","THE THIRD LESSON: THE MOTTO. Over the round arch, cut in the stone in our notation: What is written is not lost. Seven sounds. You walked under them on your first morning and could not read them. By the end of this book you will be able to read them, and write them, and you will never walk under that arch again without hearing them.","First attempt, in the boy's hand. In red beneath: You have put a hook up on lost. You are asking a question. It is not a question.","Second attempt. In red: Better. The tie on written is broken. Do not part what may not be parted.","Third attempt. In red: You have put a dot after not. Why? Below it, the boy: Because I wanted to stop before lost. And in red: So, I expect, did the scribe who cut it over the arch. He did not. Neither will you.","Fourth to eleventh attempts, filling two pages. The red marks get fewer. One is only a tick. One says: The rise has crept back. You are still asking.","At the foot of the eleventh page, very small, in the pitched hand and not in any exercise: a name, three sounds, the last one falling. It is not struck through. Beside it in red: This one is well made. W.","THE FOURTH LESSON: A NAME HEARD AT A DOOR. When you are sworn you will stand a little way off from things, with a board on your arm, and people will tell you their names because they are frightened, or because they are dying, or because nobody else is listening. You will write each one as it was said, the fear in it and the accent and the cough, so that whoever reads it in a thousand years hears that person and not you. Practise on me.","Below, the boy's attempt at the master's own designation, Woad, said at a door. In red: That is very like. You have caught my cold.","The last page. The motto once more, in the boy's best hand: the line held, the tie unbroken, no dot before lost, and the last sound falling to the bottom of the ruling and stopping there.","In red, beneath it: Kept. You may have a designation in the spring. It is not a question. Write it always as if you knew."]},
{"id":"lib_g_orpiment_meditations","title":"Meditations on the Keeping of Names","author":"Orpiment, scribe of the House","genre":"philosophy","year":"413–416 SR","origin":"the House of the Silent Veil, the Spectral Woods","where":"mausoleum","how":"shelf","at":"lib_scriptorium","shelf":"faith","style":"parch","summary":"An old Veil scribe's private meditations on her calling, written in the last years of the House, as her own colour fades and the registers are taken away.","text":["These are not entries. Nothing here happened to anyone but me. I keep them in the drawer of my desk, under the blotter, and if the Prior finds them he may burn them in the east stove, which is what it is for.","THE FIRST. ON THE CURVE. The Rule says the world forgets on a curve, steep at first and then slow, and that the House stands at the bottom of it with a cup. I have been thinking about the cup. It is a very small thing to hold under a curve that long. A novice once asked me what happens to the names that miss it. I told her that was why we must never miss. She went away satisfied. I did not.","THE SECOND. ON MY OWN NAME. I gave my name into the House forty-four years ago. It was written once in the Prior's hand and sealed and laid in the cabinet, and I have not missed it, and I am told that not missing it is the proof that it is safe. Some evenings I go and stand by the cabinet. Not to open it; it is sealed. The way you stand outside a room where someone you love is asleep, to hear them breathing.","THE THIRD. ON MY COLOUR. Orpiment is the brightest yellow in the ink-cupboard, and a poison. We grind it wet, with a cloth over the mouth. The scribe who had it before me died very old, so it is a slow poison, or a courteous one. Since the Screaming Year it is less yellow. Not on the page only: in the shell, in the cupboard, under my hand. Everyone over thirty says the same of every colour, and the young ones look at us kindly. I find I mind this more than I can account for. A designation is not a name. And yet I have answered to it for forty-four years, and it is going out like a lamp.","THE FOURTH. ON MEMORYDAY. Tonight it was my turn. I went down with the lamp to the lower hall and read the page aloud, flat and unhurried, from where the last scribe stopped. I do not know to whom I read it. Nobody in the House knows; the Rule says only that it is done. I read it the way you read to a child who has fallen asleep halfway through: you finish the chapter anyway, in case.","THE FIFTH. ON THE STACKS. Eleven times down by the roster, and I have kept the Rule each time and written nothing of what is heard there. I will set down only what I felt, which the Rule does not mention, because nobody who wrote the Rule thought it needed saying. I felt expected.","THE SIXTH. ON A WORD. The Office's clerks came again to count the shelves. They wore gloves to touch the registers, and they did not read a page. One of them, a pleasant young man, called what was on the shelves units. He was not being cruel. He did not know there was another word. I have been turning it over for a month. A unit is a thing that may be counted and not missed. A name is a thing that is missed. The whole of the House, eleven hundred years of it, is the distance between those two words, and I do not think it is a long distance, and I do not think it is getting longer.","THE SEVENTH. ON THE VELLUM. It went down the hill today in four wagons. The pool took a wheel and they spent the afternoon getting it out, and the whole House stood at the windows and watched and did not help. The registers are to be kept in the Organum, they say, drier than they have ever been. I believe them. I have never in my life believed anything that comforted me less.","The desks are clean. There is nothing to copy. One pen in the north row is still moving, and nobody has stopped it. I have been sitting at my own desk with my pen dry, listening to it, the way you listen to rain on a roof you are not sure of.","THE EIGHTH. ON TAKING AND KEEPING. The Office has asked for scribes to go down to the Grove with boards and take names for the great register, and I have been chosen, being quick and old and having nothing on my desk. I have taken names all my life. I have stood a little way off from fevers and floods and once a town on fire, and written down who it happened to. That was keeping. I do not know yet what this is. The letter says register. The letter is very well ruled.","THE NINTH. Ashfall, 416. Tomorrow I go down the hill with a board on my arm. Write what happened. Write who it happened to. Do not comfort yourself in the margin. I have kept the third rule for forty-four years and I am going to break it once, here, at the end of my own book, where nobody will read it: I am afraid, and I do not know of what, and I would give my colour to be wrong.","If I do not come back up the hill, whoever has this desk: the names were never ours. We only kept the door on the latch."]},
{"id":"lib_h_daybook","title":"The Duty-Scribe's Day-Book","author":"the scribe styled Ochre, duty-scribe of the House","genre":"journal","year":"416 SR","origin":"the House of the Silent Veil","where":"mausoleum","how":"found","at":"lib_scriptorium","style":"parch","summary":"The last entries of the scriptorium's day-book, kept by whoever lit the east stove.","text":["The day-book of the scriptorium, kept by the duty-scribe of the week, who lights the east stove at the second bell and writes here what the House did with its day. Nothing in this book is a record. Records go on vellum. This is only the day.","First day. Lit the east stove at the second bell. Damp in the flue; it smoked until the third. Fourteen at the desks. Names entered: forty-one, all from the Grove, none disputed. Old Gall's hand is going; I have moved him nearer the window. The pool is high.","Second day. Stove lit at the second bell, drew well. Fourteen at the desks, and two copying at the stove. Names entered: thirty-eight. A carter from the Office brought a letter with a seal I did not know and waited in the yard for an answer. The Prior read it in the kitchen and put it in the stove, which I note only because it was my stove.","Third day. Stove lit. Twelve at the desks; four sent down to the stacks to listen, as the roster says, and they came up quiet, as they always do. Names entered: thirty. The third desk from the east, north side, finished the Fenmark register a day early and asked for more. I gave her the Mossbank strays. She is the quickest hand we have had since I was young, and she knows it, and she is right to.","Fourth day. Stove lit late. I overslept; the bell-boy did not wake me, because there was no bell. The Office has asked for the bell. Nobody has said yes. Nobody has said no. Names entered: twenty-six. The carter came back with another letter and did not wait this time.","Fifth day. Stove lit. The Prior spoke to the whole House in the refectory, which he has done twice in my life. He said the Office is the Veil under another name and that the work goes on. He said it well. He did not say where the vellum would go. Names entered: none. We sat at the desks with our pens dry and listened to the stove.","Sixth day. Crates in the yard. Grey wood, Office stencil, numbered. They are very good crates. Two clerks from the Office, very polite, wearing gloves to touch the registers, which no scribe of the House has ever done. They counted the shelves and wrote the count on a slate and did not read a single page. I asked one of them if he knew what was on the shelves. He said, Units. He said it kindly, as if I had asked the time.","Seventh day. The vellum went down the hill. Four wagons. The pool took a wheel and they spent the afternoon getting it out, and every scribe in the House stood at the windows and did not help. I am not proud of that. I am not ashamed of it either.","Eighth day. Stove lit at the second bell. There is nothing to write on. The desks are clean. The third desk from the east, north side, is writing anyway, on the backs of her own letters, the names she remembers from the Grove. I did not stop her. I sat at my desk and wrote this.","Ninth day. The Office has sent word that the House will close for the winter and the scribes will be found other work. Found. I am to go to the Organum and copy what we wrote. Most of us are. Some are going to the Grove to take names, which is a different thing from keeping them, whatever the letter says.","Last day. I have let the stove go out. Whoever lights it next, the flue wants a hand on the damper before the second bell, or it smokes. The day-book stays here. It is not a record. Nobody will take it.","What is written is not lost. I have written that at the top of every page for thirty years, the way the House taught us, without once wondering whether it was true."]}];
